window.__BIRINCI_SEARCH__ = [{"title":"A halal morsel","stem":"honestly-earned-morsel","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#honestly-earned-morsel","hay":"a halal morsel faith and spirituality even when the flour is fresh and the water pure, dough will not rise properly if the yeast is spoiled. nor will it make good bread. to raise a child of good character, nourish them with food earned in a lawful (halal) way. — attributed to imam al-ghazali a child’s moral upbringing begins with the example set at home. an honest livelihood, integrity, and respect for the rights of others provide a firm foundation."},{"title":"Adversity and blessing","stem":"adversity-and-blessing","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#adversity-and-blessing","hay":"adversity and blessing faith and spirituality a hardship that draws you closer to allah may hold a hidden blessing. a blessing that draws you away from allah may become a misfortune. do not judge your experiences only by the pain or comfort they bring. consider how they change your heart and where they lead you."},{"title":"The church bell","stem":"church-bell","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#church-bell","hay":"the church bell faith and spirituality long ago, so the story goes, a country had a custom: when an ordinary citizen died, the church bell tolled once. for a noble, it tolled twice; for someone close to the king, three times; and for the king himself, four times. one day, the court—the very place people turned to in the hope of finding justice—convicted an innocent man. that day, the church bell tolled five times. bewildered, the townspeople gathered outside the church and asked the priest: “who could be more important than the king? why did you ring the bell five times?” the priest answered sorrowfully: “justice. today, justice died.” an unjust verdict harms more than one person: it shakes everyone’s faith in justice. speaking out against injustice protects both those who suffer and the conscience of society."},{"title":"Confidence is half the battle","stem":"confidence-is-half-the-battle","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#confidence-is-half-the-battle","hay":"confidence is half the battle faith and spirituality a teaching story tells of a principal who called several teachers into his office and gave them an assignment. “you are among our most capable teachers. we are therefore putting a class of specially selected, talented pupils in your care. this year, we want you to help them develop their abilities even further.” encouraged by this trust, the teachers prepared their lessons enthusiastically, tried new approaches, and patiently supported pupils who struggled. the children sensed that their teachers believed in them. they became more willing to ask questions and attempt difficult tasks. by the end of the school year, the class had achieved excellent results. teachers and pupils alike were delighted. the principal congratulated the teachers. “there is something i need to tell you. these children were not selected for exceptional ability. they were chosen at random.” the teachers exchanged glances. their hard work must have played a considerable part in the children’s progress. the principal continued: “and you were not selected through a competition either. i drew your names by lot.” the room fell silent. “you believed in your own abilities and in the children’s,” he said. “then you turned that belief into effort, patience, and care. that is what made the difference.” belief alone does not create success, but it can encourage people to try, learn, and persevere. if you believe someone can succeed, show them that belief and support their growth."},{"title":"The dervish’s long spoons","stem":"dervishs-long-spoons","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#dervishs-long-spoons","hay":"the dervish’s long spoons faith and spirituality one day, a dervish was asked: “what is the difference between those who truly love and those who only talk about love?” “let me show you,” he replied. first, he invited a group of people who spoke a great deal about love to share a meal. bowls of hot soup were set before them, along with spoons that had very long handles. “there is one condition,” he said. “you must hold your spoons at the very end of the handle.” the guests began to eat. but the spoons were so long that no one could bring the soup to their own mouth. however hard they tried, the soup spilled and the spoons knocked against one another. each person was intent on feeding only themselves. eventually, they left the table tired and hungry. then the dervish invited people who showed their love through their actions. they were served the same soup and given the same spoons, and the same rule applied. one guest filled a spoon and offered it to the person opposite. that person returned the gesture. soon, everyone was feeding one another. conversation and laughter rose around the table, and no one went hungry. after the guests had left, the dervish turned to those who had asked the question. “the spoons were the same. so was the soup. the difference was in how the people treated one another. at the first table, everyone thought only of themselves. at the second, everyone cared for the person opposite them too.” love shows itself in acts of care, not words alone. when we notice one another’s needs and offer our help, we can overcome difficulties together."},{"title":"The dervish’s robe","stem":"dervishs-robe","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#dervishs-robe","hay":"the dervish’s robe faith and spirituality one day, an injured bird came before the king with a complaint. “a dervish has broken my wing. i ask you for justice.” the king summoned the man. “why did you break this bird’s wing?” the man in the dervish’s robe tried to defend himself. “your majesty, i was trying to catch it. i approached, but it did not fly away. i moved closer, and still it stayed where it was. i thought i could catch it easily. but when i reached for it, it struggled to escape. that was when its wing broke.” the king turned to the bird. “why didn’t you fly away when you saw him approaching?” the bird replied: “he was wearing a dervish’s robe. had i known he was a hunter, i would have flown away. i thought he was a compassionate man who feared allah and would do me no harm. i stayed because i trusted what his robe stood for.” the king was silent for a moment. then he ordered angrily: “break his arm as well!” the bird spoke up at once. “no, your majesty! please don’t!” “why not?” asked the king in surprise. “his arm will heal, and he may hurt someone again. have him take off the dervish’s robe, so that others like me won’t be deceived into trusting him because of what he wears.” wearing something that inspires trust does not make a person worthy of it. judge people by their actions, not their clothing or titles."},{"title":"Hands at work, hope in Allah","stem":"hands-at-work-hope-in-god","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#hands-at-work-hope-in-god","hay":"hands at work, hope in allah faith and spirituality there was once a dervish who spent much of his day praying in solitude. he supported himself through his own labor: each day, he worked for a while, bought bread with his earnings and shared whatever he did not need with the poor. one day, while praying in the desert, he noticed a fox that could barely use its legs. the animal struggled to drag itself along. the dervish wondered: “how does this fox survive? it cannot hunt or even walk properly. how does allah provide for it?” just then, a lion appeared in the distance, dragging a gazelle it had caught. it set down its prey, ate its fill and walked away, leaving the rest. the fox, which had been waiting nearby, dragged itself over and ate until it was no longer hungry. the dervish marveled at what he had seen. he praised allah for providing for the fox through the lion. then he thought: “allah, who feeds this fox, will surely feed me too. i can spend the time i usually work in prayer instead. there is no need to work any longer.” from that day on, he stopped working and devoted all his time to worship. but the food he expected never arrived. no one knocked on his door or brought him even a piece of bread. day by day, he grew weaker. at last, one day, he heard an unseen voice: “i gave you the strength and health to work. why did you choose to imitate the fox that could not walk? why did you not learn from the lion? get up, work and earn your living. let those who need help benefit from your labor too. feed yourself, and feed someone in need.” the dervish realized that he had drawn the wrong lesson from what he had witnessed. he repented and returned to his former way of life, earning his bread and sharing it with those in need. trusting in allah does not mean giving up your efforts. it means doing what you can and placing your trust in him. if you have the strength and opportunity, work both to support yourself and to help others."},{"title":"Drinking the sherbet of martyrdom","stem":"drinking-the-sherbet-of-martyrdom","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#drinking-the-sherbet-of-martyrdom","hay":"drinking the sherbet of martyrdom faith and spirituality according to a traditional account, mortally wounded soldiers in ottoman times were given sherbet—a sweet drink—to leave a pleasant taste in their mouths during their final moments. doctors are said to have prepared it with water from the sacred zamzam well and carried it in small barrels. the expression “to drink the sherbet of martyrdom,” meaning to die a martyr, is sometimes linked to this account. however, the story should be treated as a traditional explanation rather than an established account of the expression’s origin. its most moving element is the idea that a person should not be deprived of tenderness and care, even at the end of life. language preserves memories, beliefs, and stories from the past. honoring that inheritance means appreciating its meaning while distinguishing tradition from documented history."},{"title":"Fasting","stem":"fasting","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#fasting","hay":"fasting faith and spirituality during ramadan, a few young men noticed an elderly man sitting quietly to one side, eating. they approached him and asked mockingly: “uncle, what’s going on? you’re eating? aren’t you fasting?” the old man answered calmly: “i am fasting, my boys. i’m simply eating and drinking.” they laughed. “what kind of fasting is that?” he replied: “i keep my tongue from lying, my eyes from looking at others with ill intent, and my hands from taking what belongs to someone else. i don’t mock people, belittle them, or gossip about them. i avoid wastefulness. my illness prevents me from going without food, but i try to make my words and actions reflect the spirit of this month.” then he asked: “and what about you? are you fasting?” the young men lowered their heads in shame. “perhaps we’re only going without food…” the spiritual value of fasting lies not only in enduring hunger, but also in disciplining our speech, thoughts, and behavior. before judging someone else, try to understand their circumstances and examine your own actions."},{"title":"The fortieth-day rite","stem":"fortieth-day-rite","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#fortieth-day-rite","hay":"the fortieth-day rite faith and spirituality in some turkic communities, the first forty days after childbirth are regarded as a time of special care for mother and baby. customs concerning outings and visitors vary between regions and families. one account of the tradition marking the end of these forty days describes a visit to relatives. the hosts prepare small gifts for the baby, each carrying a wish: coins, for a life free from want; cotton, for a gentle heart and a life long enough to see one’s hair turn gray; sugar, for sweetness and happiness; rice, for abundance in the home; salt, for a life that never loses its savor; an egg, for nourishment and good health. these objects do not determine the child’s future. they give a tangible form to the family’s hopes for a happy life. the beauty of this custom lies not in the price of the gifts, but in the care shown to mother and baby and in the blessings offered for the years ahead."},{"title":"Friend of Allah","stem":"friend-of-allah","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#friend-of-allah","hay":"friend of allah faith and spirituality an elderly man saw a barefoot child in the street. he took the child to a nearby shop and bought a pair of shoes. the child held the new shoes and gazed at them in silence. then, looking up, the child asked: “are you allah?” the man’s eyes filled with tears. he knelt beside the child. “no, little one. i’m just a person, like you.” the child thought for a moment, then said softly: “then you must be a friend of allah. last night, i was crying, and i told him i didn’t have any shoes.” the man could find no words. he helped the child put the shoes on. sometimes the help someone prays for arrives through another person’s compassion. a kindness that seems small to you may kindle a great hope in someone else’s heart."},{"title":"The gifts of Allah","stem":"gifts-of-allah","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#gifts-of-allah","hay":"the gifts of allah faith and spirituality every blessing from allah brings a responsibility. we will be called to account not simply for what we have received, but for how we have honored it: if given strength—whether we have used it justly; if given authority—whether we have treated others with compassion; if given wealth—whether we have used it with integrity and generosity; if given peace and comfort—whether we have appreciated them and given thanks; if given a life partner—whether we have shown faithfulness and care; if given a child—whether we have raised them with love, patience, and responsibility. every blessing is also a test. what reveals our character is how we value what we have been given and what we do with it."},{"title":"Help us, Almighty Allah","stem":"prayer-for-everyone","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#prayer-for-everyone","hay":"help us, almighty allah faith and spirituality to those whose hearts find no peace, who have no home,\nto children without parents, to those who stand alone,\nto those in need, with nothing to eat or drink,\nto those enduring illness, struggling to make ends meet,\nto those who long for a loved one, waiting for their return,\nto those whose hope has faded, who can see no way forward,\nto those sunk in heedlessness, left without light,\nto those who hold a wish, who still dare to dream,\nto those without faith, who feel far from you—\nbring your help, almighty allah. prayer gives voice to our deepest needs and makes room for the suffering of others. as we turn to allah, may we remember everyone in need of help."},{"title":"If fate allows","stem":"if-fate-allows","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#if-fate-allows","hay":"if fate allows faith and spirituality someone who used to say, “we’ll meet,” now says, “we’ll meet if fate allows.” i find that strange. i have not changed, and my feelings toward him are as warm as before; i would still be delighted to see him. yet those words seem to place a barrier between us. i know nothing of his fate, and he knows nothing of mine. do our two fates know each other? can they arrange a meeting on our behalf? perhaps we should not expect anything from fate. perhaps this is simply another way of saying, “out of sight, out of mind.” i did not like the phrase “if fate allows” at all. do not let distance grow between you and those you care about. do not put off seeing them by saying ‘if fate allows’—meet them today and lift their spirits. bakhtiyar sirajov"},{"title":"Light of the universe","stem":"door-to-the-heart","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#door-to-the-heart","hay":"light of the universe faith and spirituality british artist william holman hunt’s painting the light of the world was exhibited at the royal academy in london. it depicts jesus christ standing at a closed door at night, holding a lantern and knocking. he waits for the door to open. according to a story told about the painting, an art critic examined it closely and asked hunt: “it’s a beautiful painting, but there’s something i don’t understand. how is the door supposed to open? have you perhaps forgotten to paint the handle?” hunt smiled and replied: “this is no ordinary door. it is the door to the human heart. that is why there is no handle on the outside. it opens only from within. unless the person inside opens it, no one can enter.” the door to the heart opens only from within. love and kindness may knock, but each of us must choose whether to let them in."},{"title":"A prayer finds you","stem":"prayer-finds-you","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#prayer-finds-you","hay":"a prayer finds you faith and spirituality our elders used to say: be a source of support for someone living in poverty, a door that someone in need can knock on, a hand that gently strokes an orphan’s head, a pair of shoes that keeps a child’s feet warm. clear a path for someone in difficulty. help someone struggling, so that the prayer you need may find its own way to you. kindness offered from the heart becomes gratitude in another person’s heart and a prayer on their lips. sometimes the blessing we wish for ourselves grows out of the compassion we show others."},{"title":"Moderation and honesty","stem":"moderation-and-honesty","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#moderation-and-honesty","hay":"moderation and honesty faith and spirituality “those who eat sparingly have no need of a doctor; those who walk an honest path have no need of a judge,” goes a saying. its wisdom lies in moderation at the table and integrity in life. eating sparingly does not mean going hungry. it means recognizing your body’s needs and eating enough without excess. walking an honest path means living conscientiously and fairly, without wronging others. of course, moderation cannot prevent every illness, and honesty cannot protect us from every injustice. the saying offers guidance, not a guarantee: it asks us to be mindful of our choices. practice moderation in eating and integrity in life. care for your health and respect the rights of others."},{"title":"My Alif has been dotted","stem":"my-alif-has-been-dotted","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#my-alif-has-been-dotted","hay":"my alif has been dotted faith and spirituality turkish has an expression, “elifim noktalandı,” which literally means “my alif has been dotted.” it conveys the feeling of sighing in sorrow, of a heart in pain—a poetic way of saying that joy has turned to sadness. one explanation draws on the shapes of letters in ottoman script. when the letter alif (ا) is followed by he (ه), they form the word “ah,” a sigh. beside the tall alif, the small, rounded he is likened to a dot. this resemblance provides the imagery behind the expression. in this poetic reading, the upright, slender alif suggests beauty and freedom. the curved he beside it seems to carry the weight of sorrow. together, the two letters become a sigh. the shapes of the letters thus reflect a change within the heart: where there was joy a moment ago, there is now sadness. sometimes a small change can express a powerful feeling. language preserves not only our thoughts, but also the traces of our joy and sorrow."},{"title":"Staying on the right path","stem":"staying-on-the-right-path","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#staying-on-the-right-path","hay":"staying on the right path faith and spirituality a man lived idly, spending his inheritance without ever working. eventually, he grew tired of this life and went to the king, asking for guidance on how to live honestly and with purpose. the king thought for a moment, then said: “take this jar, filled to the brim with olive oil, to the other end of the city without spilling a single drop. if you spill even one, you will be beheaded.” he sent two soldiers to watch over him. the man focused all his attention on the jar. taking each step carefully, he reached his destination without spilling a drop. then he returned to the palace and appeared before the king. “your majesty, i have carried out your command exactly.” the king asked: “what did you see in the city? what happened along the way?” it was market day, and the streets were so packed that a needle dropped among the crowd would not have reached the ground. yet the man replied: “your majesty, my life was at stake. i was so intent on keeping the oil from spilling that i never took my eyes off the jar to look around. i noticed neither the people nor anything that was happening.” the king said: “there is the lesson you were seeking. bring that same care and sense of responsibility to your work and to every duty you undertake. give your attention to what you do, and remember that allah always sees you. then you will find it easier to stay on the right path.” staying on the right path begins with everyday choices. care in our work, a sense of responsibility for fulfilling our commitments, and awareness that we are accountable to allah help keep us from going astray."},{"title":"O Allah, ease our suffering","stem":"prayer-for-relief","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#prayer-for-relief","hay":"o allah, ease our suffering faith and spirituality o allah! by your names, the most merciful and the ever compassionate, bring relief to our troubles. by your names, the ever forgiving and the accepter of repentance, forgive our sins and accept our repentance. by your name, the provider, bless our sustenance. by your name, the healer, grant healing to those among us who are ill. by your name, the opener, open doors of goodness for us. sincere prayer expresses both our vulnerability and our hope. turning to allah can bring comfort to the heart and strength to endure hardship."},{"title":"O Allah, give first to the mountains and stones","stem":"give-to-me-last","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#give-to-me-last","hay":"o allah, give first to the mountains and stones faith and spirituality a prayer is said to have been recited among the turkic peoples: “o allah, bestow your blessings first upon the mountains and the rocks, the forests, the animals, and the waters. then give to people, to my next-door neighbor, and to those in need. if anything remains, give to me last!” generosity means more than sharing what we have; it also means making room for others in our wishes. this prayer calls us to care about the sustenance and well-being of all living things."},{"title":"Pray while washing the dishes","stem":"gratitude-at-the-sink","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#gratitude-at-the-sink","hay":"pray while washing the dishes faith and spirituality pray as you wash the dishes. give thanks that you have dishes to wash. they are a reminder that there was food on your table, that you fed yourself or someone you love, that you cared. in that moment, remember those who cannot find enough to eat and those who have no one to share a meal with. make room for them in your prayers. everyday chores can conceal blessings we overlook. even a dish waiting to be washed can be a reason for gratitude. thankfulness begins with noticing these blessings; compassion begins with remembering those who lack them."},{"title":"A Ramadan prayer","stem":"ramadan-prayer","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#ramadan-prayer","hay":"a ramadan prayer faith and spirituality may almighty allah accept our prayers, bless our sustenance, grant healing to those among us who are ill, and forgive our sins. may he open doors of goodness for us and fill our hearts with love and compassion! wishing you a blessed eid al-fitr! heartfelt prayer strengthens hope and awakens compassion. praying for blessings, healing, and forgiveness for others as well as ourselves draws us closer both to our creator and to one another."},{"title":"The sacrificial meat","stem":"sacrificial-meat","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#sacrificial-meat","hay":"the sacrificial meat faith and spirituality i was in fifth grade. we managed well enough and did not depend on anyone’s help. my father, a bricklayer, had joined a housing cooperative. we counted the days, telling ourselves, “in six or seven months, we’ll move into our own home.” one morning, we woke to violent shaking. an earthquake had struck. thankfully, neither our family nor anyone nearby was hurt. everyone in our building was safe. but the earthquake brought a different hardship: construction stopped, and my father lost his job. however carefully we spent, the money he earned from occasional repair jobs was not enough. all winter, he searched for work. each morning he left with hope; each evening he returned discouraged. a few days before eid al-fitr, he came home beaming. he had found a job! “they told me to bring my documents. i have to get there today!” he gathered his papers and hurried out. a few hours later, our next-door neighbor rushed in. the look on his face frightened us. “don’t panic. your father has been in an accident…” at the hospital, my father was terribly pale. his arm and legs were bandaged. a driver had run a red light and hit him. we were thankful he was alive. he tried to hide his tears. “if only this hadn’t happened… now you’ll have to look after me as well.” after some time, he came home. our stove was unlit, and the rooms were cold. a few neighbors had called the municipality to ask for coal for us, but they were told we should have registered beforehand. and so eid al-fitr passed, without sweets or gifts. the weather gradually grew warmer. the bandages came off my father’s arm, but his leg had still not healed. then eid al-adha arrived. i felt hopeful. “there are twelve flats in our building. many of our neighbors will offer an animal for sacrifice. surely someone will bring us a share. mom will make one of the meat dishes i love.” i watched from the window. in the empty lot opposite, the animals were slaughtered and the meat divided. full basins were carried into the flats. whenever a door opened, the smell of cooking meat reached our home. but no one knocked on our door. not a single portion came our way. that day, my grandmother called from the village to wish us a happy eid. she heard something in my voice. “what is it, sweetheart? has your father taken a turn for the worse?” “no,” i said quietly. “the neighbors didn’t give us any meat.” we used to spend summers with grandma. she had one cow and five or six hens. the cow gave plenty of milk. grandma always sang while she milked her. early on the third morning of eid, someone knocked. it was grandma. we ran to welcome her. with tears in her eyes, she hugged us all. “my little lambs… i’ve brought you lots of meat!” we filled the fridge. my sister immediately began cutting some up. soon, the smell rising from the stove was the very smell i had been longing for over the past two days. i could not wait for the table to be laid. standing beside the stove, i picked up a piece with a fork. i was blowing on it, lifting it towards my mouth, when i heard my father say: “oh, mom… why did you have the cow slaughtered?” my hand froze. i felt sick and dizzy. i put down the fork and ran outside. the cow that helped support grandma. the cow she milked every day while singing… had she given her up because i had wanted meat? when the meal was ready, they called me in. i did not want to come, but they insisted that i sit with them. ashamed, i picked up a piece. i could not swallow it. tears rolled down my cheeks. “what’s wrong, sweetheart?” “my tooth…” i managed to say. “my tooth really hurts…” a celebration becomes complete when its joy is shared. we should notice those in need around us without waiting for them to ask. a small portion offered in time may spare someone else a heartbreaking sacrifice."},{"title":"The dervish’s appearance","stem":"dervishs-appearance","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#dervishs-appearance","hay":"the dervish’s appearance faith and spirituality one day, a dervish was asked: “why are your clothes so untidy?” the dervish replied: “i’ve been so busy tending to my inner life that i haven’t made time to put my clothes in order.” do not judge people by their clothing alone. a neat appearance is worth caring about, but a person’s true worth lies in the goodness of their heart and the way they treat others."},{"title":"The devil out of work","stem":"devil-out-of-work","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#devil-out-of-work","hay":"the devil out of work faith and spirituality a dervish was deep in thought when he noticed the devil sitting quietly beside him. “what’s the matter?” he asked. “why are you sitting so quietly? aren’t you leading anyone astray?” the devil wearily raised his head. “you humans hurt one another so much and cause such suffering that there’s no work left for me.” it is in our hands to reduce the suffering people cause one another. showing understanding instead of causing hurt, speaking up against injustice, and supporting those in difficulty are ways to lessen that suffering."},{"title":"Life’s finest blessing","stem":"lifes-finest-blessing","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#lifes-finest-blessing","hay":"life’s finest blessing faith and spirituality someone asked a dervish: “what is the best thing life can bring us?” the dervish replied: “meeting someone who truly wants to listen to you.” someone who willingly listens gives you their time and attention. being heard with genuine care makes you feel valued and less alone."},{"title":"The pleasure of Allah","stem":"pleasure-of-allah","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#pleasure-of-allah","hay":"the pleasure of allah faith and spirituality a saying attributed to khwaja ubaydullah ahrar reads: “i explored the paths that lead to allah’s pleasure and found that the shortest was to bring joy to people’s hearts. “i explored the paths that lead away from allah’s pleasure and found that the most dangerous was to wound another person’s heart.” seek to please allah by bringing joy to others. kindness draws us closer to him; hurting others takes us further away."},{"title":"The power of justice","stem":"power-of-justice","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#power-of-justice","hay":"the power of justice faith and spirituality the sword of justice will not bend, nor can justice itself be forced to yield. justice draws its strength neither from rank nor from wealth or force. times may change, but the value of justice endures. standing up for what is right gives us moral strength."},{"title":"The price of a miracle","stem":"price-of-a-miracle","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#price-of-a-miracle","hay":"the price of a miracle faith and spirituality this widely circulated story offers a lesson in kindness and hope. it is retold here as an inspirational tale, not as a documented medical case. lisa was eight when she overheard her parents talking about her little brother, oliver. all she understood was that he was seriously ill and that her parents could not afford his treatment. only a very expensive operation could save him, and there was no one the family could borrow from. lisa heard her father whisper helplessly to her tearful mother: “only a miracle can save him now.” she went to her room and took out the glass jar she kept hidden in the cupboard. she tipped her savings onto the floor and counted the coins several times. then she put them back, closed the lid, and slipped out through the back door to the pharmacy. inside, she waited for the pharmacist to notice her. but he was so absorbed in conversation that he did not see the little girl. lisa shuffled her feet and cleared her throat. finally, she took out a few coins and placed them on the glass counter. their clatter made him turn. “what do you need, little girl?” he asked impatiently. “i’m talking to my brother. i haven’t seen him for a long time.” “i want to talk to you about my brother too,” lisa said firmly. “he’s ill. i need a miracle to make him better.” “a miracle?” “his name is oliver. there’s something bad growing in his brain. dad says only a miracle can save him now. how much does a miracle cost?” the pharmacist’s voice softened. “we don’t sell miracles here, sweetheart. i’m sorry. i can’t help you.” “but i have money. if it isn’t enough, i’ll find some more. just tell me the price.” the neatly dressed man who had been listening—the pharmacist’s brother—bent down and asked: “what kind of miracle does your brother need?” “i don’t know,” lisa replied. “i just know he’s very ill. mom and dad say he needs an operation, but they haven’t got the money. that’s why i brought mine.” “how much do you have?” “one dollar and eleven cents,” she said quietly. “that’s all of it. but i can find a little more if i have to.” the man smiled. “one dollar and eleven cents… what a lovely coincidence! that’s exactly the price of the miracle your brother needs.” he took her money, held her hand, and said: “take me to your home. i’d like to see your brother and speak to your parents. let’s see what we can do about that miracle.” the man was dr. carlton armstrong, a neurosurgeon. the family was not charged for the operation. before long, oliver had recovered and returned home. his parents were overjoyed. one day, his mother whispered to his father: “that really was a miracle. i wonder how much the operation would have cost us.” lisa smiled. she knew exactly what a miracle cost: one dollar and eleven cents, and an unshakable belief that her brother could be saved. hope moves us to seek help; compassion moves us to offer it. sometimes, when a child’s innocent faith meets another person’s skill and kindness, a way forward emerges where none seemed possible."},{"title":"Begin with self-examination","stem":"begin-with-self-examination","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#begin-with-self-examination","hay":"begin with self-examination faith and spirituality a dervish was asked: “where does misfortune come from?” he replied: “begin by examining your own actions. “consider your eyes—have you looked at what is forbidden? “consider your tongue and your ears—have you spoken ill of others or listened to gossip? “consider your hands—have you reached for what is not rightfully yours or tainted your livelihood with dishonest gains? “consider your steps—have they taken you somewhere you should not have gone?” not every misfortune is caused by our own actions. yet we are responsible for examining ourselves honestly, recognizing our mistakes and putting them right. be mindful of what you look at, what you say, how you earn your living and the path you follow."},{"title":"Why wait for the last day of the world?","stem":"why-wait","category":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","href":"categories/iman-ve-meneviyyat.html#why-wait","hay":"why wait for the last day of the world? faith and spirituality one day, a lecturer at an american university begins the class with a question: “imagine that the world will end tomorrow. by this time tomorrow, everything will be over, and there is no way to escape it. how would you spend the time you have left?” the students answer one by one: “i would pray and ask allah to forgive my sins.” “i would say goodbye to everyone i love.” “i would spend the remaining time with my family.” “i would visit my parents.” “i would enjoy my favorite foods one last time.” “i would finally get all the sleep i wanted.” “i would walk in the woods and enjoy the beauty of nature.” “i would watch the sunrise and the sunset.” “i would look at the stars at night.” “i would cook my favorite meal and gather everyone i love around the table.” “i would go somewhere i had always dreamed of visiting and spend my final moments there.” “i would find the people i had hurt, apologize and ask for their forgiveness.” the lecturer writes every answer on the board, then asks with a smile: “so why wait until the world is about to end to do these things?” do not leave what matters most until your final day. express your love, apologize, make time for those you cherish and enjoy life’s simple pleasures today."},{"title":"A father’s footsteps","stem":"fathers-footsteps","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#fathers-footsteps","hay":"a father’s footsteps family and upbringing a father said to his son: “watch your step, son. be careful where you’re going.” his son replied: “you watch your step too, dad. i’m following in your footsteps.” guiding a child means more than giving advice; it means walking the path yourself. children learn from your words and take their example from your actions."},{"title":"A festive gift","stem":"festive-gift","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#festive-gift","hay":"a festive gift family and upbringing i asked a wise elder: “what should i buy for the celebrations?” he smiled. “bring joy to someone’s heart. make the children smile. if you can, ease the burden of someone in need and receive their blessing. give others a reason to celebrate too.” a gift’s worth lies in the thought and care behind it. the joy we share is often the greatest gift of all."},{"title":"A mother’s love","stem":"mothers-love","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#mothers-love","hay":"a mother’s love family and upbringing i am one year old—completely happy. my mother is always beside me. i am two—she is no longer beside me all the time. what has changed? i am three—my mother is at work, and my nanny is with me. i am five—my mother is in a meeting, and i am with my grandmother. i am seven—my mother is at a seminar, and i am at school. my teacher is with me. i am thirteen—a teenager. my mother still needs to work. i spend more and more time with my friends. i am seventeen—my mother comes home tired from work. i am either in class or at a café with friends. i am twenty—young, caught up in life. when my mother is not at home, my friends are there. i am twenty-three—i have started working. now my mother is at home, while i am at work. i am thirty—and i wonder: when did my mother and i last sit down for an unhurried conversation? when did we last enjoy a day together? somewhere along the way, i have missed something… i am thirty-five—married. my mother has her home, and i have mine. i am forty—my mother waits for me to visit, just as i once waited for her to come home. i am fifty—i, too, am growing older. but one wish has never aged: to embrace my mother, rest my head in her lap, and be a child again beside her. i am fifty-five—the mother of my children is beside me. my own mother is no longer alive. i live with the ache of missing her. amid everyday demands, we sometimes put off spending time with those dearest to us. there is always more work to do. visits are postponed; conversations are left unfinished. meanwhile, children grow up and parents grow older. life is like a film unfolding before our eyes. but we cannot rewind its scenes or pause a moment and live it again. hold your child today. take their hand and listen. make time for your parents today. do not put off spending time together until tomorrow: tomorrow will be a different day. to paraphrase erich fromm’s view in the art of loving, motherly love offers unconditional acceptance and shelter; it cannot be forced into existence or obtained by demanding it. love is felt not only in what we hold in our hearts, but in the time and attention we give. tomorrow cannot bring back the day we could have shared with someone we love."},{"title":"The advice from his mother that Fakir Baykurt never forgot","stem":"mothers-lasting-lesson","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#mothers-lasting-lesson","hay":"the advice from his mother that fakir baykurt never forgot family and upbringing an account of the turkish writer fakir baykurt’s childhood tells how he once said to his mother: “mom, i’d love some tea. shall we have a cup?” his mother agreed and took him to a nearby teahouse run by hüseyin, who was known for his limp. “please bring my boy a glass of tea,” she asked. when the tea arrived, the boy eagerly picked up the glass and took a large gulp. it burned his mouth, and he dropped the glass. it landed on the dirt floor without breaking. he looked anxiously at his mother, expecting a slap. instead, she calmly called the owner and ordered another glass of tea. this time, the boy blew on his tea and drank it in small sips. years passed, but baykurt never forgot that day. whenever he asked his mother why she had neither scolded nor slapped him, she left the question unanswered. he finally received his answer years later, at the village school where he taught. one day, his mother, elif, sat in on a lesson. during the class, baykurt lost his temper, raised his voice, and scolded the children harshly. afterwards, he asked: “well, mom? did you like my teaching?” “what can i say, son? it wasn’t bad.” her answer disappointed him. “school inspectors observe my lessons and give me high marks. and all you can say is ‘it wasn’t bad’?” his mother turned to him. “for years, you’ve asked why i didn’t slap you when you spilled that tea. now i’ll tell you. listen carefully.” she paused, then continued: “if i had lost my temper with you that day, the lion inside you would have turned away, hurt. if i had beaten you, that lion would have died. perhaps you would never have become the teacher you are today. be patient, son. don’t kill the lions inside these children with shouting and blows.” the way we respond to a child’s mistake can strengthen or undermine their confidence. guidance offered with patience and love helps children learn from their mistakes while still believing in their own worth."},{"title":"Father and son","stem":"father-and-son","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#father-and-son","hay":"father and son family and upbringing after a bitter argument with his father, a young man left home. hurt and angry, his father refused even to hear his son’s name mentioned. every night, his mother went into his room, sat on the edge of the bed, and held his pillow close. “i miss him,” she would say. “please, let’s find him and bring him home.” his father stayed silent and refused to discuss it. days became weeks, and weeks became months. one day, unable to bear his wife’s tears any longer, the father took a folded piece of paper from his pocket. “go to this address. you’ll find him there. but don’t tell him i gave it to you.” he added a few more words. she listened carefully, though her thoughts were already with her son. the address led to a repair workshop. her son was busy in his work clothes. for a while, she stood in the doorway watching him. then she called his name, her voice trembling. he looked up. for a moment, he could not believe his eyes. then he hurried over and embraced her. he asked after everyone, leaving his father until last. “how is he? is he still angry with me?” his mother looked into his face. “come home, my dear. we miss you so much.” his expression hardened. “no, mom. i don’t want to go back to someone who hasn’t looked for me even once. he’s probably happier without me.” she handed him the sweets she had brought. then she said quietly: “i won’t force you. but there’s something your father asked me to tell you. he said to be careful of the man you’ve become friends with this past month. and he hopes you’ll make peace with your old friend.” the young man froze. “dad… how does he know about that?” his mother did not answer. she hugged him once more and left. as soon as she got home, she asked her husband: “if you knew where he was, why didn’t you tell me? how long have you known?” his eyes filled with tears. “since the day he left. sometimes i passed the workshop at lunchtime to see whether he was eating, whether he was all right. some evenings, i went just to catch a glimpse of him before coming home.” “then why didn’t you go over and talk to him?” he lowered his head. “my pride wouldn’t let me. i thought he should make the first move… but every day, i hoped he would come home. he’s my son.” just then, there was a knock at the door. the mother opened it and found her son standing outside. he came in and stopped in front of his father. for a few moments, neither spoke. then his father stepped towards him. “i should have come and talked to you, son.” tears came to the young man’s eyes. “and the things i said… i’m sorry, dad.” they embraced, and his mother joined them. the hurt between them had not vanished in an instant. but now they were together, ready to begin talking it through. love that is never expressed in words or actions can feel like absence. what matters most is not who makes the first move towards reconciliation, but that someone makes it before more time is lost."},{"title":"A wise father’s advice to his son","stem":"fathers-advice","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#fathers-advice","hay":"a wise father’s advice to his son family and upbringing my dear son, let good judgment guide you through life. be thoughtful in your dealings with others, measured in your words, and fair in your actions. wisdom means looking beyond your own advantage and considering the rights of others. value your life. its worth lies not simply in its length, but in how you live it. make room each day for learning, love, and kindness. as fruit sweetens with ripening, we mature by learning from what we experience. listen to those with a wealth of life experience. but remember: wisdom comes not merely from growing older, but from reflecting on what life has taught us. a thoughtless act in youth can trouble the heart for years. when making a decision, consider not only what you want today, but what will let you live with a clear conscience tomorrow. if you make a mistake, own it, put it right where you can, and learn from it. the good you sow when you are young may bear fruit in later years. honesty, diligence, and respect for others can sustain you throughout your life. strength is a gift, but it should serve judgment and conscience. use it to protect and build, not to crush others. remember that none of us is here forever. cherish the life allah has given you. let the thought of mortality deepen your appreciation of today and of the people beside you, rather than turn you away from life. in my own life, i have tried to keep two simple habits: eating in moderation and staying active. i offer them not as a cure for every ailment, but as part of caring for myself. pay attention to your body’s needs, and do not neglect them. notice, too, how people approach learning. some think carefully, ask questions, and consider the consequences of their actions. some have not yet found their way, but are willing to listen and learn. good counsel can help them find direction. others neither recognize what they do not know nor listen to anyone else. believing they know everything, they close the door to learning. strive to be thoughtful. never be ashamed to ask when you do not know. listen to advice and weigh it carefully. above all, guard against the belief that you have nothing left to learn. this is my wish for you: a clear mind, a peaceful conscience, and a heart open to learning. wisdom is not knowing everything, but recognizing what you do not know and being willing to learn. let judgment guide you, conscience keep you true, and strength serve them both."},{"title":"Grandfather and grandson go shopping","stem":"stay-calm-arif","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#stay-calm-arif","hay":"grandfather and grandson go shopping family and upbringing a grandfather was shopping with his grandson at the supermarket. as they walked down the aisles, putting groceries into their basket, the boy demanded everything he saw. “get me this! i want that too!” whenever his grandfather said no, the boy shouted even louder. each time, the old man said quietly: “stay calm, arif. we’re nearly finished. we’ll be home soon.” eventually, the boy grabbed a chocolate egg from a shelf, threw it on the floor, and stamped on it. his grandfather took a deep breath. “stay calm, arif. just a little longer, and we’ll be going home.” then he turned to a nearby member of staff. “i’m very sorry. i’ll pay for that too.” after paying at the checkout, they left the store. a woman who had witnessed the whole scene followed them outside and approached the grandfather. “i was so impressed by your patience! no matter how much arif misbehaved, you never once raised your voice.” the grandfather thanked her and smiled. “the boy’s name is rafiq. i’m arif.” real strength is keeping your composure when things get difficult. sometimes the first person who needs to hear “stay calm” is you."},{"title":"Hold my hand","stem":"hold-my-hand","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#hold-my-hand","hay":"hold my hand family and upbringing a father and his little daughter were crossing a bridge. glancing at the river below, he grew a little anxious. “sweetheart, hold my hand tightly so you don’t fall.” the girl looked up at him. “no, daddy. you hold mine.” “what’s the difference?” he asked, puzzled. “there’s a big difference! if i’m holding your hand, i might let go when i get scared. but if you’re holding mine, i know you won’t let go, no matter what.” trust means knowing that someone will hold on when you are afraid. don’t wait for those you love to ask for help. be there when they need you, and reach for their hand."},{"title":"I’m tired, Mom","stem":"im-tired-mom","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#im-tired-mom","hay":"i’m tired, mom family and upbringing “i’m tired, mom.” “of what, son?” “everything. work, friends, going out, trying to please everyone. i’m exhausted.” “rest your head in my lap, son. let it all go for a moment. let me share your pain.” “thank you, mom. i’m so glad you’re here. with you, i forget how old i am and everything i’ve been through. may you have many more years ahead of you.” “a mother gives you life, son. i’ll always be here for you.” “mom, we wear you out too. forgive us.” “you don’t wear me out. i’m tired because i no longer have a mother of my own. i have no mother’s lap to rest my head in. don’t wear yourself out, son. cherish what you have.” a mother’s embrace is the most comforting refuge in the world. whoever has a mother has a place to rest and lay down their burdens. cherish what you have."},{"title":"Mother and son","stem":"mother-and-son","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#mother-and-son","hay":"mother and son family and upbringing the man set the suitcases against the wall, straightened up, and took a deep breath. “mom, this really is a lovely place.” “of course it is, son,” the elderly woman replied. for a while, they stood in silence, looking around the room. she opened her small suitcase and took out her nightdress, slippers, a few towels, and a framed photograph. she placed the photograph on the bedside table. it showed a boy in school uniform, standing stiffly at attention. his eyes were full of tears, his lips drawn into a trembling pout. the man went to the window and pulled back the curtain. pale daylight entered the room. it was freezing outside. he rested his hands on the radiator and watched mud-spattered cars passing along the street. “will this wardrobe be big enough for you, mom?” he asked without turning around. she did not hear him. sitting on the bed, leaning slightly to one side, she was gazing at the frightened child in the worn frame. the photograph had been taken on his first day of school. the memory returned as vividly as if it were yesterday. the evening before, she had spent hours telling him what a wonderful place school was. yet the next morning, she had barely managed to get him into the classroom. she had stayed at school all day, going to him at every break. “i’m here, son. i’m waiting for you.” but each time, he had gripped her hand more tightly and cried: “don’t leave me, mom. i’m so scared.” the sun was setting. the man took his hands off the radiator and rested them against the misted window. “open the other suitcase too, mom. let’s put your things away.” “don’t let me keep you, son. i can do it myself. there isn’t much, anyway.” she reached out with trembling hands and took his hand. for a moment, she seemed about to say something. then she changed her mind. she swallowed. “the children will be wondering where you are.” the door creaked shut. the elderly woman was left alone in the room with its peeling paint and the frightened little boy in the photograph. she sat on the edge of the bed for a while, then put on her slippers and went to the window. her son was getting into his car. she was frightened. the day before, she had listened for hours to how lovely this place was. but she was still frightened. his handprint remained on the glass. gently, she rested her cheek against it. her eyes filled with tears, and her lips began to tremble. “don’t leave me, son. i’m so scared,” she whispered. her words faded against the misted glass. outside, her son sat in his car and waved. that night, the other residents on her floor heard her sobbing. such sounds were not unfamiliar in the corridors of the care home. growing older does not lessen our need for love and closeness. caring for a parent means more than providing comfort and meeting daily needs; it also means helping them feel remembered and less alone."},{"title":"Only my mother truly weeps","stem":"only-my-mother-truly-weeps","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#only-my-mother-truly-weeps","hay":"only my mother truly weeps family and upbringing only my mother’s tears are true; all the rest are just for show! these words express the depth of a mother’s love. her ability to feel her child’s pain as her own is one of the most tender expressions of that selfless love."},{"title":"Raising a child","stem":"raising-a-child","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#raising-a-child","hay":"raising a child family and upbringing a boy often defied his parents. whatever they told him to do, he did the opposite. things were not going well at school either: he showed little interest in lessons and left his homework unfinished. his mother hit him in anger, while his father shut him in his room as punishment. neither approach made things better. one day, as his mother was taking him to school, they came across a man performing with a bear. a crowd had gathered around them. the man played a tambourine, and the bear, with a ring through its nose, jumped and danced in response to his movements. the woman stopped and thought, “i can’t manage one child, yet this man can make even a bear obey!” she approached him. “my son won’t listen to me. how did you train this animal?” the man smiled. “there’s a way to do everything. i have my own methods.” “could you help me too?” the bear handler looked at the boy. “bring him to me for a week. i’ll teach him to do as he’s told.” delighted, the woman began taking her son to the man every day. within a few days, the boy changed. he no longer argued with his mother and quietly did what she asked. by the end of the week, she thought her problem was solved. she thanked the man and blessed him. the neighbors were astonished. the once unruly boy now seemed quiet and obedient. his teacher was pleased too and congratulated his mother. before long, however, other problems emerged. the boy no longer rebelled openly, but he began secretly taking his classmates’ belongings, covering up what he had done, and lying. his teacher called his mother. “i’m concerned about some changes in your son’s behavior. he takes other children’s things and then denies it. we need to understand why.” distraught, the woman went looking for the bear handler. but he had already moved on. seeing the parents’ distress, an experienced neighbor said: “i know a kind, wise man. go and speak to him. perhaps he can help you find a way forward.” the mother took her son to see him and explained what had happened. the wise man said: “let me meet your son a few times over the coming week. i’d like to get to know him and understand what he has been through.” after their second meeting, he asked to speak to the mother. he looked deeply concerned. “your son is afraid to admit when he has done something wrong. he believes that telling the truth will always lead to punishment. how have you been treating him?” she described the punishments at home and then told him about the bear handler. the wise man sighed. “you wanted to change your child’s behavior, but you did not try to understand his feelings. fear can silence a child for a while. but silence is not understanding, and obedience is not the same as learning right from wrong. a child who fears punishment may try to hide a mistake instead of putting it right.” he paused, then continued: “raising a child means building trust, not breaking their will. listen to your son, make time for him, and show him your love. explain the rules clearly and follow them yourself. when he makes a mistake, help him understand its consequences and make amends without humiliating him.” he concluded: “i can offer guidance, but i cannot change your relationship at home for you. change begins when your child feels safe, loved, and heard in your company.” the aim of raising a child is to nurture understanding of right and wrong, rather than silence born of fear. love, trust, clear boundaries, and a parent’s own example are the foundations of that process."},{"title":"Puppies for sale","stem":"puppies-for-sale","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#puppies-for-sale","hay":"puppies for sale family and upbringing a little boy saw a sign in a shop window that read “puppies for sale” and went inside. “how much are the puppies?” he asked. “between thirty and fifty dollars,” the shopkeeper replied. “each one has a different price.” the boy took all the money from his pocket, placed it on the counter, and counted it carefully. “i only have thirteen dollars and twenty-seven cents,” he said, disappointed. then he asked hopefully: “could i at least look at them?” the shopkeeper smiled and called to his wife in the back room. a moment later, she appeared with five adorable, plump puppies following her. one lagged behind, struggling to walk. the boy could not take his eyes off it. “what’s wrong with its leg?” “the vet says it was born with a problem in one of its leg bones. it will always have difficulty walking.” the boy immediately said: “that’s the one i want!” the shopkeeper looked surprised. “i wouldn’t have expected you to choose that one. if you want it, you can have it for free.” the boy’s expression changed. looking straight at the shopkeeper, he said firmly: “i don’t want you to give it to me for free. it’s worth just as much as the other puppies. i’ll give you thirteen dollars and twenty-seven cents now, then pay five dollars a week until i’ve paid the full thirty.” the shopkeeper tried again to change his mind. “but it won’t be able to run and play with you like the others.” “it deserves a chance too. it’s just as valuable as the rest,” the boy replied. then he bent down and lifted his trouser leg. his leg, weakened by polio, was supported by metal braces. he looked at the shopkeeper and said quietly: “i can’t run and play either. this puppy and i both need a friend who understands us and gives us support.” a living being’s worth is not measured by what it can or cannot do. true friendship means understanding and supporting one another, rather than turning away because of differences."},{"title":"The rotten seed","stem":"rotten-seed","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#rotten-seed","hay":"the rotten seed family and upbringing after his wife died, a father refused to care for his young son, who was often ill. he dismissed the boy as a “rotten seed” and eventually placed him in a children’s home. years passed. one day, the father saw his grown son outside a school. he could hardly believe his eyes. before him stood a strong, healthy young man with bright, lively eyes. looking at him with regret, he exclaimed: “i never thought you would recover and grow so strong. look at you now! how did this happen? how did you overcome all those illnesses?” the young man’s eyes filled with tears. he looked at the father who had once rejected him, then pointed to an elderly woman approaching them with a smile. “after you said, ‘a rotten seed will never sprout,’ and left me at the children’s home, this woman adopted me. she said, ‘a seed watered with love deserves a chance to grow.’ she never withheld her love or care from me.” a child’s worth is not measured by their health. cruel words can crush hope, while love and steady care can help a child discover their strength. no child is a “rotten seed.”"},{"title":"Spinach","stem":"spinach","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#spinach","hay":"spinach family and upbringing we lived in a remote village without even a proper road. my mother seemed to cook nothing but spinach. in my childish mind, i wondered whether my father’s death had left her unable to think clearly. “eat plenty. it will make you strong,” she would say. morning, noon, and night, there was spinach and cornbread on our table. i grew so tired of spinach that i could hardly bear to look at it. one day, i told her: “when i grow up, get a job, and have a family, i’ll never buy spinach. nobody will cook it in my house!” my mother smiled. she said she could hardly wait for that day and promised that, however old she was, she would dance at my wedding. eventually, i left the village to continue my secondary education in town. i scraped by on a small scholarship. when i came home once a month, i would not touch the spinach my mother served. i dreamed of returning to the dormitory and eating something different, something tastier. i kept studying. with a government student loan, i completed university, then a master’s degree and a doctorate. by then, my mother had grown old, and her back was bent. my fiancée and i returned to the village for our wedding. that day, my mother cried—and danced, just as she had promised all those years before. at the time, i did not understand why tears ran down her face as she danced. the years passed. my mother died two years ago. one day, in her old chest, i found a diary she had kept. on one page, she had written: “all i had left was some spinach seed. i grew spinach and a little maize. we had next to nothing. i cooked the same meal over and over so my children would not go hungry. but i did not complain. “‘spinach is very good for you. that’s why i give it to you three times a day,’ i would tell them, and my heart would ache. it was all i could do for them. i love my children more than my own life.” i learned to love spinach at fifty. now my wife often wonders why i buy it so frequently. she thinks i love the taste. she does not know that whenever i smell it cooking, i remember everything my mother endured to raise us—and the way she danced at my wedding with tears streaming down her face. sometimes a meal we grew tired of as children concealed a mother’s poverty and quiet sacrifice. we should not have to wait years to recognize the love behind it."},{"title":"Teeth falling out","stem":"fallen-teeth","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#fallen-teeth","hay":"teeth falling out family and upbringing a king dreamed that all his teeth had fallen out. in the morning, he summoned a dream interpreter. “what does this dream mean?” he asked. “your majesty, all your loved ones will die before you.” the king was shaken by these words. then, in anger, he sent the interpreter away. he summoned another interpreter and described the same dream. the second interpreter replied: “your majesty, you will outlive all your loved ones.” the king’s face brightened. he rewarded the interpreter and sent him on his way. both interpreters had said the same thing. the first had emphasised the deaths of the king’s loved ones; the second had emphasised that the king would live on. the impact of words depends not only on their meaning, but also on how they are spoken. wisdom includes telling the truth with consideration for the listener’s feelings."},{"title":"The most beautiful gift","stem":"most-beautiful-gift","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#most-beautiful-gift","hay":"the most beautiful gift family and upbringing for a father—honor,\nfor a mother—respect,\nfor a child—tenderness,\nfor a spouse—love,\nfor a friend—loyalty,\nfor society—service,\nfor those who have passed away—a prayer,\nfor the world—humanity,\nfor the life to come—the good we do in this one. the most beautiful gifts cannot always be bought. respect, love, compassion, and loyalty nurture our relationships; the good we do for others gives meaning to our lives."},{"title":"The nest builder","stem":"nest-builder","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#nest-builder","hay":"the nest builder family and upbringing “the female bird builds the nest,” says a proverb. it honors the care and effort a woman brings to family life, turning a house into a home. appreciating that work means more than saying thank you: it means sharing the care and responsibility. recognize and value a woman’s contribution to her family. a home grows stronger through mutual love, respect, and shared effort."},{"title":"A word spoken in anger","stem":"nails-lesson","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#nails-lesson","hay":"a word spoken in anger family and upbringing in a village there lived a boy who was very quick-tempered and impatient. even the slightest remark would make him angry, and when he lost his temper, he did not hesitate to hurt the person in front of him. his father was deeply concerned about this side of his son’s character. one day, he gave him a bag of nails and said: ‘my son, whenever you get angry with someone and say something hurtful, hammer one of these nails into the fence around the yard.’ at first, the boy thought this was pointless. but the next day he became angry and hammered a nail into the fence. then he lost his temper again and hammered in another. as the days passed, the number of nails in the fence began to grow. after a while, the boy began to pay attention to his behavior. reaching for a nail every time he became angry made him think. at last, one day he went to his father and said: ‘father, i will try not to lose my temper anymore.’ his father smiled and replied: ‘very good. now, for every day that you control yourself and hurt no one, pull one nail out of the fence.’ the boy did as he was told. days passed, then months, and at last all the nails had been removed from the fence. the boy called out joyfully to his father: ‘father, look! i have pulled out all the nails.’ his father took him over to the fence and said quietly: ‘you have removed all the nails. but look: the marks they left are still there. the same thing happens when you get angry and lash out at someone with your words. you may apologize afterward, you may feel sorry, and that person may even forgive you. but a wound in the heart does not always heal without a scar.’ the boy looked at the holes in the fence for a long time. from that day on, when he became angry, he learned first to remain silent and then to think. a word spoken in anger can leave a mark on the heart that even an apology cannot erase. when anger rises, pause and think before you speak."},{"title":"The value of your family","stem":"hour-with-dad","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#hour-with-dad","hay":"the value of your family family and upbringing one evening, a little boy approached his father. “dad, can i ask you something?” “of course, son.” “how much do you earn in an hour?” his father frowned. “why do you want to know?” “i’m just curious. please tell me, dad.” at last, his father answered: “i earn a hundred dollars an hour.” the boy lowered his head and fell silent for a moment. then he asked hesitantly: “could you lend me fifty dollars?” his father grew angry. “so that’s why you asked! you probably want another toy you don’t need. i don’t work all day for that. go to your room and get to bed!” without a word, the boy went to his room and quietly closed the door. his father paced for a while. as his anger faded, he realized he had never asked what his son wanted the money for. perhaps it was something important to him. feeling ashamed, he went to the boy’s room and knocked softly. “are you asleep, son?” “no, dad. i’m awake.” his father came in and sat beside him. “i’m sorry i snapped at you earlier. i had a difficult day and took it out on you. here are the fifty dollars you asked for.” the boy thanked him happily. then he pulled his savings from beneath his pillow and began to count them. seeing the money, his father grew angry again. “if you already had money, why did you ask me for more?” the boy gathered the notes and looked up at him. “because i didn’t have enough. now i have exactly a hundred dollars. dad, can i buy an hour of your time? please come home early tomorrow so we can have dinner together.” his father’s eyes filled with tears. he held his son close and apologized once more. caring for your family means more than earning money. children need your love, your attention, and time with you—things no gift can replace."},{"title":"Waiting for the teeth","stem":"waiting-for-the-teeth","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#waiting-for-the-teeth","hay":"waiting for the teeth family and upbringing on a cold winter evening, an elderly couple walked into mcdonald’s and sat down. the husband went to the counter and ordered a hamburger, a large portion of fries, and a large coke. back at the table, he cut the hamburger in half and placed one half in front of his wife. he counted out the fries, dividing them equally. then he took a sip of coke and passed it to her. people nearby watched sympathetically. “they must be short of money,” they thought. “they’re sharing a meal for one.” the husband began eating with a healthy appetite. his wife left her food untouched, watching him and occasionally taking a sip of coke. a young employee noticed and approached their table. “excuse me, sir. i couldn’t help noticing that your wife hasn’t touched her food. would she prefer something else? i’d be happy to bring it.” the man smiled. “thank you, son, but we’re quite all right. we’ve been married for sixty years. we share everything.” still puzzled, the employee turned to the wife. “then what are you waiting for, ma’am?” she nodded toward her husband and replied calmly: “the teeth, dear. i’m waiting for the teeth!” everyone has their own way of living, and every family has its own habits. what seems unusual to us may be an ordinary part of someone else’s daily life."},{"title":"Weeds","stem":"weeds","category":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","href":"categories/aile-ve-terbiye.html#weeds","hay":"weeds family and upbringing when we were schoolchildren, we spent the summer holidays with our family in our garden. my late father wanted to teach us the value of hard work, so he left us chores to do while he was at work. we watered the melons he had planted. we carried drinking water from the well in our neighbor uncle aghasalim’s garden, since the water in our own well was unfit to drink. we had learned to dig the soil. we also did the weeding: with a spade, we turned the soil over, crushing and breaking up the weeds. but they were not so easy to get rid of. one day, father called us over and explained, “if you want to get rid of the weeds, dig deep with the spade; until you pull out their roots, the job isn’t finished.” deal with the roots of problems and bad habits. removing only what is visible above the surface will not keep them from returning. bakhtiyar sirajov"},{"title":"Those who give love receive love","stem":"those-who-give-love-receive-love","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#those-who-give-love-receive-love","hay":"those who give love receive love love and marriage an old story set in china tells of a young woman named li-li who married and moved into a home with her husband and his mother. before long, tensions arose between the two women. their temperaments were very different. a remark that seemed harmless to one would hurt the other, and even small matters turned into arguments. the strain wore down both li-li and her husband, who felt caught between two people he loved. one day, li-li visited an old friend of her father’s, a man who prepared herbal remedies. she poured out her troubles, then said bitterly:\n“i can’t bear it anymore. i want to be rid of her for good.” the old man listened and brought her a container of herbs. he told her it was a slow-acting poison. then he added:\n“but there is one condition: you must treat your mother-in-law kindly. don’t interrupt her or argue with everything she says. take care of her, so no one notices any hostility between you.” li-li returned home and began adding the mixture to her mother-in-law’s food. she cooked her favorite meals, asked how she was feeling, and listened instead of starting arguments. at first, she did this only to avoid suspicion. but gradually, she began to notice things about her mother-in-law that she had never seen before. sometimes there was worry behind her sharp words, and concern behind her criticism. her mother-in-law was changing, too. she responded to li-li’s kindness with warmth and began treating her like her own daughter. arguments gave way to quiet conversations, and resentment slowly softened into affection. one day, li-li looked at her mother-in-law and realized she was afraid of losing her. she hurried back to the old man.\n“please help me! give me something to save her. i love her. i don’t want anything to happen to her!” “don’t be afraid, li-li,” he said gently. “the mixture i gave you wasn’t poison. you haven’t poisoned her.” li-li stared at him in astonishment. “what was poisoning your relationship was the resentment that had built up between you,” he continued. “by changing the way you treated her, you gave yourselves a chance to know and understand each other. at first, kindness was something you chose to practice. then it became something you truly felt.” li-li’s eyes filled with tears. she could hardly wait to go home and embrace her mother-in-law. resentment can keep us from seeing the good in another person. listening, understanding, and showing care can open the way to change. genuine closeness grows where both people treat each other with respect."},{"title":"Before you marry","stem":"before-you-marry","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#before-you-marry","hay":"before you marry love and marriage a young couple preparing to marry received this advice from their elders: “don’t compare your marriage with anyone else’s. don’t say, ‘look at her husband…’ or ‘look at his wife…’ from the outside, you can never know what life is really like in someone else’s home. build your own life together. “don’t expect perfection from each other. everyone has flaws. learn which differences you can accept with understanding and which problems you need to work on together. don’t bring up past mistakes every time you argue. “don’t expect your partner to read your mind. speak openly and calmly about your wishes, worries, and hurt feelings. listen to understand, not just to reply. “sharing a life doesn’t mean losing your individuality. you will each have your own thoughts, interests, and need for time alone. in the familiar parable, hedgehogs seek enough closeness for warmth without hurting one another. stay close, but give each other room to breathe. “neither of you should rule over the other or be expected to obey without question. be companions who walk through life side by side. make decisions together, share responsibilities, and respect each other’s right to an opinion. “don’t promise, ‘everything will always be wonderful.’ instead, say, ‘when things get difficult, you won’t have to face them alone.’ “even when you’re upset with each other, remember to say goodnight. not every disagreement can be resolved in one evening, but you can still show kindness while you work through it. let a new day bring another chance to listen and make peace.” a strong marriage is built by two people who know how to listen, understand, and speak with care—not by two perfect people. sharing a home means supporting each other, not owning each other."},{"title":"Keep your hearts close","stem":"keep-your-hearts-close","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#keep-your-hearts-close","hay":"keep your hearts close love and marriage why do we raise our voices during an argument when the person beside us can already hear us? one way to picture it is this: as hurt grows, so does the distance between our hearts. we raise our voices as though we could shout across that distance. yet shouting often makes it harder, not easier, to hear one another. where there is love and trust, a quiet word can be enough. sometimes a whisper, a glance, or a hand resting on another’s hand says more than a long speech. when we feel heard and understood, there is less need to raise our voices. closeness does not mean never disagreeing. it means treating each other with care even when we disagree. so when anger rises, pause. say what you need to say without belittling the other person. explain your hurt without trying to hurt them in return. if you cannot speak calmly, take a break and return to the conversation later. let the argument end—not the bond between you. aim to understand each other, not to silence each other. choose words that leave a way back to closeness once the anger has passed."},{"title":"Early marriage","stem":"early-marriage","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#early-marriage","hay":"early marriage love and marriage i had just graduated from university and started teaching at a school. one day, i was in the teachers’ lounge marking exam papers. i was erasing and correcting some of the girls’ wrong answers so they could move up to the next class. a man i did not recognize entered the room. after watching me for a moment, he asked: “why are you changing the girls’ answers?” i put down my pen. “if some of these girls fail, their fathers will take them out of school and marry them off at twelve or thirteen. if they pass, they may get a few more years of education. in that time, they could learn, gain confidence, and perhaps find a different path for their lives.” the man was silent for a while. then he said goodbye and left. about half an hour later, the principal called me into his office. “you spoke to someone in the teachers’ lounge. what did you tell him?” i grew anxious. “did he complain about me?” “no. he had come to inspect the school.” my heart sank. the principal continued: “he said there was a young teacher here who cared about the children’s futures. he asked me to pass on his thanks.” i breathed a sigh of relief. yet my unease remained. changing a few answers would not be enough to protect those girls’ futures. their right to stay in school should never depend on an exam mark—or a teacher secretly altering it. a teacher’s care can change a child’s life. but no girl should have to rely on such a chance to protect her childhood and education. we all share a responsibility to oppose child marriage and support girls in learning and shaping their own futures."},{"title":"Friendship and love","stem":"friendship-and-love","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#friendship-and-love","hay":"friendship and love love and marriage write your quarrels on leaves, so that autumn may carry them away and take the hurt with them. write your anger on clouds, so that it may fall with the rain and your heart may clear with the sky. write your hatred on snow, so that it may melt when the sun returns. but write friendship and love in children’s hearts—through kind words, care, and the example you set. as the children grow, let that love grow with them and find its way into other hearts. nurture love rather than resentment. the kindness we plant in others can live on long after us."},{"title":"I kiss your eyes","stem":"i-kiss-your-eyes","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#i-kiss-your-eyes","hay":"i kiss your eyes love and marriage do you know why people say, “i kiss your eyes”? because the eyes bear so much of the pain of longing: they watch and wait, shed tears, and search for a loved one. perhaps that is why they deserve the joy of reunion most of all. our eyes hold the longing we cannot put into words. to kiss a loved one’s eyes is to offer tenderness to the ache of missing someone."},{"title":"I love you","stem":"i-love-you","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#i-love-you","hay":"i love you love and marriage the story goes that a nightingale fell in love with a white rose. every night, he perched on her branch and sang of his love. but the rose remained indifferent, offering no answer to his songs. one day, the nightingale gathered his courage and opened his heart once more. “i love you with all my heart.” the rose answered sharply: “leave me alone. i don’t love you.” however much he pleaded, her answer did not change. overwhelmed by the pain of rejection, the nightingale saw no way forward and took his own life. according to the legend, his blood turned the white rose’s petals red. the rose fell silent. nothing could bring the nightingale or his song back. and so, the tale says, the red rose came to mean “i love you.” unrequited love can hurt deeply, but it does not diminish a person’s worth. love should never have to be proved through self-destruction. being gentle with another person’s feelings matters, as does respecting their choices and valuing your own life."},{"title":"Loving from afar","stem":"loving-from-afar","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#loving-from-afar","hay":"loving from afar love and marriage two friends meet and start talking. “i think i’ve fallen for someone.” “that’s wonderful! does she know?” “no.” “then why don’t you tell her?” “i would, but i’m afraid of disturbing my peaceful life. i’d only be asking for trouble. we’d start going to the cinema and the theater, walking hand in hand. then would come the arguments and the making up: ‘why were you looking at her?’ ‘why did you smile at that woman?’ if we’re going to break up in the end, why put ourselves through all that?” he pauses, then adds: “i’d rather just love her from afar.” sometimes we fear the end of a love that has not even begun. in trying to protect ourselves from a possible separation, we may also deny ourselves the chance of closeness and joy."},{"title":"The purple jacket","stem":"purple-jacket","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#purple-jacket","hay":"the purple jacket love and marriage a tale about the yörük communities tells of a purple jacket placed among a bride’s belongings. this short traditional garment was kept at the very bottom of her dowry chest, with the hope that she would never need to wear it. the historical basis of this custom is disputed. the yörük culture research centre at süleyman demirel university links the account to literary fiction associated with osman şahin, rather than to a documented practice. other publications still present it as tradition. for the purple jacket was no ordinary piece of clothing. it was a call for help. when a woman put it on and stood on a rooftop, in the village square, or somewhere everyone could see her, her message was: “i am suffering in this marriage. i was married against my will, or my husband is mistreating me. i want to leave. help me!” in the tale, that call never went unanswered. the villagers would gather around the woman, defend her, and demand an explanation from her husband. the shame lay not in her asking for help, but in his having driven her to that point. at the sight of the purple jacket, everyday life seemed to stop. people busy milking cows, spinning wool, or weaving rugs would set their work aside. mothers and grandmothers would leave everything and hurry to her. if a wedding was taking place, the music would fall silent and the celebrations would pause. one woman’s distress became the concern of the whole community. a man who had abused his wife would be ashamed to appear in public. even at the teahouse, no one would meet his gaze. he could not stand in the way of her wish to leave, and if he sought to marry again, few would trust him. in this tale, the purple jacket is a promise that a woman will not be left alone: “we will hear you. we will stand beside you.” there is no shame in asking for help when facing abuse. the shame lies in inflicting it or turning a blind eye. solidarity begins with hearing a woman’s voice, protecting her safety, and respecting her decisions."},{"title":"The flower","stem":"flower","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#flower","hay":"the flower love and marriage water a flower too much, and it rots. too little, and it withers. give it the water it needs, and it grows and blooms. in love and in raising children, care should respond to the needs of the person receiving it. neither smothering nor neglect helps them flourish; thoughtful care gives them room to grow."},{"title":"Take care of those you love","stem":"five-more-minutes","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#five-more-minutes","hay":"take care of those you love love and marriage on a sunny day in the park, a woman pointed out a child on the swings to the man sitting beside her. “the boy in the red shirt is my son.” the man smiled. “and the one in blue is my son, jamil.” a little later, he checked his watch and called out: “jamil, time to go home.” “five more minutes, dad. please!” his father agreed. after a while, he called again. jamil asked for another five minutes, and his father smiled and nodded. “you’re a very patient father,” the woman said admiringly. a sad smile crossed his face. “it’s more than patience,” he said. “last year, my older son, kamal, was killed by a drunk driver on a road near here. i never spent enough time with him. i always thought we had plenty of time ahead of us. now i’d give anything for just five more minutes with him. i’ve promised myself i won’t make the same mistake with jamil.” he fell silent for a moment and watched his son. “he thinks he’s getting five more minutes to play. but those five minutes are a gift to me, too—a chance to watch him, hear him laugh, and be here with him.” make time for the people you love today. five ordinary minutes together may be one of life’s most precious gifts."},{"title":"An eraser for marriage","stem":"eraser-in-marriage","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#eraser-in-marriage","hay":"an eraser for marriage love and marriage one day, a father asked his newly married son to bring him a sheet of white paper, a pencil and an eraser. “what for, dad?” his son asked. “bring them, and you’ll see.” the son brought the paper and pencil but could not find an eraser. “go and buy one, then,” his father said. puzzled, the son did as he was asked and sat down beside him. “write something,” his father said. “what should i write?” “whatever comes to mind.” the son wrote a sentence. “now erase it.” he rubbed it out. “write another sentence.” “dad, what are you trying to do?” “be patient. write something else.” the son wrote, and his father asked him to erase it again. after they had done this several times, his father asked: “is any of your writing still on the paper?” “no, dad. i’ve erased it all. but what does this mean?” his father rested a hand on his shoulder. “son, you need an eraser like this in marriage too. you must learn to forgive your wife’s small mistakes instead of holding on to every hurt. she needs one too, so she can show the same understanding towards you. otherwise, the pages of your married life will soon be filled with grievances.” then he added: “if you want love and respect, you must both give them freely. when you make a mistake, remember to apologize, put it right and forgive each other. you must each have an eraser.” love and respect in marriage depend not only on forgiving mistakes, but also on recognizing and correcting your own. mutual understanding keeps small hurts from growing into lasting resentment."},{"title":"Honesty (Lunaria annua)","stem":"mother-of-pearl-flower","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#mother-of-pearl-flower","hay":"honesty (lunaria annua) love and marriage a court was hearing the divorce case of a couple in their eighties. everyone in the courtroom was saddened that two people who had shared fifty years of marriage now wanted to part. the judge looked at the woman, whose eyes were red from crying, and gently asked: “tell me, why do you want a divorce?” she took a deep breath. “this man has worn me out. i’ve felt alone for fifty years…” she fell silent. only the click of a camera broke the stillness. the journalists following the case waited for her to continue. “i have an honesty plant (lunaria annua) that i cherish,” she said, wiping her eyes. “these flowers remind me of the first flowers he brought me. i have always cared for them tenderly. we never had children. i cared for that plant as though it were my child. “after a while, it began to wilt. one day, my husband told me that if i got up at night to water it, it would recover. so i promised i would. for years, i’ve been getting up at night to look after it. not once did he say, ‘you sleep. i’ll take care of it tonight.’” her voice trembled. “the other night, i was exhausted and didn’t wake up. he didn’t wake me either. in the morning, i found that the plant hadn’t been watered. it may seem a small thing to you. but to me, it felt as though nothing i had cherished in fifty years had ever mattered to him.” the judge turned to her husband. “is there anything you would like to say?” the old man rose, leaning on his walking stick. “i worked as a gardener during my military service. that was when i met fatima. i used to make bouquets for her from the loveliest flowers.” he glanced at his wife, then continued: “we were newly married when she began having neck pain. i took her to a doctor. i remember the doctor telling her not to stay still for too long, but to get up and walk around occasionally. fatima wouldn’t take care of herself, and i worried about her. “then the plant began to wilt. i told her it would recover if she got up at night to water it. i hoped that, if nothing else, the plant would persuade her to get up and move a little. she gave me her word.” his voice faltered. “every night, i woke her and watched how tenderly she cared for it. she was protecting her plant, and i thought i was protecting her. once she had gone back to bed, i would empty the excess water from the saucer beneath the pot so the roots wouldn’t rot.” he paused. “the other night, i didn’t wake up either. we’ve grown old… when i saw how hurt she was in the morning, i didn’t know what to say. i had kept silent for fifty years, and i stayed silent again.” no one spoke. even the journalists had tears in their eyes. after so many years under the same roof, two people were hearing, for the first time, what lay behind each other’s silence. quiet care may come from love, yet remain unseen when it is never spoken of. love means caring, but also listening, sharing our feelings, and trying to understand one another."},{"title":"The red dress","stem":"red-dress","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#red-dress","hay":"the red dress love and marriage the red dress hung in my mother’s wardrobe. among the faded clothes she had worn for years, it seemed to glow. i had come to visit her. the moment i saw her, i knew she was nearing the end of her life. looking at the dress, i said: “mom, what a beautiful dress! i’ve never seen you wear it.” “i never have, sweetheart,” she answered softly. “come and sit beside me. there’s something i want to tell you before i go.” i sat on the edge of her bed. she drew a deep breath. “i don’t have much time left. i always thought i was setting you a good example. now i realize that some of the lessons i taught you were wrong.” “what do you mean, mom?” “listen, darling. all my life, i believed a good woman should put herself last. everyone else’s wishes had to come first. mine were buried beneath theirs. i thought my turn would come one day. but that day never came. my life was spent making sacrifices for your father, your brothers and sisters, and you.” “you did everything a mother could do for us.” “i don’t regret loving you or caring for you. but i should have cared for myself too. i never learned to ask for anything for myself. your father is sitting in the next room now, staring angrily at the walls. when the doctor told him i was dying, he came in and said, ‘you can’t die! do you hear me? what am i supposed to do without you?’” she paused to catch her breath, then continued: “i worked for you seven days a week. i was always the first awake and the last to bed. i took the burnt toast and the smallest portions. every penny i saved went on your clothes and books, sometimes even when you didn’t need them. i never bought myself something beautiful simply because i liked it.” her eyes turned towards the red dress. “last year, for once, i bought that dress for myself. i had a little money put aside to repair the washing machine. when i brought the dress home, your father said, ‘where are you going to wear that? to the opera?’” her voice trembled. “and i thought he was right. as though i needed a special reason to wear something beautiful. apart from trying it on in the shop, i never wore it.” she fell silent for a moment. “i used to think that if i forgot myself in this world, i would find happiness in the next. i think differently now. i want to believe that allah wants us to love the life he has given us and make room for our dreams while we are still alive. if a miracle happened and i could get out of this bed, i would live differently.” then she took my hand. “don’t forget yourself the way i did, darling. promise me.” i promised. those were her last words to me. a little later, she took her final breath. caring for the people you love does not mean giving up on yourself. make room for their dreams, but keep your own alive too. your life, your joy, and your needs matter as well."},{"title":"True love","stem":"true-love","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#true-love","hay":"true love love and marriage once upon a time, all the feelings lived on an island. one day, word spread that the island was sinking. everyone hurried to prepare their boats and leave. only love lingered. as though trusting that a miracle would save the island, love waited until the very last moment. but when water surrounded everything, there was no choice left but to ask for help. just then, wealth passed by in a large boat. “wealth, will you take me with you?” love called. “i can’t. my boat is full of gold and silver. there’s no room for you.” love turned to pride, who was sailing away in a beautiful sailboat. “pride, please help me!” “i can’t take you aboard. you’re soaking wet. you’ll spoil my boat.” sorrow was passing nearby. “sorrow, may i come with you?” “oh, love, i’m so sad… i need to be alone.” happiness was not far away either, but was so absorbed in joy that love’s cries went unheard. suddenly, a voice called out: “come, love. i’ll take you!” love was so relieved that asking the rescuer’s name never crossed their mind. upon reaching the shore, the rescuer dropped love off and continued on. only then did love realize how much was owed to this stranger. meeting a wise elder, love told the story and asked: “who was it that saved me?” “time,” the elder replied. “time? why was time the one to help me?” the elder smiled. “because only time knows the true value of love.” the worth of true love reveals itself over time. love is sustained not merely by beautiful words, but by years of care, faithfulness, and standing by each other through hardship."},{"title":"What women endure","stem":"what-women-endure","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#what-women-endure","hay":"what women endure love and marriage what women have endured in this world… even in fairy tales, they were cast as witches or cruel stepmothers. what wasn’t said about them… they were not allowed to be themselves. yet they deserved to be seen as whole human beings, with dreams, thoughts and love of their own. woman… “long hair, little sense,” they said. “your place is in the kitchen. stay out of matters like these,” they said. oh, woman, what you have endured in this world… too few recognized your worth. too few saw your love, your heart, your kindness. when you stood up for your rights, you were difficult. when you stayed silent, you were naïve. when you showed your love, some imagined that they owned you. those you lifted to the skies with your love sometimes forgot even to look down at you. they did not ask, “how are you?” they barely noticed whether you were there. they forgot that you, too, had a soul. they forgot you were human. they hurt you, crushed you, treated you as though you did not exist… yet you wanted to make life more beautiful, to offer support, to change something for the better. the heart that gave so much love was left longing to receive it. they spoke to you of heaven and made your life a hell. they looked into your eyes and denied the pain they saw there. they did not hear the cry rising within you. most of all, you loved being a mother. you loved looking into your child’s eyes with tenderness. there were moments when the only love in the world you could truly trust was the love in your child’s arms. valuing women means more than praising their sacrifices. it means hearing their voices, recognizing their work and respecting their choices and dignity. love and peace grow where everyone is valued equally."},{"title":"What is loyalty?","stem":"what-is-loyalty","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#what-is-loyalty","hay":"what is loyalty? love and marriage it is remembering, not turning away when times are hard, not closing your door to a friend… it is caring when a friend is crying, feeling their pain and sharing their sorrow… loyalty is more than a word. it means being there with a heart that understands. a balm for the wounded, water for the thirsty. loyalty is understanding— not abandoning a rose because it has thorns. loyalty means standing by those you love through difficult times and not turning away because of their imperfections. its worth is shown in the care you give, not merely in the promises you make."},{"title":"Can a broken heart love again?","stem":"can-a-broken-heart-love-again","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#can-a-broken-heart-love-again","hay":"can a broken heart love again? love and marriage one day, a man asked a dervish: “can a broken heart love again?” “of course it can,” the dervish replied. the man hesitated. “but have you ever drunk water from a broken cup?” the dervish smiled. “have you ever given up drinking water because a cup broke?” a broken heart has not lost its capacity to love. give yourself time to heal, but do not let one painful experience close your heart to love forever."},{"title":"Yellow and red flowers","stem":"yellow-and-red-flowers","category":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","href":"categories/sevgi-ve-evlilik.html#yellow-and-red-flowers","hay":"yellow and red flowers love and marriage it is said that, in the past, flowers placed in a window sometimes carried a quiet message. yellow flowers meant, “someone in this house is ill; please keep your voices down as you pass.” red flowers meant, “a young woman lives here; please mind your language.” a gentle gesture can sometimes say more than many words. courtesy means not only expressing ourselves thoughtfully, but also noticing another person’s unspoken request."},{"title":"A compliment","stem":"compliment","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#compliment","hay":"a compliment friendship and human relations maria lived with her mother in a small apartment and worked as a secretary at a large company. her days all seemed much the same. at work, she often felt invisible: she arrived, completed her tasks, and quietly went home. one morning, she noticed a newly opened hat shop on her way to work. a hat in the window caught her eye, and she stepped inside. a little girl was there with her mother, while another customer was choosing a hat. maria tried on several. as she put on the last one, the girl called out excitedly: “mom, look! doesn’t that lady look beautiful in that hat?” her mother turned and smiled. “it really does suit you.” the other customer nodded. “as though it were made for you!” maria looked in the mirror. it had been a long time since she had looked at herself so closely—or so kindly. she smiled and stood a little straighter. she decided to buy the hat, went to the counter, paid, and left the shop. it was the same street, yet everything seemed different. the flowers outside the florist’s looked brighter, and the morning air felt fresher. even the city’s noise seemed to have a pleasant rhythm. at the office, she returned the security guard’s greeting with a smile. in the lift, someone pressed the button for her floor, and she thanked them warmly. she spoke more easily with her colleagues and joined their conversations with interest. “you’re glowing today!” one of them said. at lunchtime, her manager invited her out to discuss a new project. this time, maria shared her ideas without holding back. she was still smiling when she returned home that evening. thinking over the day, she marveled: “who would have thought a hat could make such a difference?” her mother opened the door and beamed. “maria, you look lovely! your eyes are shining the way they did when you were little.” “it’s all thanks to my new hat, mom! i’ve had such a wonderful day.” her mother looked puzzled. “what hat?” maria reached up. there was no hat. for a moment, she was bewildered. had she left it in the taxi? at the office? then she retraced the moments in the shop: the mirror, the kind words, the counter… she had taken the hat off and put it down while reaching for her purse. and there she had left it. maria smiled. it had not been the hat that made the difference. a few sincere words had helped her see herself more kindly and let her happiness show. the world was still the same. but maria no longer felt invisible in it. a sincere compliment can remind someone of a beauty they struggle to see in themselves. do not hold back a kind word: sometimes a single sentence can give someone’s confidence a much-needed lift."},{"title":"A glass of milk","stem":"glass-of-milk","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#glass-of-milk","hay":"a glass of milk friendship and human relations a well-known story associated with dr. howard kelly goes like this: a young man was selling small household items door to door to pay for his education. one day, hungry and out of money, he decided to ask for something to eat at the next house. a young woman opened the door. embarrassed, he lost his courage. “could i please have a glass of water?” she noticed his tired face and sensed that he was hungry. a moment later, she returned with a large glass of milk. he drank it slowly, then asked: “how much do i owe you?” “nothing,” she said with a smile. “my mother taught us to give without expecting anything in return.” “then thank you, from the bottom of my heart.” as he walked away, it was not only his hunger that had eased. he felt less alone, and his faith in allah and in human kindness had been renewed. years passed. the woman became seriously ill, and her local doctors referred her to a hospital in a larger city. dr. howard kelly was asked to consult on her treatment. when he heard where she came from, a distant memory stirred. he went to her room and recognized her immediately. she was the woman who had given him the milk. dr. kelly took a close interest in her care. after a long course of treatment, she recovered. on the day she was to leave, he asked to see the bill before it was delivered. he wrote a few words on it and had it sent to her room. she was afraid to open the envelope. how many years would it take her to pay? at last, she unfolded the bill. her eyes filled with tears as she read the note: “paid in full with a glass of milk.” beneath it was the signature: dr. howard kelly. give because someone needs your kindness, not because you expect it to return. a small act of care can leave a lasting impression and inspire another kindness years later."},{"title":"A handful of roasted chickpeas","stem":"handful-of-roasted-chickpeas","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#handful-of-roasted-chickpeas","hay":"a handful of roasted chickpeas friendship and human relations a little boy went into a nut shop with his mother. as soon as he spotted the roasted chickpeas, he began pleading: “mom, can i have some? please!” when the shopkeeper realized that the boy’s mother wasn’t going to buy any, he decided to treat the boy. “come on, help yourself to a handful. they’re on me.” but the boy kept his hands by his sides. “i’d rather you gave them to me.” the shopkeeper scooped up a generous handful and handed them over. then he asked: “why didn’t you take some yourself? why did you want me to do it?” the boy grinned. “because your hands are bigger than mine!” the shopkeeper burst out laughing. “you clever little fellow!” sometimes noticing a small difference can work to your advantage. being quick-witted means spotting what others overlook."},{"title":"A pair of boots","stem":"pair-of-boots","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#pair-of-boots","hay":"a pair of boots friendship and human relations one day, a teacher asked her pupils to write a story on any subject they liked. she promised a pair of boots to the writer of the best story. the children finished their stories and handed them in. the teacher read them one by one and enjoyed them all. unable to choose a favorite, she decided to draw lots. she asked each pupil to write their own name on a slip of paper, fold it, and place it in the box on her desk. she mixed the slips, drew one out, unfolded it, and read aloud: “amina!” the classroom burst into applause. amina hugged the boots to her chest, unable to hold back her tears of joy. that evening, the teacher’s eyes filled with tears as she told her husband what had happened. puzzled, he asked: “what a lovely way to settle it! but why are you crying?” she replied: “afterwards, i opened all the other slips. none of the other children had written their own name. every single one had written amina’s—the girl whose family had the least. they all wanted her to win the boots.” finding joy in someone else’s happiness is a victory too. sometimes true compassion means quietly giving up your own chance for someone who needs it more."},{"title":"Being lonely","stem":"being-lonely","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#being-lonely","hay":"being lonely friendship and human relations i went to visit someone i knew. when i entered the room, i found him sitting silently with his head bowed. he looked sad. “are you unhappy?” i asked. he raised his head and looked into my eyes. “no. i’m lonely.” sometimes loneliness lies behind a sorrowful expression. noticing someone’s silence, listening, and being there for them can be a profound act of care."},{"title":"Discussion and conflict","stem":"discussion-and-conflict","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#discussion-and-conflict","hay":"discussion and conflict friendship and human relations as they did every day, javid bey and his team gathered in the meeting room to discuss the state of their project. each person briefly described the previous day’s progress and any difficulties they had encountered. it was kamal’s turn. like the others, he reported on the work he had done, but — as if hoping for help from his colleagues — he spoke in more detail about the problem he faced. as soon as he finished, nadir accused him in front of everyone: “you are to blame; because of you our work has stopped and we can do nothing.” the room froze. kamal was deeply embarrassed and did not know what to do. before the exchange could escalate, javid bey, the project manager, stepped in. “let’s stop here for today and return to this later,” he said. the team dispersed. javid bey knew what to do: speak to nadir privately and in a friendly way. he invited him to the company café for a cup of tea. after a little small talk, he said calmly, “nadir, the way you treated kamal today was wrong. you accused him without understanding the situation, and you did it in front of everyone. put yourself in his place. he was hoping for our help and was accused instead. i’m sure he hoped we would think the problem through together and find a solution.” nadir lowered his head in shame. javid bey’s words had taught him a lesson: he understood that he had acted wrongly. later, whenever nadir remembered the conversation, he thanked javid bey. he was also grateful that their conversation had remained private. conflict focuses on who is right; discussion focuses on what is right. if you want the truth, discuss — do not quarrel. bakhtiyar sirajov"},{"title":"Everyone is equal here","stem":"everyone-is-equal-here","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#everyone-is-equal-here","hay":"everyone is equal here friendship and human relations the young, newly elected head of the local council decided to explore the neighborhood on foot. after walking for a while, he stopped at a teahouse on a side street and ordered some tea. a boy of twelve or thirteen approached him. “would you like your shoes polished, sir?” his shoes were already clean, but he did not want to turn the boy away. “all right. do a good job, and i’ll pay you double.” the boy answered calmly: “i do a good job for everyone.” the young man looked surprised. “what do you mean?” “our teacher says we should do our work honestly for everyone. it’s not right to do it carefully for one person and carelessly for another.” taking out his brush, he added: “i buy medicine for my mother with this money. i want to earn it honestly.” the young man fell silent. the boy’s simple words held an important lesson for his own work: good service should not depend on extra money or someone’s status. when the boy finished, the young man paid him for his work and gave him some additional money to help. as they talked, he learned that the boy was growing up without his father. the boy attended school and worked afterward to help his mother. the young man gave him his contact number and said he would visit the family to see how he could support them. later, he also met the boy’s teacher and thanked him for teaching his pupils the value of honesty. to remember their conversation, he had a sentence written on the side of his desk nameplate that faced him: “everyone matters equally here.” he tried to keep those words in mind whenever he made a decision: whoever stood before him deserved the same respect and fair treatment. you should not need a wealthy or influential customer to do your work conscientiously. honest service is measured by respect for each person’s rights, not by the promise of extra money."},{"title":"Forgiveness","stem":"forgiveness","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#forgiveness","hay":"forgiveness friendship and human relations forgiving someone does not oblige you to love them, speak to them, or continue the relationship. nor does it mean accepting that they were innocent or right. forgiveness means no longer allowing hurt, resentment, and hatred to rule your life. it means gradually easing the weight of past pain. give yourself time. when you are ready to forgive, let go of that burden without forgetting what you have learned. as you make room for peace, protect your boundaries too. forgiveness does not excuse a wrong; it means choosing not to carry its burden forever. you can forgive while recognizing that trusting again and continuing the relationship are separate choices."},{"title":"Meeting someone and knowing them","stem":"beyond-first-impressions","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#beyond-first-impressions","hay":"meeting someone and knowing them friendship and human relations of some people, all we can say is: meeting them brought us joy;\nknowing them brought disappointment. a first impression never tells the whole story. an acquaintance begins with a meeting, but character reveals itself over time through actions."},{"title":"Go, but come again","stem":"go-but-come-again","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#go-but-come-again","hay":"go, but come again friendship and human relations a story about ottoman hospitality tells of hosts placing departing guests’ shoes by the door with the toes pointing towards the house—a gesture reserved for those they hoped to see again. it was a wordless invitation: “you may be leaving, but come back soon. our door is open to you.” the guests carried away more than pleasant memories of their visit. they left with the joy of knowing they would be warmly welcomed again. warmth does not always need grand words. a small gesture can give someone a precious sense of belonging: “there is a place for me here. they hope i’ll return.”"},{"title":"Gossip","stem":"gossip","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#gossip","hay":"gossip friendship and human relations a sharp-tongued man had been spreading rumors about someone in his village. when the matter came to a head, he was called before the village elder. “it was only a joke!” he protested. “people misunderstood me. how is that my fault?” but the man whose reputation he had damaged would not let the matter rest. “you have dragged my good name through the mud. i want justice.” unable to deny what he had said, the gossip finally backed down. “all right. i take it all back. please forgive me.” the elder listened carefully. sensing that the man still did not understand the harm he had caused, he said: “bring a feather pillow to the marketplace. cut it open and scatter the feathers on the wind. then gather every last one and bring them to me. after that, we can talk about forgiveness.” the man reluctantly agreed. what a strange thing to ask, he thought as he carried the pillow to the square, cut it open, and shook the feathers into the air. the wind scattered them in every direction. he ran back and forth, trying to catch them, but however hard he tried, he managed to collect only a handful. he returned to the elder with his head bowed. “i couldn’t gather the rest. the wind carried them everywhere.” “do you understand now how difficult it is to take back your words?” the elder asked. the man looked at the feathers in his hand and sighed. “yes. my words have spread just like these feathers. saying ‘i take it back’ cannot bring them home.” gossip is like feathers scattered on the wind: easy to spread, hard to gather up again. an apology matters, but it cannot always undo the harm. think before you speak."},{"title":"How to respond to insults","stem":"unwelcome-gifts","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#unwelcome-gifts","hay":"how to respond to insults friendship and human relations (a parable about a samurai) an elderly, wise samurai taught his students the ways of wisdom and the art of combat. one day, a warrior known for his rudeness and cruelty entered during a lesson. he had a favorite tactic: he would insult his opponents until they lost their temper, then exploit the mistakes they made in anger to defeat them. this time, he used the same tactic to provoke the samurai into a fight. he hurled insults at him, but the samurai paid no attention and continued teaching. the warrior repeated his insults several times. when he still received no response, he left in frustration. the students, who had watched closely, asked: “master, why did you put up with those insults? why didn’t you challenge him to a fight?” the samurai answered with a question: “if someone brings you a gift and you do not accept it, who keeps it?” “the person who brought it,” the students replied. “think of envy, hatred, and insults in the same way. if you do not accept these ‘gifts,’ they remain with the person who brought them.” do not accept someone else’s insult as the truth about you. meet hostility with composure rather than hostility of your own, so that another person’s anger does not dictate your behavior."},{"title":"A test of humanity","stem":"humanity","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#humanity","hay":"a test of humanity friendship and human relations kamal had studied and worked hard for years. at last, the day of the examination that could shape his future had arrived. his father, kazim, was a demanding man. he followed his son’s studies closely and was certain he would succeed. kamal found the examination room and took his seat. he felt nervous when the papers were handed out, but relaxed as he read the questions. he knew the answers and wrote with confidence. with half an hour left, he looked up and glanced through the window. suddenly, he sprang from his chair and ran toward the door. “come back! if you leave now, your exam will be invalidated! you’ll lose a whole year!” the proctor called. kamal did not stop. he raced down the stairs and out through the back of the building. when he returned home several hours later, his father already knew what had happened. he scolded his son, then lost control of his anger and slapped him. “why did you leave? why did you abandon the exam?” kamal remained silent. he did not believe his father would listen to him then. he feared that anything he said would only make him angrier. throughout the following year, kazim kept bringing up the incident. each time, it hurt kamal, but he did not argue. he studied again and, the next year, earned a place at medical school. years passed. kamal became a skilled, compassionate doctor. although he had grown up in the city, he chose to work at a hospital in a remote village. some time later, his family came to visit. the examination was no longer mentioned. one snowy, bitterly cold evening, the telephone rang. a woman in a nearby village was ill, and someone was asking for help. kamal picked up his medical bag. his father did not want him walking the mountain roads alone in such weather. “i’m coming with you.” they walked through the blizzard for almost an hour before finding the house. an elderly man opened the door. “it’s my wife, doctor. please come in.” kamal examined the woman, gave her initial treatment, and explained the care she would need. she looked at him gratefully. “you came to help us in this terrible cold, my son,” she said in a trembling voice. “what would the world be like without people like you? you remind me of a young man…” she paused to catch her breath. “years ago, i went to the city. i was pregnant. suddenly, i became unwell in the street and was seized by terrible pains. there was nobody nearby to ask for help. i learned later that an examination was taking place in the building beside me. “before long, a young man came running. he went to such trouble to get me to the hospital in that heat. it took us half an hour. the doctor said that any further delay could have put my life in danger. my son was born that very day.” she fell silent for a moment, then smiled. “i asked the young man his name. he said it was kamal. he had seen me through the window during his exam and, realizing i was alone, had run out to help. “‘i can start my studies a year later,’ he told me. ‘i couldn’t leave you there.’” kazim looked up at his son. kamal was listening without a word. “i named my son kamal,” the woman continued. “i brought him up hearing about that young man’s kindness. i wanted him to become just as compassionate. he’s a teacher now. alongside his lessons, he tries to teach his pupils to care about others.” her eyes filled with tears. “if only i could see that young man again. i would tell him that the year he lost gave two people a future. i would ask him to forgive me for what helping me had cost him. you can learn a profession, my son. what matters is never losing your humanity. he did not leave me alone in that street.” kamal gently took her hand. “there is nothing to forgive, mother. be at peace,” he said softly. kazim lowered his eyes. inside the house, he tried to hide his tears. once outside, he could no longer hold them back. he wept as he walked beside his son along the snowy road. “kamal, my son…” he could say no more. that night, in a stranger’s home, he had finally heard the answer he had refused to listen to years before. our worth lies not only in our profession, but in the choices we make when someone needs us. what looks like a loss may bring lasting good to many lives. listen before you judge."},{"title":"I wish for a friend","stem":"i-wish-for-a-friend","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#i-wish-for-a-friend","hay":"i wish for a friend friendship and human relations i ask for neither wealth nor land,\nonly a faithful friend.\nwhat property has ever prayed for the soul of the person who owned it?\ni wish for a friend who will pray for mine when i am gone. our truest wealth lies in faithful friends. a friend who stands beside us in life and remembers us in prayer after we are gone is worth more than any possession."},{"title":"If the road feels easy, thank your companion","stem":"sharing-the-burden","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#sharing-the-burden","hay":"if the road feels easy, thank your companion friendship and human relations my mother used to tell me: “i was very young when i married your father. i could barely cook, and i wasn’t much good at sewing either. “one day, my mother-in-law—your grandmother—was getting ready to visit a neighbor. she had washed and dried the wool filling for a quilt. she spread the quilt out in front of me and said: “‘have this stitched by the time i get back.’ “i picked up the needle and began. but however hard i tried, i struggled to push it through the thick quilt. i kept pricking my fingers. in the end, i started crying. my hand hurt, and i had no idea what i would say to her if i couldn’t finish. “just then, your father came home for his lunch break. when he saw me crying, he sat beside me and took the needle from my hand. he finished stitching the quilt himself, ate his lunch, and went back to work. “he never said a word to his mother. when she returned and saw the finished quilt, she was very pleased.” whenever i remember my mother’s story, i think of the saying: “if the road does not tire you, it is thanks to your companion.” life’s journey is easier with someone who shares your burdens instead of judging you when you struggle. sometimes love is shown not through grand declarations, but through a quietly offered helping hand."},{"title":"Interfering in other people’s lives","stem":"respecting-boundaries","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#respecting-boundaries","hay":"interfering in other people’s lives friendship and human relations may allah never leave us with so little to do that we start meddling in other people’s lives! caring for someone does not mean making their decisions for them. unwanted advice can become a burden; genuine help begins with respect for a person’s choices and boundaries."},{"title":"Know your friend","stem":"know-your-friend","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#know-your-friend","hay":"know your friend friendship and human relations if you want to know your friend, watch how they behave in hardship, under pressure, and during a disagreement. and when they must choose between your friendship and their own interests… the strength of a friendship shows in difficult times and disagreements as well as in happy moments. a true friend considers their own interests without forgetting what is fair to you or the trust you share."},{"title":"Compassion","stem":"compassion","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#compassion","hay":"compassion friendship and human relations i was walking home from work on a bitter winter night when i noticed a man struggling to stay on his feet. he swayed, clinging to a post by the roadside to keep from falling. he looked about thirty and was neatly dressed. some passers-by assumed he was drunk and made loud remarks about drinking. others laughed at him. but no one stopped to ask what was wrong. i approached him. “are you all right? do you need help?” his lips barely moved. “i’m ill…” i put an arm around his waist to steady him, then slowly helped him to a nearby taxi stand. snow had begun to fall again. the street was emptying, and the cold was seeping into our bones. i was almost ready to give up hope of finding a taxi when one pulled up beside us. i explained the situation to the driver. together, we helped the man into the back seat and headed for the hospital. the medical staff attended to him immediately. we waited. the driver stayed too. after a while, the doctor on duty told us: “it’s a good thing you didn’t leave him out there. his life was at risk in that cold.” as the man recovered, he looked at us and managed a faint smile. he was still too weak to speak, but his eyes said a great deal. when the doctors said he could go home, we helped him back into the same taxi. as we approached his house, we saw his wife and son outside. knowing that he had diabetes, his wife had grown anxious when he failed to return. she and their son had come out to wait for him. the moment the taxi stopped, they ran towards us. when the man stepped out, they wrapped their arms around him. for a while, none of them let go. the hours of weariness vanished. seeing their joy brought tears to our eyes too. after we said goodbye, i asked the driver: “how much do i owe you?” he shook his head. “you don’t owe me anything, my friend. i owe you. you let me be part of something good. i don’t think i’ve cried in twenty years… tonight, you gave me that feeling again.” i hugged him. when we parted, the night no longer felt quite so cold. i decided to walk home. who knew? perhaps i would meet someone else along the way who needed help. compassion goes beyond feeling sorry for someone. it begins when we stop, ask, and offer a helping hand. sometimes approaching a person instead of judging them can change a whole family’s world."},{"title":"People who need your help","stem":"request-for-help","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#request-for-help","hay":"people who need your help friendship and human relations do not be too quick to resent people who contact you only when they need something. perhaps they are struggling and asking for help because they trust you. seeing their request in this light may soften your hurt. do not immediately assume the worst of someone who turns to you for help. sometimes their request reflects both need and trust. understanding can ease resentment."},{"title":"Permission or forgiveness?","stem":"permission-or-forgiveness","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#permission-or-forgiveness","hay":"permission or forgiveness? friendship and human relations rear admiral grace hopper helped popularize the saying: “it’s easier to ask forgiveness than it is to get permission.” this is sometimes understood as: act first, then apologize if anyone objects. but there is a difference between encouraging initiative and using the saying to excuse irresponsibility. sometimes, waiting endlessly for approval prevents a good idea from ever being tried. imagine a company where the same information is copied manually into several spreadsheets every week, taking hours. an employee realizes that a simple automated process could make the task much easier. as the proposal passes from one manager to another, a response could take weeks. instead, the employee builds a separate demonstration without altering existing data or disrupting anyone’s work. they show their colleagues the result: a task that once took hours now takes only minutes. when someone objects that the trial was not agreed on in advance, the employee explains: “i built a separate demonstration to see how we could make the work easier. i haven’t changed the existing system. if i should have let you know beforehand, i apologize.” the point is to test a useful idea responsibly, rather than disregard the rules. when the risk is low, mistakes can be corrected, and other people’s rights are respected, a small initiative can demonstrate more than lengthy discussions. initiative becomes valuable when it is paired with responsibility. showing what is possible through small, reversible steps can be more effective than waiting endlessly for approval. but apologizing afterwards does not remove the responsibility to think beforehand."},{"title":"How we treat people","stem":"how-we-treat-people","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#how-we-treat-people","hay":"how we treat people friendship and human relations approach your relationships as you would a fire: stay too far away, and you will freeze.\nget too close, and you will burn. — attributed to saadi shirazi healthy relationships need both closeness and boundaries. leave enough space to remain yourself, but stay close enough to share their warmth."},{"title":"Mockery is unacceptable","stem":"mockery-is-unacceptable","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#mockery-is-unacceptable","hay":"mockery is unacceptable friendship and human relations children need to learn from an early age that no one should be mocked or belittled because of their appearance or because they are different. everyone deserves respect. being different is no reason to be ridiculed. respect for another person’s dignity is learned in childhood through the words and actions of adults."},{"title":"Relationships","stem":"relationships","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#relationships","hay":"relationships friendship and human relations deep within human life lies a truth: our relationships are a mirror of our inner selves. the warmth born at the family hearth, the bonds rooted in kinship, the mutual understanding that grows in friendship—each is among life’s most precious treasures. the respect and kindness shared among colleagues are like an unseen light that brings warmth to our daily work. kindness is not simply a way of behaving; it is an expression of a person’s inner richness. it is a silent bridge that brings hearts closer, warms cold moments, and makes understanding possible without words. if we protect and strengthen this bridge, life will become more meaningful, people will grow closer, and the world will become a slightly better place to live. relationships grow stronger through mutual respect, understanding, and kindness. to maintain our closeness, we must remember to care for one another. etibar siracsoy"},{"title":"Make time for people","stem":"make-time-for-people","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#make-time-for-people","hay":"make time for people friendship and human relations my father needed to transfer some money, so i went to the bank with him. we spent nearly an hour there. eventually, i lost patience. “dad, let me show you how to do your banking online. what do you think?” he looked at me with surprise. “what would that do for me, son?” “you wouldn’t have to come to the bank and wait an hour just to transfer money. you could do your shopping online too. everything would be easier and more convenient.” confident that i could persuade him, i was ready to go on enthusiastically. “so, if i did what you suggest, i wouldn’t need to leave the house?” he asked. “that’s right, dad. you could even have groceries brought to your door. you could order through services like amazon and have your purchases delivered.” his reply left me speechless. “son, in this hour you consider wasted, i saw four friends and talked to bank staff who have known me for years. “you know i often feel lonely. i need to see people and have someone to talk to. i enjoy getting dressed, leaving the house, and coming to the bank. i have the time, and these encounters give me pleasure. “two years ago, when i was ill, the man i always buy fruit from came to see me. he was genuinely upset that i was unwell. he sat beside my bed, asked how i was, and lifted my spirits. “a while ago, when your mother fell during her morning walk, our local grocer came to her aid. he helped her up, put her in his car, and brought her home. he knew us and knew where we lived. “if i did everything without leaving home, how would those connections ever develop? why should i give up seeing people just to have everything delivered to my door? “i don’t simply want to buy things, son. i want to know the person i buy them from, ask how they are, and exchange a few words. i need company and a sense of belonging. “can amazon deliver that to my doorstep too? “technology makes life easier, but it isn’t life itself. spend your time with people, son. let your devices help you do that, not get in the way.” saving time is useful, but an hour spent with people is not necessarily an hour wasted. enjoy the convenience of technology without forgetting the value of a greeting, a conversation, and being there for one another."},{"title":"The friendship of horses","stem":"friendship-of-horses","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#friendship-of-horses","hay":"the friendship of horses friendship and human relations two horses live on a farm near my home. from a distance, they seem much like any other horses. only when you come closer do you notice that one is blind. the owner has continued to care for it, providing a safe, comfortable home. but the horse also has a friend who makes life a little easier. as you approach the farm, you hear the gentle ringing of a bell. it hangs from the other horse’s halter. the blind horse follows the sound, using it to find its companion and make its way along. watch them for a while, and you begin to notice their quiet understanding. the horse with the bell turns now and then to check on its friend. the other walks steadily towards the sound. in the evening, as they return to the stable, the leading horse does not hurry. it walks a little, then stops and waits. when its companion catches up, it moves on again, as though saying: “i’m here. i’m waiting for you.” one offers guidance; the other offers trust. together, they make their way home. sometimes we feel god’s compassion in the care of a friend. at times, we need a voice to guide us; at others, it is our turn to make sure someone is not left behind."},{"title":"The doorbell","stem":"doorbell","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#doorbell","hay":"the doorbell friendship and human relations last winter, i lost my job. to make ends meet, i took temporary work delivering newspapers. one day, i found that the letterbox at one of the houses had been sealed shut, so i knocked on the door. an elderly man answered. “why have you sealed your letterbox?” i asked. “i did it deliberately,” he replied. then, with a faint smile, he added: “may i ask a favor? when you bring the newspaper each day, please knock or ring the bell and hand it to me.” i was puzzled. “of course. but wouldn’t it be easier for both of us if i put it through the letterbox? you wouldn’t have to come to the door.” “it’s no trouble, young man. i’m home every day.” he paused before continuing. “my wife has passed away, and my son lives abroad. i live here alone. who knows how much time i have left?” i noticed tears gathering in his eyes. “but didn’t you subscribe because you wanted to read the newspaper?” i asked. “i don’t read newspapers, my boy... i subscribed to the sound of the doorbell.” then he clasped his hands and said quietly: “do me one more kindness. this is my son’s phone number. if you knock one day and i don’t answer, please call him and let him know.” sometimes it is not the newspaper someone is waiting for, but the person at the door. a greeting and a brief conversation can remind someone living alone that they have not been forgotten."},{"title":"People you should not befriend","stem":"choosing-friends","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#choosing-friends","hay":"people you should not befriend friendship and human relations do not befriend the arrogant. they will not try to understand you, listen to your troubles, or consider your feelings—and you will be hurt. do not befriend those who think they know better than everyone else. they talk too much, listen too little, and belittle you with their words—and you will be hurt. do not befriend the dishonorable. they break their promises, abandon you in difficult times, and fail to value friendship—and you will be hurt. choose friends by how they treat you and what they do, not by sweet words. a true friend listens, respects you, and stands beside you when times are hard."},{"title":"Thoughtfulness","stem":"thoughtfulness","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#thoughtfulness","hay":"thoughtfulness friendship and human relations one of the most precious blessings in a person’s life is to receive attention and care. sometimes even a brief call from a relative, a friend, or an acquaintance, simply to ask how we are, rises like the sun in our hearts. in that moment, we realize that somewhere in this vast world there are people who remember us and value our presence in their lives. everyone needs to be loved, remembered, and valued. a sincere greeting, a heartfelt inquiry, or an unexpected call can sometimes be worth more than the most precious gift. for these small gestures give us a sense of belonging and remind us of our place in the hearts of those around us. a few minutes of attention may brighten someone’s day, awaken hope in their heart, and strengthen their love of life. sometimes words that touch the heart, the care we receive, and the closeness we feel give us fresh strength to go on living. for one of the surest sources of happiness is knowing that we have not been forgotten. asking how someone is and remembering them may seem like small things, yet they can offer great emotional support. let us not withhold our care, so that no one feels forgotten. etibar siracsoy"},{"title":"Welcome home, my dear","stem":"welcome-home-my-dear","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#welcome-home-my-dear","hay":"welcome home, my dear friendship and human relations when my late father, hafız ali, was fighting at gallipoli, my mother was seven months pregnant with me. i never saw him, never knew him. not even a photograph of him remained. those were terrible years: mobilization, occupation, the struggle for independence, poverty and hardship. our childhood passed in want, with the constant worry of finding enough to eat. whenever my mother went out, she would come to me first. “son, i’m going to the market. if your father comes home, let me know at once!” “i’m going to your aunt’s. if your father comes home, let me know at once!” “i’m going next door. if your father comes home, let me know at once!” my mother never stopped waiting for him. i grew up, began earning a living and opened a shop. whenever she went out, she would still stop by, tell me where she was going and add: “if your father comes home, be sure to let me know!” the years passed, and my mother grew old. but her habit never changed. leaning on her walking stick, she would come to me and say: “if your father comes home, come and fetch me!” one day she became seriously ill and took to her bed. in her final moments, she said goodbye to us. “you have cared for me so well. forgive me for any trouble i have caused you.” then she turned slowly towards me. “if your father comes home, tell him, ‘mother waited for you until the end of her life.’” suddenly, she raised her head and looked towards the door. a smile spread across her face. “welcome home, my dear. welcome home!” with those words, she breathed her last. recounted as the memories of cevdet alkalp, an elderly cobbler who lived in balıkesir. the account is attributed to mustafa doğru, a researcher, writer and geography teacher at bursa çınar anadolu high school. in türkiye, march 18 marks the anniversary of the çanakkale naval victory and a day of remembrance for the fallen. on that day in 1915, the allied fleet’s attempt to force its way through the dardanelles towards istanbul was repelled. in remembering this victory, we remember not only those who died on the battlefield, but also the families who waited years for their return. may all those who fell at gallipoli rest in peace. may no mother spend her life waiting for someone who will never return. the pain of war does not end on the battlefield; sometimes it becomes a lifetime of waiting at home. we owe remembrance and respect both to those who gave their lives for their homeland and to those who waited for them."},{"title":"Why shout?","stem":"why-shout","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#why-shout","hay":"why shout? friendship and human relations one day during class, a teacher asked the students: “why do people shout when they argue?” “because they lose their temper,” one student replied. “but the other person is right beside them. why should they need to shout to be heard? can’t they speak calmly even when they’re angry?” the teacher asked. the students offered different answers. after listening, the teacher said: “think of it this way: as people argue, their hearts grow further apart. it is as though they shout to bridge that distance and make their voices reach each other. yet the louder they become, the further apart their hearts drift.” after a brief pause, the teacher continued: “now think of people who love each other. they often speak softly because their hearts are close. as that closeness deepens, even a whisper is enough. sometimes they need no words at all: a glance is enough to understand each other.” then the teacher addressed the class: “when you argue, do not let your hearts drift apart. do not say things that widen the distance between you. one day, that distance may become so great that finding your way back to each other is difficult.” in an argument, try not only to be heard, but also to listen. patience and respect keep hearts close; hurtful words drive them further apart."},{"title":"Your friends","stem":"your-friends","category":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","href":"categories/dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri.html#your-friends","hay":"your friends friendship and human relations a young man was preparing to get married. after a busy week of wedding preparations, he turned to his father. “dad, could you do me a favor? i can’t manage everything on my own. would you call the friends on this list and invite them to my wedding?” “of course, son,” his father replied. on the wedding day, the young man approached his father, visibly upset. “dad, i asked you to call all my friends!” “i did, son. i called every one of them.” “there were fifty names on the list, but i can only see fifteen of them here!” his father answered calmly: “i spoke to all fifty. but instead of inviting them to a wedding, i told them you were facing a difficulty and needed your friends’ help. i asked them to come to this address, today, at this time.” then he rested a hand on his son’s shoulder. “look around you, son. these people came here not to share your celebration, but to stand by you in a difficult moment.” friendship is measured in care and trust, not numbers. treasure those who come to your aid when times are hard; true friendship reveals itself through actions over time."},{"title":"A generous man","stem":"generous-man","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#generous-man","hay":"a generous man morals and character a generous man was asked: “when you help people in need, do you expect their gratitude?” he replied: “i think of myself as a ladle in a cook’s hand. the food passes through it, but the ladle did not provide the meal. i simply share what allah has given me. rather than expect praise, i am grateful for the chance to help.” do not make kindness a debt others must repay. share your blessings humbly and respect the dignity of those you help. the opportunity to give is itself a blessing."},{"title":"A mature person","stem":"mature-person","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#mature-person","hay":"a mature person morals and character there comes a time when you grow to appreciate the sincerity of old friends more deeply. you stop seeking new acquaintances simply to surround yourself with people. you no longer argue with those unwilling to understand, and you step away from relationships that leave you emotionally drained. that is when you realize you are becoming more mature. maturity means valuing genuine relationships, maintaining your boundaries, and protecting your inner peace. knowing whom to keep close is wisdom; so is knowing whom and what to step away from."},{"title":"Question the question","stem":"question-the-question","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#question-the-question","hay":"question the question morals and character a story is told of a berkeley graduate who visited a rabbi in new york and asked for help studying the talmud. the rabbi asked: “do you know aramaic? what about hebrew? were you introduced to the torah as a child?” the young man answered no to every question, then added: “but i’ve defended a dissertation on socratic logic. i want to study the talmud to fill some gaps in my education.” “studying the talmud requires serious preparation,” the rabbi said. “but since you’re so keen, let me ask you a question first. two men climb down a chimney. one has a clean face; the other’s face is covered in soot. which one washes his face?” the young man answered confidently: “the one with the dirty face, of course!” “consider this instead: the clean-faced man sees the soot on his companion’s face, assumes his own face is dirty too, and goes to wash. the dirty-faced man sees a clean face before him and feels no need to wash.” “interesting! may i have another chance?” the rabbi repeated the question: “two men climb down a chimney. one has a clean face, and the other has a dirty face. which one washes?” “this time i know: the one with the clean face!” “but both might wash. the clean-faced man looks at his companion and goes to wash. the other notices that his companion went to wash after looking at him and realizes that his own face must be dirty.” the young man paused. “i hadn’t considered that. ask me again.” the rabbi repeated the question. this time, the young man did not hesitate: “they both wash!” “perhaps neither does. the dirty-faced man sees his companion’s clean face and stays where he is. the clean-faced man notices that his companion isn’t washing and assumes that his own face must be clean as well.” thoroughly confused, the young man asked for one last chance. the rabbi began: “two men climb down a chimney. one has a clean face, and the other—” “neither washes!” the young man interrupted. the rabbi smiled. “again, you rushed to answer. why didn’t you ask how one man’s face stayed clean when they both came down the same chimney? how do we know what they saw or thought? each time, i introduced a new assumption, and you accepted it as part of the question.” he paused, then continued: “sometimes the problem is not that we cannot find an answer, but that we seek one without examining the question. first, we must distinguish what we know from what we merely assume.” not every question has a single, definite answer. clear thinking begins with examining the question and separating facts from assumptions, rather than rushing to a conclusion."},{"title":"Facing a problem","stem":"facing-a-problem","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#facing-a-problem","hay":"facing a problem morals and character is there a problem in your life? if not, why worry? if there is, can you do something about it? if you can, put your energy into finding a solution. if you can’t, what will worrying change? do what you can, and learn to accept what is beyond your control. constant worry does not solve the problem—it only drains your strength."},{"title":"Avoid these questions","stem":"avoid-these-questions","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#avoid-these-questions","hay":"avoid these questions morals and character pause before asking someone who is looking for work, “still haven’t found a job?” think twice before asking an employed person how much they earn, a couple without children whether they have children yet, or someone who is single when they are getting married. and when someone doesn’t know something, don’t embarrass them by saying, “you didn’t even know that?” behind every answer may be a story the person is not ready to share: worry, loss, disappointment, or simply a personal choice they do not have to explain. a question that satisfies our passing curiosity may reopen someone else’s old wound. before you ask, consider whether you want to support the person or merely satisfy your curiosity. tact means respecting other people’s privacy. sometimes care is shown by asking a question; sometimes it is shown by choosing not to."},{"title":"Be clear about your goal","stem":"shared-goal","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#shared-goal","hay":"be clear about your goal morals and character two people were trying to move a heavy chest through a doorway. however hard they pushed, it would not budge. eventually, both stopped, sweating and out of breath. one wiped his forehead and said: “what a heavy chest! all that effort, and we still couldn’t get it inside.” the other looked at him in surprise. “inside? weren’t we trying to get it out?” before working together, make sure you are working toward the same goal. otherwise, you may get in each other’s way while thinking you are helping."},{"title":"Keeping your cool","stem":"keeping-your-cool","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#keeping-your-cool","hay":"keeping your cool morals and character when you find yourself in a difficult situation, pause and think rather than panic. calmly considering what is happening can help you make a sound decision and find a way to turn things around. composure may not remove a difficulty, but it makes the way forward easier to see. take a moment to think calmly before you act."},{"title":"Being human","stem":"being-human","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#being-human","hay":"being human morals and character “becoming a scholar is easy; becoming a decent person is hard,” the saying goes. but what does it mean to be a decent person? it means living by your conscience: acting honestly and fairly, preserving your own dignity without diminishing anyone else’s. it means being considerate, courteous, and humble. respecting people for their humanity, rather than their wealth or position. recognizing your own shortcomings, not just those of others. it means thinking about the consequences of your actions, admitting your mistakes, and taking responsibility for what you do. keeping your composure in an argument and not losing heart when difficulties arise. it means keeping your word even when it is inconvenient. refusing to measure a person’s worth by money or status, to covet what belongs to others, or to take what is rightfully theirs. it means not lying, spreading slander, or betraying someone for personal gain. standing up for those treated unjustly, even when staying silent would be easier. it means not turning people against one another or taking pleasure in someone else’s suffering. knowing how to love—and showing that love through care and action, not words alone. our knowledge shows what we have learned; our actions show who we have become. our humanity is revealed in our everyday choices: to be honest, to respect others’ dignity, and to care when someone is hurting."},{"title":"Believe in yourself","stem":"believe-in-yourself","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#believe-in-yourself","hay":"believe in yourself morals and character know your worth! do not shy away from hard work! build your strength and develop your abilities! never lose hope! listen to your conscience! stay determined! keep your intentions pure! preserve your dignity! hold firmly to your faith! believing in yourself does not mean thinking you can do everything. it means trusting your ability to learn and grow. let hope, determination, and dignity sustain you along the way."},{"title":"Do not deepen another’s pain","stem":"do-not-deepen-anothers-pain","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#do-not-deepen-anothers-pain","hay":"do not deepen another’s pain morals and character do not boast about your wealth to someone struggling financially, your health to someone who is ill, or your carefree life to someone weighed down by sorrow. do not flaunt your freedom before someone deprived of theirs, or your strength before someone feeling powerless. speak sensitively about your children with someone longing for a child, and about your parents with someone grieving theirs. share your joy, but remain mindful of another person’s pain. sometimes listening and being present matter more than speaking. do not turn your blessings into boasts that deepen someone else’s hurt. choose your words with understanding: seek to recognize their feelings rather than remind them of what they lack."},{"title":"Think before speaking","stem":"think-before-speaking","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#think-before-speaking","hay":"think before speaking morals and character before you speak, ask yourself: will your words offer comfort, help with a difficulty, or bring clarity where it is needed? speaking kindly does not mean hiding the truth. even a painful truth can be told without humiliating someone. if your words serve only to wound, silence is the better choice. the value of words lies in the good they do, not merely in how pleasant they sound. speak the truth with respect, and never speak simply to hurt."},{"title":"If it did not work, start again","stem":"try-again","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#try-again","hay":"if it did not work, start again morals and character days come and go. some end in joy, others in weariness or sadness. sometimes our plans remain unfinished, and however hard we try, we do not get the result we hoped for. at such moments, do not turn “i didn’t finish” into self-blame, or “i couldn’t do it” into a reason to give up. something that did not work out today may still work out another day. rest a little and gather your strength. tomorrow may bring another chance to try again, take a different approach, or take even a small step forward. an unfinished task does not mean you are incapable. give yourself time: moving forward sometimes means starting again, and sometimes it means resting before you continue."},{"title":"Education or character?","stem":"education-or-character","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#education-or-character","hay":"education or character? morals and character one day, a king asked his vizier: “which matters more in a person: education or character?” “character, your majesty,” the vizier replied. the king disagreed. he ordered the finest animal trainer in the kingdom to be brought to the palace. “how long would it take you to teach a cat to carry a tray and serve guests?” he asked. “six months, your majesty.” six months later, the trainer returned with the cat. the guests assembled, and the gathering began. holding a tray, the cat calmly approached each guest. everyone watched in amazement. the king turned to his vizier with a smile. “now tell me: education or character?” before answering, the vizier released a little mouse he had brought with him. the moment the cat saw it, it dropped the tray and raced after it. “character, your majesty,” the vizier said quietly. the king fell silent. the cat’s hunting instinct had overridden its training. but this incident could not serve as a measure of human character: people can reflect on their desires, change their behavior, and take responsibility for their choices. knowing how to behave well is not the same as putting that knowledge into practice. what matters is being able to apply what we have learned even in difficult moments. education and character should complement each other, rather than be set against each other."},{"title":"The choice is in our hands","stem":"in-your-hands","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#in-your-hands","hay":"the choice is in our hands morals and character one day, a young man decided to outwit a wise monk. he caught a little bird, concealed it between his palms, and held his hands behind his back. “tell me, wise man,” he asked, “is the bird in my hands alive or dead?” he thought to himself: “if he says it’s alive, i’ll squeeze it to death. if he says it’s dead, i’ll open my hands and let it fly away. either way, i’ll prove him wrong.” the monk looked at him and answered calmly: “the answer, my son, is in your hands.” the young man hesitated and lowered his head. the monk continued: “you want to prove me wrong, but this bird’s fate depends on your choice. you can hurt it, or you can set it free. wisdom is shown not by trapping someone with a clever question, but by how you use the power you have.” the young man brought his hands forward and opened them. the bird spread its wings and flew away. this time, he did not try to prove himself right. he chose to do what was right. not everything in life is in our hands, but we are responsible for our choices. a good intention gains its value through the deed that carries it out. when you have the power, choose to protect rather than to hurt."},{"title":"How we express sorrow","stem":"how-we-express-sorrow","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#how-we-express-sorrow","hay":"how we express sorrow morals and character some ease their sorrow by talking,\nsome turn it into poetry or stories,\nsome reveal it in a glance or a sigh,\nand some hide it behind a smile. sorrow does not speak with one voice. sometimes the eyes say what the lips cannot; sometimes a weary heart lies behind a smiling face. do not measure someone’s pain only by what they say. a smile does not always mean peace, nor silence strength. listen without judging, and be there when they are ready to speak."},{"title":"Letting go","stem":"letting-go","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#letting-go","hay":"letting go morals and character there is grace in letting go— of those who show no appreciation, who trample on sincerity, who change their loyalties for personal gain, who are swollen with pride and arrogance, who envy others and spread gossip, who make a habit of lying, who know nothing of decency, who undermine your values, who turn a blind eye to ignorance, who scorn their homeland and disown their people, who forget their mother tongue, who fail to value a mother’s love, a father’s dignity, and a child’s affection… there is grace in letting go— of those who think you could never let them go. walking away from certain people is not weakness; it is self-respect. there is wisdom in refusing to depend on those who do not value you."},{"title":"He would not give so little","stem":"kings-generosity","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#kings-generosity","hay":"he would not give so little morals and character one day, a king met a little boy who caught his attention and offered him a gold coin. but the boy would not take it. “why not?” the king asked, surprised. “if i take it home, my parents will ask, ‘where did you steal that money?’” “then tell them the king gave it to you.” the boy answered at once: “they certainly won’t believe that! they’ll say, ‘if it came from the king, he would have given you more!’” the king laughed heartily at the boy’s quick wit and gave him a purse full of gold. a well-timed, witty remark can achieve more than a long speech. wit is at its best when it makes someone laugh without causing offense."},{"title":"Honey or dry bread?","stem":"honey-or-dry-bread","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#honey-or-dry-bread","hay":"honey or dry bread? morals and character better to share dry bread with a wise person than honey with a fool. good company offers more than a rich meal. choose companions whose conversation deepens your understanding and broadens your view of the world."},{"title":"How can I free myself from my thoughts?","stem":"letting-go-of-painful-thoughts","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#letting-go-of-painful-thoughts","hay":"how can i free myself from my thoughts? morals and character one day, a man came to a wise scholar and asked: “painful thoughts won’t leave me alone. i feel trapped by them. how can i free myself?” instead of answering, the scholar stood up, wrapped his arms around a nearby post, and shouted: “help! someone free me from this post!” the man stared in astonishment. “i came to you for advice, and now look at you! the post isn’t holding you—you’re holding it. open your arms, and you’ll be free!” the scholar let go and said quietly: “you’ve just found part of your answer. sometimes we return to a painful thought again and again, holding it as tightly as i held that post. we don’t have to cling to every thought or follow wherever it leads.” we cannot always choose which thoughts arise, but we can learn how to respond to them. letting go does not mean forcing ourselves to forget. it means learning not to let pain govern our whole lives. sometimes that takes time and support."},{"title":"Now I understand","stem":"now-i-understand","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#now-i-understand","hay":"now i understand morals and character in the words of someone who has weathered life’s ups and downs: i no longer try to make everyone like me. i understand that, whatever i do, some people simply won’t. i know my own worth now. i do not owe everyone proof of it. i keep my distance from people who build themselves up by putting others down. i can now tell the difference between words and intention: those who want to act look for a way; those who do not often find a “but.” i do not share my secrets with people who gossip. if they betray someone else’s trust, i cannot assume they will respect mine. i no longer exhaust myself trying to persuade someone who refuses to listen. not every conversation needs to become an argument, and not every disagreement needs to become a battle. i make no room in my life for people who habitually lie, make empty promises, and go back on their word. i distance myself from those who mistake cruelty and harshness for strength. i value people who can recognize their mistakes, apologize, and change their behavior. now i understand that i am happier with people around whom i can be myself. maturity lies less in trying to change others than in recognizing your own worth and boundaries. knowing whom to welcome into your life and when to step away is a way of respecting yourself and finding peace."},{"title":"I have been cleaning","stem":"inner-housekeeping","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#inner-housekeeping","hay":"i have been cleaning morals and character today i did some cleaning.\nnot in my home, but within myself… i looked into corners of my heart i hadn’t visited in a long time. one by one, i brought to light old hurts, fragments of broken hopes, and grievances i had never voiced. i began with the disappointments. i didn’t try to relive each one or work out who had been right and who had been wrong. i simply realized that i no longer wanted to carry them. how much room they had taken up inside me…\ni thought i had forgotten them long ago. but forgetting, it turned out, was not the same as being free. i made room for forgiveness—for others and for myself. not because what had happened was acceptable, but because i didn’t want to carry the pain for the rest of my life. then i faced my envy. how much beauty in my own life had i overlooked while watching other people’s lives! there had been times when i had treated someone else’s success as my own failure. i put gratitude in its place. i remembered what i had, the people beside me, and all i could still do. it felt as though i had rediscovered something long lost: the value of my own life. i looked carefully into my heart. thankfully, i found no deep-rooted hatred or malice. but some old wounds still ached. i didn’t blame myself for them. they needed time to heal. then came my fears. at first, i was afraid even to approach them. i had grown so used to living with them that imagining their absence felt strange. each was linked to the next: the fear of making mistakes, of losing what mattered, of not being liked… every worry had given rise to another fear. and i had let those fears persuade me to put off my dreams, my joy, my life. “i’ll wait a little longer…”\n“once everything is sorted out…”\n“i’ll start later…” how many todays had i lost while waiting for that later? i couldn’t make all my fears disappear in a single day. but i tried to listen to them and understand: which were protecting me, and which were standing in my way? i realized that fear didn’t always have to stop me. sometimes it was possible to take a step while still being afraid. i didn’t blame myself for the way i had lived before. yesterday, i hadn’t understood what i understood today. today i did some cleaning within myself. i made room for joy.\nfor hope, love, and faith in myself… i made room for kindness, for giving and sharing without expecting anything in return. i promised myself that loving others would not mean forgetting myself. i welcomed back a little playfulness, a little courage, and a little childlike openness. i remembered that i didn’t need a special reason to laugh. i made room for silence, too. sometimes the noise inside me has to settle before i can hear my own voice. finally, i began learning to accept myself, my past, and the things i could not change. that didn’t mean surrendering to everything. it meant ending the struggle with what i couldn’t change, so i could give my energy to what i could. today i did some cleaning within myself.\nthere was still more to do.\nbut inside, i had room to breathe again. caring for your heart means understanding your feelings, rather than suppressing them, and gradually letting go of what weighs you down. self-compassion makes room for love, hope, and peace."},{"title":"I would not trade it for anything","stem":"sunlit-day","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#sunlit-day","hay":"i would not trade it for anything morals and character a day,\na beautiful day,\na sunlit day—\ni wouldn’t trade it for anything. that is why i cannot love war,\nthat is why i cannot love cruelty,\nthat is why i cannot love lies. i know that in the sunlight,\ni know that alongside love,\nnone of these can live:\ninjustice,\nfear,\nhunger… — necati cumalı, a translated excerpt from the poem “güneş delisi” to cherish the beauty of life is to stand for peace, compassion, and truth. a sunlit day is truly precious when people can enjoy it freely, without fear or hunger."},{"title":"Between justice and compassion","stem":"justice-and-compassion","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#justice-and-compassion","hay":"between justice and compassion morals and character according to a tale told about the gallipoli campaign, morphine was so scarce that it was given only to wounded soldiers who still had a chance of survival. one day, among the severely wounded brought to dr. tarık nusret was his own son. seeing that he could not save him, the doctor made no exception for his son and withheld the injection. two hours later, his son died a martyr of his wounds. the father held his child’s lifeless body and whispered: “forgive me, my son... it was not yours to take.” refusing to let personal ties influence a decision can be a painful test. yet justice and compassion must remain inseparable: even when recovery is no longer possible, a person’s suffering and dignity still matter."},{"title":"Self-belief and truth","stem":"telling-lies","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#telling-lies","hay":"self-belief and truth morals and character “do you tell lies?” “yes.” “what kind of lies?” “a lie i tell only to myself. every day, i say, ‘you’re stronger, more knowledgeable, and more capable than anyone else.’” “perhaps you really are?” “no, i can’t claim that. there are people who are stronger, know more, or can do things better than i can.” “then why do you tell yourself that?” “believe it or not, those words motivate me. they make me want to work on myself—to become stronger, learn more, and improve my skills.” you do not need to think you are better than everyone else. saying “i can learn; i can improve” can give you strength too. self-belief becomes a foundation for growth when it is matched by effort. bakhtiyar sirajov"},{"title":"May your face always wear a smile","stem":"welcoming-smile","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#welcoming-smile","hay":"may your face always wear a smile morals and character a smiling vinegar seller attracts more customers than a scowling honey seller. people are drawn not only to what you offer, but also to how you offer it. a sincere smile and a warm welcome can leave a sweeter impression than honey."},{"title":"Keep your self-respect","stem":"keep-your-self-respect","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#keep-your-self-respect","hay":"keep your self-respect morals and character “don’t become a byword on people’s lips, or dust for them to walk on.,” says an azerbaijani expression. it calls on us to preserve our dignity in words and actions, avoid getting caught up in gossip, and refuse to accept humiliation. but remember: what people say about you does not always reflect how you live. you cannot silence every voice. what matters is staying true to your conscience and keeping your self-respect. do not let other people’s opinions become the measure of your life. take responsibility for your words and actions, and protect your boundaries. neither gossip nor someone else’s contempt determines your worth."},{"title":"Every day has a lesson","stem":"every-day-has-meaning","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#every-day-has-meaning","hay":"every day has a lesson morals and character do not dismiss any day of your life as wasted: good days bring joy,\ndifficult days bring experience,\nthe hardest days sometimes teach the deepest lessons,\nand the happiest days become unforgettable memories. a day’s meaning may not be clear while you are living it. cherish joy and be kind to yourself in hardship. understanding what an experience has taught you sometimes takes time."},{"title":"From complaints to solutions","stem":"beyond-complaining","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#beyond-complaining","hay":"from complaints to solutions morals and character a man with years of experience wants to teach a lesson to a group of people who keep complaining about the same problems. he tells them a funny joke. everyone laughs heartily. a little later, he tells the same joke again. this time, only a few people laugh. when he repeats it a third time, no one even smiles. repeating the same complaints without looking for a solution can have a similar effect: eventually, the words lose their impact and the listeners grow weary. speaking about a problem matters, but repeating it does not, by itself, solve it. sharing your troubles can bring relief, but complaining alone does not change the situation. looking for a way forward, asking for help, and taking a small step where possible can be the beginning of change."},{"title":"Do not share every sorrow with everyone","stem":"choose-your-confidant","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#choose-your-confidant","hay":"do not share every sorrow with everyone morals and character not every sorrow is for everyone to hear. one day, someone may use the pain you confided in them to hurt you again. share your troubles with someone who understands you and honors your trust. openness matters, but so does choosing whom to trust."},{"title":"Other people’s thoughts","stem":"other-peoples-thoughts","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#other-peoples-thoughts","hay":"other people’s thoughts morals and character one day, a man lost his comma (,). his words ran together, and he forgot where to pause for breath. another day, he lost his period (.). his thoughts stretched on endlessly; he could no longer finish one before moving to the next. then he lost his exclamation mark (!). his joy, anger, and wonder fell silent. one day, he lost his question mark (?). he stopped asking questions and accepted everything he heard without question. next, he lost his colon (:). he forgot how to explain his thoughts or give reasons for what he said. by the end of his life, all he had left were quotation marks. and inside them were nothing but other people’s thoughts. when we stop questioning, thinking, and expressing our feelings, we begin to lose our own voice. learning from others is valuable, but repeating their thoughts must not take the place of thinking for ourselves."},{"title":"Our strengths can become our downfall","stem":"strengths-and-vulnerabilities","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#strengths-and-vulnerabilities","hay":"our strengths can become our downfall morals and character the peacock’s enemy is its beautiful tail. the nightingale’s sweet song draws it into a cage. the fox’s fine fur lures hunters to set their traps. the qualities we admire and value in ourselves can sometimes become our vulnerabilities and bring us trouble. what we consider an advantage can also leave us exposed to danger. knowing our worth matters, but so does understanding where our strengths make us vulnerable."},{"title":"Words to avoid","stem":"words-to-avoid","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#words-to-avoid","hay":"words to avoid morals and character disagreements are part of family life. but the words we choose can either help us understand one another or damage the closeness between us. “stop talking nonsense!” this tells your partner, “your opinion does not matter to me.” you can disagree without dismissing what they say: “i see this differently. i’d like to understand your point of view too.” “you’re overreacting!” something that seems minor to you may deeply trouble your partner. rather than dismissing their feelings, ask: “what worries you most about this? i want to listen.” “look at her husband—or his wife! why can’t you be like that?” comparisons can leave someone feeling inadequate and unappreciated. instead of holding up another person as an example, explain what you need: “it matters to me that we share the housework. can we find an arrangement that works for both of us?” “you wouldn’t understand!” this closes the door to conversation. if you cannot find the words or are not ready to talk, say so honestly: “i’m finding it hard to explain how i feel. please give me a little time, and we can talk later.” “don’t go there! don’t speak to that person! don’t wear that!” your partner’s decisions are not yours to control. explain your concerns, listen to their perspective, and seek agreement on matters that affect you both. adding “please” to an order does not automatically make it a respectful request; you must also recognize the other person’s right to choose. “this worries me. can we talk about it and try to understand each other?” respect matters even when we disagree. explain your feelings, make your needs clear, and listen in return. closeness grows when we hear each other, not when we silence each other."},{"title":"Strength and sorrow","stem":"strength-and-sorrow","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#strength-and-sorrow","hay":"strength and sorrow morals and character o allah, grant me either the strength to bear my sorrows or only as much sorrow as i have the strength to bear. no one can remain strong through every sorrow. seeking strength, asking for help, and recognizing our limits in difficult times are not signs of weakness."},{"title":"The flower of honesty","stem":"flower-of-honesty","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#flower-of-honesty","hay":"the flower of honesty morals and character in a distant kingdom, a young prince was preparing to take the throne. by royal custom, he had to marry before his coronation. he invited the young women of the kingdom to the palace so that he could choose his future wife. the daughter of a woman who worked at the palace loved the prince. when she heard about the invitation, she told her mother: “i want to go too.” her mother was worried. “the daughters of wealthy, distinguished families will be there. i’m afraid you’ll be disappointed.” “i know, mother. but i’d like the chance to meet him, even just once.” on the appointed day, the palace hall filled with young women in beautiful gowns. the prince gave each of them a seed. “plant this and care for it,” he said. “return in six months with the flower you have grown. i will marry the woman who brings the most beautiful flower.” the young women went home full of hope. the servant’s daughter planted her seed in a pot. every day, she checked the soil, watered it as needed, and eagerly waited for the first green shoot. but days and weeks passed, and nothing appeared. she sought advice from gardeners and followed their suggestions. still, the seed did not sprout. when the six months were over, her mother asked: “will you really go with an empty pot?” the young woman looked down at it. “yes. i’ve done everything i could. i can’t take a different flower and pretend i grew it from his seed.” at the palace, flowers bloomed in every color. her own pot held only soil. embarrassed, she stood to one side. the prince entered the hall and examined the pots one by one. when he reached her, he asked: “where is your flower?” “i cared for the seed for six months, but it never sprouted,” she replied. “i have no flower to show you.” the prince listened carefully. then he turned to the gathering. “i have made my choice.” he pointed to the young woman holding the empty pot. a murmur of astonishment filled the hall. “but she has no flower!” “none of the seeds i gave you could grow,” the prince explained. “this young woman had the courage to bring me the result as it was. she has brought me the most precious flower of all: honesty.” honesty sometimes means having the courage to stand before everyone with an empty pot. there is no shame in falling short of the result you hoped for. pretending you achieved it means betraying your conscience."},{"title":"Plucking the goose","stem":"plucking-the-goose","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#plucking-the-goose","hay":"plucking the goose morals and character one bitter winter day, a king changed out of his royal robes and went for a walk with his grand vizier. they came upon an old man working beside a stream, dipping hides into the icy water and beating them as he worked the leather. “peace be upon you, venerable elder,” the king said. “and upon you, ruler of the world,” the old man replied. “what did you do during the six?” the king asked. “unless you add six to six, there isn’t enough for thirty-two.” “and did you never get up in the night?” “i did, your majesty. but other households have reaped the benefit.” the king smiled. “if i send you a goose, can you pluck it?” “certainly, your majesty—and without a squawk!” the king and his vizier walked on. once they were out of earshot, the king asked: “did you understand our conversation?” “no, your majesty,” the vizier admitted. “then work it out before nightfall,” the king said sternly, “or it will cost you your head!” after escorting the king back to the palace, the frightened vizier hurried to the stream. the old man was still at work. “what were you and the king talking about?” he demanded. “you must explain!” the old man looked him up and down. “each answer will cost you a hundred gold coins.” the vizier had little choice. he handed over the money. “first, how did you know he was the king?” “i work with hides. one look told me what his fur coat was worth. only a king could afford such a thing.” the vizier produced another hundred coins. “what did six have to do with thirty-two?” “he was asking what i had done during the six months of summer, if i still had to work in this bitter cold. i told him that unless i worked six months in summer and six in winter, i couldn’t keep these thirty-two teeth fed.” the vizier paid a third time. “and what did he mean about getting up in the night?” “he was asking whether i had children. i told him i did, but they were all daughters. they have married and now have households of their own to look after.” the vizier paused before asking his final question. “and the goose he promised to send you—who was that?” the old man glanced at the gold and smiled. “that one you can work out for yourself!” hearing the words is not the same as understanding them. high office does not guarantee wisdom; sometimes an ordinary person, taught by life, sees what a royal adviser cannot."},{"title":"The strongest shield","stem":"strongest-shield","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#strongest-shield","hay":"the strongest shield morals and character sometimes the strongest shield in life is the distance you know how to keep. keeping your distance in relationships that hurt you is an act of self-respect. closeness matters, but so does protecting your personal boundaries."},{"title":"The three best things","stem":"three-best-things","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#three-best-things","hay":"the three best things morals and character a father said to his son: “my son, we should strive for three things in life: to eat the finest food, sleep in the most comfortable bed, and live in the most beautiful home.” the boy was puzzled. “but, dad, we’re poor. how can we have those things?” his father smiled. “when you’re hungry, even a simple meal tastes like the finest food. when you lie down tired after a day of hard work, your bed feels wonderfully comfortable. and when you make room for others in your heart and earn a place in theirs, you will find the most beautiful home of all.” life’s pleasures are not found only in expensive things. savoring a simple meal, resting after honest work, and finding a place in people’s hearts can bring great happiness too."},{"title":"Think kindly, speak kindly, act kindly","stem":"thought-word-and-deed","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#thought-word-and-deed","hay":"think kindly, speak kindly, act kindly morals and character think good thoughts, for your thoughts are reflected in your words. speak with kindness, for your words pave the way for your actions. act with care, for repeated actions become habits. cultivate good habits, for they shape your values. honor your values, for they guide your choices, and your choices shape your life. our thoughts, words, actions, and habits are connected. by putting good intentions into everyday practice, we help steer our lives in a better direction."},{"title":"Change your thinking and try again","stem":"learning-to-say-yet","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#learning-to-say-yet","hay":"change your thinking and try again morals and character instead of saying, “i can’t do this,” say, “there are things i still need to learn. with more knowledge and practice, i can get better.” instead of saying, “i give up,” say, “what if i tried another way? if this approach hasn’t worked, a different one might help.” instead of stopping at “this is too hard,” say, “it’s difficult, but i can take it step by step. i need a little more time, effort, and perhaps some help.” instead of saying, “i can’t do any better,” say, “i’m pleased with what i’ve achieved, but i can keep learning and improving.” instead of blaming yourself with “i made a mistake—i wish i hadn’t,” ask, “how can i put it right? what have i learned from it?” instead of saying, “they’re so clever. i could never be like them,” say, “by looking at how they learn and work, i can find an approach that helps me.” the way you speak to yourself influences your next step. adding “yet” to “i can’t do this” leaves room for learning. a mistake becomes an opportunity to grow when you learn from it and try again."},{"title":"Turning hardship into opportunity","stem":"obstacles-and-opportunities","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#obstacles-and-opportunities","hay":"turning hardship into opportunity morals and character sometimes life runs smoothly; at other times, unexpected difficulties arise. obstacles are not always easy to overcome, but they can offer a chance to learn, find another way, and move forward. being shaken by hardship is not weakness. give yourself time, ask for help if you need it, and let what you learn guide your next step. sometimes an obstacle is where a new path comes into view."},{"title":"Whoever handles honey licks their fingers","stem":"grocers-sticky-fingers","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#grocers-sticky-fingers","hay":"whoever handles honey licks their fingers morals and character a beekeeper brings several containers of honeycomb to a grocer to sell. they agree on a price and begin weighing it. the grocer has only a one-kilogram weight, so he breaks the honeycomb into pieces by hand and weighs it a kilogram at a time. but before placing each new piece on the scales, he licks the honey from his fingers. once, twice, three times… the beekeeper notices that some of his honey is reaching the grocer’s mouth before it reaches the scales. he objects, but the grocer carries on as though nothing has happened. at last, the beekeeper loses patience. “come on, now! you’ve licked away half my honey!” still licking his sticky fingers, the grocer gives him a sly smile. “no harm intended, my friend. you can’t handle honey without licking your fingers!” it is natural to gain something from the work you do. but that is no excuse for helping yourself to someone else’s share."},{"title":"Do not climb down a well on his rope","stem":"alis-unreliable-rope","category":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","href":"categories/exlaq-ve-xarakter.html#alis-unreliable-rope","hay":"do not climb down a well on his rope morals and character there was once a craftsman named ali who made rope from flax fibers. he was careless about his work, and his ropes were weak. they often snapped and caused accidents. before long, people began calling him “ali of the rotten ropes.” one day, a lamb fell into a deep well. a man who offered to rescue it asked the owner to fetch a rope. but as soon as he saw it, he shook his head. “i know this rope. it’s ali’s work. you can’t trust his rope to lower you into a well!” the lamb’s owner smiled. “now, don’t be unfair to him. his rope will certainly get you down. whether it will bring you back up—well, that i can’t promise!” trust is earned through the quality of your work. carelessness does more than damage your reputation; it puts those who rely on you at risk."},{"title":"Communication","stem":"communication","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#communication","hay":"communication word, silence, and communication make time to talk with older people and children alike. in older people’s memories, you may discover lessons drawn from experience; in children’s questions, the beauty of seeing the world with fresh eyes. listening to older people helps us learn from lives already lived. listening to children helps us rediscover wonder in ordinary things. every age has something to teach us."},{"title":"Language","stem":"language","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#language","hay":"language word, silence, and communication language is a flower that blossoms on the tongue;\nwithout water and care, it withers away. a mother tongue thrives when it is cherished. keep it alive in your daily conversations, in the books you read, and in the words you teach your children."},{"title":"Silence","stem":"silence","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#silence","hay":"silence word, silence, and communication silence is the answer to some questions. either the time to answer has not yet come, or the person asking does not deserve a reply. you do not owe an answer to every question. sometimes wisdom lies in waiting for the right moment; sometimes, in remaining silent with someone who will not value your words."},{"title":"The silent corridor","stem":"silent-corridor","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#silent-corridor","hay":"the silent corridor word, silence, and communication two organizations were working together: one was the sponsor, and the other was the agency developing the project. the project team worked tirelessly for months to prepare the documents. when everything was ready, they sent the materials to the sponsor and waited for a response. days passed, then weeks. still, there was silence. the uncertainty within the team grew, spreading like a shadow along the corridor: “have the documents arrived? have they been reviewed?” no one knew. inquiries to the sponsor brought only vague, evasive answers. a few months later, the team decided to go directly to the sponsor’s office to clarify the matter. there, the truth finally emerged: “yes, we received the documents, but we have not accepted them. the submission format was wrong, and the required procedure hadn’t been followed.” the team froze in place. “why didn’t you tell us that?” they asked. “we could have corrected it right away.” frustrated, the employees returned to the office and complained to their management: “we did make inquiries, but we should not have settled for vague answers. we should have asked for a clear answer about whether the documents had been accepted. when no clear answer came, we should have met with those responsible sooner to resolve the matter. the sponsor, too, should have told us promptly why the documents had not been accepted. we could then have corrected the shortcomings sooner and avoided months of waiting. this uncertainty cost us time and shook our trust.” in a partnership, making inquiries is not enough; we must also seek clear answers. the responding party must communicate its decision and the reasons for it promptly. when uncertainty persists, it is important to clarify the matter directly with those responsible. sometimes the greatest harm to a partnership comes not from failure, but from silence. bakhtiyar sirajov"},{"title":"Well-chosen words","stem":"well-chosen-words","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#well-chosen-words","hay":"well-chosen words word, silence, and communication those who choose their words thoughtfully need not regret them and apologize later. before you speak, consider how your words will affect the person listening. words carry greater value when spoken at the right time, in the right setting, and with respect."},{"title":"Share what you know","stem":"share-what-you-know","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#share-what-you-know","hay":"share what you know word, silence, and communication share what you know, but do not lecture others. listen carefully, but do not take everything you hear as the truth. silence does not mean ignorance, just as talking a great deal does not mean knowing a great deal. belittling others is foolish; showering them with undeserved praise is a sign of cowardice. share your knowledge with humility, think critically about what you hear, and know when to remain silent. neither put people down nor place them on a pedestal. see them as they are and give them their due."},{"title":"The candles' conversation","stem":"candles-conversation","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#candles-conversation","hay":"the candles' conversation word, silence, and communication four candles burned slowly in a room. it was so quiet that even their whispers could be heard. the first candle spoke: “i am faith. when people neglect me, my light grows weak. i am about to go out.” its flame grew smaller until, at last, it disappeared. the second candle said: “i am loyalty. when promises are forgotten and people turn their backs on one another, i lose my strength. i can hardly keep burning.” just then, a gentle draft put out its flame. the third candle spoke sadly: “i am love. when people stop making time for one another and showing that they care, my light begins to fade. sometimes even those closest to each other forget to say how much they love one another.” as it finished speaking, its flame flickered and went out. at that moment, a child entered the room. when the child saw the three unlit candles, their eyes filled with tears. “you were meant to keep shining… why have you gone out?” then the fourth candle spoke: “don’t be afraid. as long as i am burning, we can light the others again. i am hope.” the child wiped away the tears, carefully picked up the burning candle, and lit the others one by one. once again, the room filled with light. hope gives us the strength to restore faith, loyalty, and love. if we keep that light alive and act on it, we can rekindle much that we thought had been lost."},{"title":"Those who know and those who do not","stem":"knowing-what-you-know","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#knowing-what-you-know","hay":"those who know and those who do not word, silence, and communication adapted saying. a similar four-part formulation appears in al-ghazali’s book of knowledge, section vi, attributed there to al-khalil ibn ahmad. those who do not know, and know that they do not know, are ready to learn. teach them! those who know, but do not realize that they know, are asleep. awaken them! those who do not know, and do not realize that they do not know, are foolish. avoid them! those who know, and know that they know, can show the way. follow them! wisdom begins with recognizing what you know and what you do not. help those willing to learn, encourage those unaware of their knowledge, be wary of those confident in their ignorance, and learn from those who truly understand."},{"title":"The shepherd’s word","stem":"shepherds-word","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#shepherds-word","hay":"the shepherd’s word word, silence, and communication once, in a village, there lived a poor but honest shepherd named hasan. one day, the local landowner summoned him. “hasan, i hear you know the mountain pastures well. this summer, i shall entrust my flock to you. if you bring the animals back healthy and well fed in the autumn, i shall give you a cow.” hasan agreed. all summer, through rain and heat, he tended the flock and kept watch over it at night. in the autumn, he brought the animals back to the village healthier and better fed than before. he went to the landowner, hoping to receive the promised cow. but the man gave him a mocking smile. “i never intended to give you a cow. i only said that to make you work harder. you’re poor anyway—what would you do with a cow?” tears filled hasan’s eyes. yet he composed himself and answered calmly. “you’re right, sir. i am poor. but i have a conscience, and i keep my word. you may be wealthy, but if your word is worth nothing, you are the one who is truly poor.” the shepherd’s words spread throughout the village. the landowner lost people’s respect, and they became reluctant to do business with him. he, too, eventually understood: a person’s worth is measured not by wealth or position, but by how faithfully they keep their word. our true wealth lies in our conscience, the promises we keep, and the trust we earn. riches cannot guarantee respect, while a broken promise can undermine trust."},{"title":"What is a word?","stem":"what-is-a-word","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#what-is-a-word","hay":"what is a word? word, silence, and communication some words wound;\nsome words are pearls. a word can break a heart or win one. what makes it a pearl is saying it in the right place, at the right time, and from the heart."},{"title":"Words and silence","stem":"words-and-silence","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#words-and-silence","hay":"words and silence word, silence, and communication sometimes we say more by remaining silent. words are more than sounds—they reveal our intentions. if a word will wound a heart or a sentence extinguish hope, pause before you speak. if what you are about to say offers neither understanding nor comfort and serves no good purpose, silence may be the better choice. remember: not every word blossoms. some are thorns that wound whatever they touch. silence can hold wisdom. sometimes it is the most meaningful answer. words spoken thoughtfully and from the heart can give someone strength. careless words can leave wounds that take a long time to heal. choose both your words and your silences with care. silence speaks too—sometimes more deeply and more honestly than words. the worth of words lies not in how many we speak, but in the good they do. think before you speak, and be mindful of what your silence says."},{"title":"Call the shepherd","stem":"call-the-shepherd","category":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","href":"categories/soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet.html#call-the-shepherd","hay":"call the shepherd word, silence, and communication after many years of study, a student came to his teacher to ask for his blessing before setting out. “you shall have my blessing,” the teacher said, “but first you must answer a question. let me see how well you have understood what i have taught you.” “please, master, ask,” the student replied confidently. “imagine you are returning to your village. along the way, a shepherd’s dogs rush at you. what would you do to protect yourself?” “i would pick up some stones and throw them at the dogs to drive them away.” “you might drive away one or two. but what if they all came at you together?” the student thought for a moment. “then i would run.” “and if the dogs ran faster and caught up with you?” the student fell silent. unable to think of another way out, he asked: “what should i do, master?” “call the shepherd,” the teacher replied. “ask him to call off his dogs. you thought about fighting each dog yourself, but it never occurred to you to ask for help from the person who could calm them.” not every difficulty can be overcome alone or by force. sometimes the wisest step is to find someone who can influence the situation and ask for help."},{"title":"A different path","stem":"different-path","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#different-path","hay":"a different path wisdom and life lessons one day, a cow returning to its barn had to cross a forest where there was no path. it climbed uphill, wandered downhill, and wound its way around trees and bushes, leaving a crooked trail behind it. the next day, a dog followed the trail to reach the other side of the forest. then came a flock of sheep. seeing a path already there, the shepherd led his flock along it without looking for another route. before long, people began using it too. they turned this way and that, ducked beneath branches, and complained about the difficult walk. yet no one stopped to ask: “could there be an easier way?” as more feet passed over it, the trail widened into a road. people and laden animals spent three hours reaching a place they could have reached in half an hour by a more direct route. still, everyone followed in the footsteps of those who had gone before. years passed. a town grew where the forest had stood. the old road became a main thoroughfare and eventually the town’s busiest street. the bends the cow had made while wandering among the trees remained unchanged. now cars became stuck along those same bends. everyone complained about the traffic, but few wondered why the road followed such a route. at the edge of town, the old forest seemed to whisper: “one cow’s wandering footsteps have determined the path of generations…” a path is not necessarily the best one simply because it has been used for years. before following a habit, try to understand its purpose. sometimes progress means changing your route, not walking faster."},{"title":"A human being emerged","stem":"human-being-emerged","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#human-being-emerged","hay":"a human being emerged wisdom and life lessons i multiplied thought by heart—and the result was love. i added effort to love—and the result was devotion. i shared bread according to my conscience—and the result was justice. i added love, effort, conscience, devotion, and justice—and the result was a human being. — attributed to yaşar kemal love, effort, conscience, devotion, and justice make us truly human. our humanity shows when we put these values into practice, not merely into words."},{"title":"Appearances can be deceptive","stem":"light-of-the-world","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#light-of-the-world","hay":"appearances can be deceptive wisdom and life lessons a father wolf is teaching his cub about life. he points to a flock of sheep and says: “their meat tastes good.” then he points to the shepherd. “but his staff is bitter. be careful.” the cub notices the dog beside the shepherd and exclaims happily: “look, dad! he looks like us!” the father replies: “run when you see him, my boy. we’ve suffered most at the hands of those who look like us but aren’t on our side.” outward resemblance does not mean that people share the same intentions or goals. when deciding whom to trust, we should look at their behaviour and actions, not their appearance."},{"title":"A more effective way","stem":"sharpen-the-axe","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#sharpen-the-axe","hay":"a more effective way wisdom and life lessons a hunter walking through a forest came upon a curious sight. a woodcutter was swinging his axe with all his strength, yet making little progress. sweat ran down his forehead, and every blow took a tremendous effort. the hunter approached him. “your axe is blunt. why not stop for a moment and sharpen it? the work would be much easier.” the woodcutter stared at him in surprise. “what are you talking about? i haven’t got time to sharpen my axe. i still have twenty more trees to cut down!” working efficiently means thinking about how we work, not just how hard. time spent preparing and improving our tools is not wasted; it saves effort later."},{"title":"A windmill turning without wind","stem":"windmill-turning-without-wind","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#windmill-turning-without-wind","hay":"a windmill turning without wind wisdom and life lessons if a windmill turns when there is no wind, someone must be making it turn. what you see may have a cause you cannot see. look beyond the result to understand what sets it in motion."},{"title":"At the bookstore","stem":"bookstore","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#bookstore","hay":"at the bookstore wisdom and life lessons after a long search, a medical student found the book he needed in a bookshop. he picked it up eagerly, but his smile faded when he saw the price on the back. he could not afford it. hiding his disappointment, he leafed through the pages until he found the section his lecturer had assigned. seeing that the staff were busy with customers, he stood to one side and began reading. he remembered as much as he could, then put the book back and quietly left. for the next month, he returned every other day. he studied among the shelves, believing no one had noticed. a week before his final exams, he came back as usual. this time, the book was missing. he searched shelf after shelf, growing increasingly anxious. as he was about to leave empty-handed, a member of staff called him over. “come and sit down. there’s something we’d like to tell you.” he took the book from beneath the desk and placed it in front of the student. “a customer wanted to buy this the other day. but we could see you hadn’t finished reading it, so we put it aside for you. whenever you come in, you can sit here and study in comfort.” for a moment, the student could not find the words to reply. he had thought he was going unnoticed. yet the bookshop staff had understood his difficulty all along and helped him without embarrassing him. years later, he became a doctor. he never forgot the kindness he had received at the bookshop: he treated patients who could not afford his care without charging them. kindness that preserves a person’s dignity can leave a lifelong impression. the care you show one person may, through them, reach many others."},{"title":"At the doctor’s office","stem":"doctors-office","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#doctors-office","hay":"at the doctor’s office wisdom and life lessons i was waiting for my doctor’s appointment. having missed breakfast at home, i had bought a sandwich and some apple juice from the café. as i began to eat, i noticed a boy sitting by the window watching me. he kept looking at the food in my hands. “he must be hungry,” i thought. i felt sorry for him. i went back to the café and bought him a sandwich and juice too. he thanked me and began eating eagerly. i felt pleased, thinking i had helped someone. just then, his mother returned from the reception desk. seeing the food in his hands, she hurried over, alarmed. “did you give him that?” i nodded. “the doctor said he mustn’t eat before his examination… i’ve taken time off work, and we’ve come a long way. now we may have to reschedule.” her eyes filled with tears. i did not know what to say. a moment earlier, i had been certain i was doing something kind. now i realized that i had never asked why the boy had not eaten. “i’m sorry… i wanted to help, but i should have asked you first.” i sat there holding my half-eaten sandwich. i had noticed the boy’s gaze, but acted without understanding his situation. good intentions do not always guarantee a good outcome. asking, listening, and understanding the situation before offering help are also acts of compassion."},{"title":"Another day","stem":"another-day","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#another-day","hay":"another day wisdom and life lessons the day began... the day ended... every morning when we open our eyes, we see that a new day has begun. yet often we do not notice when evening arrives or when another day slips away from our lives. time flows like a quiet river, while we stand on its bank, watching the passing moments drift away. sometimes we ask ourselves: what wisdom lay behind god’s gift of this day to me? did i cherish this divine trust? what did i add to this day, and what did i take from it? what light did i bring to my soul, and what opportunity did i let slip away? the greatest treasure in a human life is time. for other things we lose may sometimes return, but not even a single moment that has passed comes back. that is why every day, every hour, and even every breath has its own value and meaning. and so, i have lived another day... in the endless book of time, another page of my life has turned. only god truly knows what was written on that page. every new day is a precious trust given to us. reflecting at the end of the day on how we have spent it helps us live more meaningfully tomorrow. etibar siracsoy"},{"title":"Small changes, great value…","stem":"small-changes-great-value","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#small-changes-great-value","hay":"small changes, great value… wisdom and life lessons a monotonous life quietly wears down the human spirit. when every day is the same as the one before, when the same tasks are repeated, or when time passes without our doing anything at all, an invisible emptiness and a sense of inner unease take hold. for the spirit, like life itself, longs for movement, change, and something new. sometimes a small change enters our lives, and it is as though a new window opens within us. ordinary days suddenly take on color, and our hearts fill with a pleasant excitement, a zest for life, and a sense of well-being. in truth, the beauty of life lies not only in the chance events that come our way, but also in how much beauty and freshness we ourselves can bring into it. we should not be mere spectators in our own lives. sometimes choosing a new path, meeting someone new, seeing a new place, learning, creating, or making time for something we love is enough to change the color of life. for something new need not be a major event; sometimes a small step can greatly lift our spirits. life is not a page handed to us already written. we write each day ourselves. it is therefore in our own hands to fill the pages of our lives not with repetition, but with new feelings, new dreams, and beautiful memories. we do not need to wait for major changes to bring something new into our lives. even a little time devoted to learning and the things we love can give meaning to our day. etibar siracsoy"},{"title":"Eyes that do not see","stem":"seeing-without-understanding","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#seeing-without-understanding","hay":"eyes that do not see wisdom and life lessons some people think that because their eyes are open, they see everything. yet they miss the meaning of what they see, the pain of those they pass, and the truth right before them. truly seeing takes attention, reflection, and compassion. it means understanding what you notice and caring about the suffering you recognize."},{"title":"The dry well","stem":"dry-well","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#dry-well","hay":"the dry well wisdom and life lessons one day, a farmer’s donkey fell into an old, dry well. hearing the animal’s distressed braying, the farmer called his neighbors for help. for hours, they tried to get the donkey out. everyone had a suggestion, but nothing worked. eventually, they gave up hope and decided to fill the well with earth. they each took a shovel and began throwing earth into the well. as the first shovelfuls landed on its back, the donkey brayed even louder. then, after a while, it fell silent. the farmer peered into the well and could hardly believe what he saw. the donkey was shaking each shovelful off its back and stepping onto the earth beneath its feet. with every shovelful, it rose a little higher. the earth meant to bury it was becoming a stairway to safety. at last, the donkey reached the top of the well and stepped out unharmed, to everyone’s astonishment. life sometimes places a heavy burden on your back. when you can, shake it off, find your footing, and take another step upward. sometimes the very thing that makes life difficult can become part of the way out."},{"title":"The Diderot effect","stem":"diderot-effect","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#diderot-effect","hay":"the diderot effect wisdom and life lessons in his essay regrets on parting with my old dressing gown, the eighteenth-century french philosopher and writer denis diderot describes how a single possession can lead to a succession of new expenses. it all begins with a luxurious scarlet dressing gown. when diderot puts it on, the room where he once felt comfortable suddenly looks different. none of his old belongings has changed, yet beside the beautiful gown they now seem shabby and out of place. the simple furnishings that once served him well no longer satisfy him. one change leads to another. a leather armchair replaces his plain chair. an ornate cabinet takes the place of his wooden bookshelf. the pictures and decorations change. eventually, even his old worktable gives way to an expensive writing desk. the room becomes more elegant, but diderot feels less at ease. the new gown has changed not only what he wears, but how he sees everything around him. things he never needed before now seem essential. he sums up his predicament: “i was the master of my old dressing gown; i have become the slave of the new one.” he decides to keep his old rug. let that modest possession remind him of his former life and keep him from losing himself in luxury. today, the urge to follow one new acquisition with other purchases to match it is known as the diderot effect. sometimes a purchase does more than meet a need: it creates needs we did not have before. one new possession does not require you to replace everything around it. before buying something, ask yourself: “do i really need this, or do i only want it to match something else?” let your belongings make life easier, not dictate your choices."},{"title":"Free time","stem":"free-time","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#free-time","hay":"free time wisdom and life lessons some people talk to you when they have time to spare. others make time to talk to you. time is a treasure we cannot recover once it is gone. cherish those who willingly make room in their lives to listen to you and be there for you."},{"title":"The truth we see and the truth we do not","stem":"istanbul-woman-remembers","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#istanbul-woman-remembers","hay":"the truth we see and the truth we do not wisdom and life lessons a tale set during the occupation of istanbul is told in the voice of a young woman: it was during the occupation of istanbul. i had just finished school, and families were beginning to visit ours with marriage proposals. one day, a young man came with his family. he introduced himself as a lawyer. he was tall, handsome, and quietly composed. we liked each other and became engaged. i loved him. at night, i worked by lamplight, embroidering linens for the home i imagined we would share. every stitch held a hope for our future. then a rumor spread through the neighborhood. “ayşe’s fiancé isn’t a lawyer at all. he earns his living carrying coffins outside the mosque.” what hurt most was not the work itself, but the thought that he had lied to me. my father and i went to the mosque. from a distance, we saw him carrying a coffin with several other men. we took that sight as proof of everything we had heard. when i asked him to explain, he said he could not tell me everything. i took his silence as an admission that he had lied. we broke off the engagement. five years passed. i married and had a son. one day, walking through the city with my little boy, i met him again. he stopped when he saw me, then spoke hesitantly: “if you have time, may i invite you for a cup of tea?” i agreed. we went to a nearby law office. his name was on the door. i could not hide my surprise. “so you really are a lawyer?” “yes.” “then why were you carrying coffins outside the mosque?” he was silent for a while. then he lowered his head. “i wish i could have explained it to you then. the city was occupied. we were helping to get weapons to those resisting in anatolia. some of the coffins held weapons, not a body. revealing that secret could have put other people’s lives at risk. i couldn’t even tell you.” this time, i was the one who fell silent. the scene i had witnessed years before had not changed. but its meaning had changed completely. i had seen the coffin on his shoulder. i had known nothing of the responsibility he carried. what you see may be only part of the truth. before judging, ask, listen, and remember that there are things you do not know. sometimes a mistaken judgment keeps us from recognizing someone’s unseen sacrifice."},{"title":"The world will not end if I put it off a few days","stem":"cost-of-indifference","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#cost-of-indifference","hay":"the world will not end if i put it off a few days wisdom and life lessons when i asked one of our employees how he was getting on with an assigned task, his reply disappointed me deeply. “the world won’t fall apart if i don’t do it in the next few days.” true, i thought, the world would not fall apart. but what about the trust between us? would that not collapse too? would that attitude not harm our working relationship, our mutual respect, and our shared work? a delay of a few days was not the real issue. it was his attitude toward a commitment. responsibility means not only doing the work but also keeping your word, honoring the trust others place in you, and making sure people can rely on you. it is wrong to treat a commitment so irresponsibly. even more troubling was the way he expressed that attitude. to me, such an answer sounds rude, to say the least, and shows too little respect for the other person’s time, effort, and trust. i admit that these words broke my heart. i knew then that we could not continue working together in quite the same way. a professional relationship depends on trust, and when trust is shaken, the partnership also faces a serious test. most disappointing of all, my colleague did not recognize that his words were inappropriate, apologize, or complete the task. because my trust in him had been shaken, i had to solve the problem another way. the experience reminded me that an organization’s most valuable asset is not time, money, or technology, but the trust people place in one another. it can take years to build and can sometimes be lost with a single sentence. trust is an organization’s most valuable asset. it can take years to earn and a single sentence to lose. those who keep their word protect that trust. bakhtiyar sirajov"},{"title":"The door that will not open may not be yours","stem":"different-door","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#different-door","hay":"the door that will not open may not be yours wisdom and life lessons one day, jamal went to see his manager, mr. selim. he had heard that promotions were coming up and wanted to say that he hoped to be considered. mr. selim listened, then replied: “jamal, i’m pleased with your work. but i don’t think this company is the right fit for you. we’ve decided to end your employment.” jamal had arrived hoping for a promotion. the news stunned him, and he left the company deeply disappointed. two years later, they met again. after exchanging greetings, jamal told his former manager that he had started his own company and that business was going well. mr. selim was delighted to hear it. jamal said: “at the time, your decision was very hard to accept. looking back, though, i can see that losing my job pushed me to explore other possibilities. i started my own business and began doing work i love. i’m happy with my life now. what felt like an ending became a new beginning, and i’m grateful for that.” a door that will not open is not always the end of the road. sometimes losing an opportunity prompts us to seek a path that suits us better. what matters is not remaining trapped in disappointment, but recognizing new possibilities and taking action."},{"title":"It’s pointless","stem":"its-pointless","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#its-pointless","hay":"it’s pointless wisdom and life lessons there is no point in advising someone who refuses to listen, arguing with someone unwilling to understand, or befriending someone who is two-faced. who you give your time and trust to shapes your life. time spent in relationships without mutual respect and sincerity cannot be reclaimed."},{"title":"Never lost","stem":"never-lost","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#never-lost","hay":"never lost wisdom and life lessons kindness offered from the heart,\nan act shaped by love,\na sincerely spoken word,\na gesture of courtesy,\na smile full of affection,\na step taken in the name of justice,\nrespect for the values we hold dear— none of these is ever lost. they light the way for others and bring warmth to your own life and peace to your heart. sincere words and kind deeds leave a lasting mark. the light you bring into someone else’s life brightens your own as well."},{"title":"The lecturer and the groom","stem":"audience-of-one","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#audience-of-one","hay":"the lecturer and the groom wisdom and life lessons a lecturer was invited to give an educational talk in a village. when he entered the hall, he found just one man sitting there. he greeted him and asked: “no one else has come. what do you think—should we go ahead with the lecture?” his sole listener replied: “sir, i’m a stable hand. i look after twenty horses. if nineteen ran away and only one remained, i wouldn’t leave it hungry and thirsty. i’d still feed it and give it water.” encouraged by these words, the lecturer began. he talked at great length. when he finally finished, he asked: “well, did you enjoy my lecture?” the stable hand replied: “i look after horses, so i didn’t understand much of what you said. but i do know one thing: just because only one horse is left, i wouldn’t give it enough feed for twenty!” speaking well is not the same as speaking at length. a useful talk meets the listener’s needs and offers as much as they can understand and absorb."},{"title":"Life is short","stem":"life-is-short","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#life-is-short","hay":"life is short wisdom and life lessons life is short. spend it with people who bring you joy and make you feel loved. the worth of a life is measured not only in years, but in the love and joy we share. genuine relationships bring meaning to our lives and peace to our hearts."},{"title":"The liver","stem":"liver","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#liver","hay":"the liver wisdom and life lessons why do azerbaijani speakers call a close friend jan-jiyar, a beloved child jiyargusham, or someone precious to them jiyarparam? and why can aghjiyar, the word for “lung,” also describe a coward? in azerbaijani, jiyar is more than an anatomical term. it also appears in expressions of affection, closeness, sorrow, and character. similar associations existed in ancient cultures. in mesopotamia, the liver was regarded as a seat of life, the soul, and emotions. when people sought to explain their inner world, they gave symbolic meaning not only to the heart but also to the liver. yet not every expression involving the liver can be traced to a single belief. each expression has its own history and development. today, someone saying jiyarparam—literally “a piece of my liver”—is not thinking about anatomy. they mean that another person is as precious as a part of themselves. this is the power of language: a word for part of the body becomes an expression of our deepest feelings. language carries not only our thoughts but also the ideas of earlier generations. an everyday word can hold generations of love, sorrow, and ways of understanding the world."},{"title":"Living in the past","stem":"living-in-the-past","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#living-in-the-past","hay":"living in the past wisdom and life lessons living in the past is like walking while looking over your shoulder. with your eyes fixed on what is behind you, you miss the obstacles ahead and stumble. you can neither change what has happened nor see the opportunities opening up before you. learning from the past can guide you; remaining trapped in it holds you back. you cannot change yesterday, but you can choose the step you take today."},{"title":"Newton’s second law","stem":"newtons-second-law","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#newtons-second-law","hay":"newton’s second law wisdom and life lessons a physics teacher walks into the teachers’ lounge after a lesson and complains to his colleagues: “these students just don’t get it! i explained newton’s second law three times. by the end, even i understood it—but they still didn’t!” teaching begins with understanding. before blaming students for not understanding, teachers should examine their own grasp of the subject and how they explain it."},{"title":"Learning from loss","stem":"learning-from-loss","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#learning-from-loss","hay":"learning from loss wisdom and life lessons do not see life only in terms of what you have lost. recognize your successes and the lessons your setbacks have taught you. even when things do not turn out as you hoped, the experience can help you find your next step. a setback does not mean all your efforts were wasted. if you learn from what happened, you carry that experience with you as you move forward."},{"title":"Changing fortunes","stem":"pawn-and-the-king","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#pawn-and-the-king","hay":"changing fortunes wisdom and life lessons never look down on anyone — one day, they may rise to power. never feel secure just because you hold power — one day, you may face defeat. never belittle someone because of their present position, or assume your own is unshakable. those who seem powerless today may rise tomorrow, while the mighty may face defeat."},{"title":"Draining the coffers","stem":"draining-the-coffers","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#draining-the-coffers","hay":"draining the coffers wisdom and life lessons one day, a king went out with his courtiers. as they passed through a village, they came upon an old man planting saplings in his field. the king called out: “why work so hard at your age, grandfather? you have been blessed with a long life, but you may not live to taste the fruit of these trees.” the old man replied: “we need not eat the fruit ourselves, my son. those before us planted, and we enjoyed the harvest. now let us plant so that those who come after us may enjoy it.” the king was pleased with this answer and ordered that the old man be given a purse of gold. pointing to the purse, the old man smiled. “you see, my son? our saplings have already borne fruit.” the king liked that answer too and ordered a second purse of gold to be brought. the quick-witted farmer continued: “other people’s trees bear fruit once a year. mine have borne fruit twice in a single day!” the king laughed and ordered a third purse of gold. this time, the vizier hurriedly intervened: “your majesty, we must leave! at this rate, the old man won’t just fill his field with trees—he’ll empty our coffers!” you may never taste the fruit of every tree you plant. but just as you have benefited from the efforts of those before you, leave something good for the generations to come."},{"title":"The qualities of water","stem":"qualities-of-water","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#qualities-of-water","hay":"the qualities of water wisdom and life lessons consider the qualities of water: when it reaches a tree’s roots, it gives life. it can put out burning wood. it washes away dirt. mixed with flour, it becomes dough—the beginning of bread. its droplets form a rainbow in sunlight. it brings life to the places through which it flows. left stagnant and unrefreshed, it can turn murky and foul. the human heart is like water: honest conversation, shared joy, and mutual care renew it. if it goes too long without warm human connections, it too can grow stagnant. genuine connection, sharing, and mutual care nourish the heart. solitude can bring peace, but we also need understanding and closeness."},{"title":"Hello, teacher! Do you remember me?","stem":"do-you-remember-me","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#do-you-remember-me","hay":"hello, teacher! do you remember me? wisdom and life lessons “hello, sir! do you remember me? i used to be one of your pupils.” “yes, i do. i taught you in primary school. how lovely to see you after all these years! what do you do now?” “i’m delighted to see you too. i became a teacher, just like you.” “that’s wonderful! what made you choose teaching?” “you did. you were my example. i wanted to help children grow and teach them to believe in themselves.” “that makes me very happy. but tell me, how did i become your example?” “one day, a boy in our class came to school with a new watch his parents had bought him. my family couldn’t afford one. i liked that watch so much that, during the long break, i took it from his pocket. “after the break, my classmate told you his watch was missing. you addressed the whole class: “‘whoever took it, please give it back.’ “no one said a word. so you closed the door and told us you would check all the boys’ pockets. i thought the most humiliating moment of my life had arrived. “you lined us up along the wall and asked us to cover our eyes with our hands. then you checked our pockets one by one. even after you found the watch in mine, you carried on checking everyone else’s. “finally, you returned the watch to its owner. but you never said who had taken it. you didn’t speak to me or my parents about it that day—or at any time during the rest of the school year. “you spared me the shame of being exposed in front of everyone. you didn’t let one thoughtless act define me as a thief. i understood how wrong i had been, but i didn’t feel worthless. “it was a lesson i never forgot. i realized that teachers do more than pass on knowledge. they help children recognize their mistakes, learn from them, and believe they can become better people. i wanted to be a teacher like you.” the teacher listened closely. noticing his surprise, the young man asked: “don’t you remember? you found the watch in my pocket.” “i remember what happened. but i didn’t know the watch was in your pocket.” “how could you not know? you found it yourself!” the teacher answered quietly: “because while i was checking your pockets, i had my eyes closed too.” true teaching does not mean overlooking a mistake. it means helping someone put it right without stripping them of their dignity. sometimes a single act of sensitivity towards a child can shape the course of a lifetime."},{"title":"The one left alive feels dead","stem":"living-with-loss","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#living-with-loss","hay":"the one left alive feels dead wisdom and life lessons when two people love each other wholeheartedly and one dies, the one who truly dies is the one who survives. when one of two people who deeply love each other dies, the one left behind lives with longing and feels dead while still alive. separation makes the depth of that love even more keenly felt."},{"title":"The most beautiful places in the world","stem":"place-in-loving-hearts","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#place-in-loving-hearts","hay":"the most beautiful places in the world wisdom and life lessons the most beautiful places in the world are the places you hold in the thoughts, prayers, and hearts of those you love. the most beautiful places are not always found on a map. being remembered with love, included in someone’s prayers, and cherished in someone’s heart brings a sense of belonging and peace."},{"title":"The road to the cotton field","stem":"beyond-the-cotton-field","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#beyond-the-cotton-field","hay":"the road to the cotton field wisdom and life lessons we could barely read or write when a new teacher came to our village. we did not yet know that he would become more than a teacher to us—someone who would support us like an older brother or a father. mr. aydin was saddened by the state of our school and the hardship we faced. when he learned that we were all expected to work in the cotton fields after leaving school, his eyes filled with tears. he pointed to a road leading out of the village. “where does that road go?” “to the cotton field,” we answered. then he pointed to another. “and that one?” we had no answer. we had never been beyond our village. as far as we knew, it was the whole world. that day, he also asked what we wanted to be when we grew up. again, we fell silent. we had never thought about it. mr. aydin soon taught us to read and write. as the days passed, we read more confidently and learned to put our thoughts on paper. one day, he brought a bundle of letters to class. he said they had been written by doctors, engineers, scientists, and people in other professions. “from now on, they will be your pen friends,” he told us. for years, we received letters from people we had never met. they wrote about their work, what gave life meaning, and the importance of knowledge and education. each letter opened a window onto a world we had never known. until then, we had never even dreamed of continuing our education or doing anything other than growing cotton. we believed there was only one road ahead of us: the road to the cotton field. through our pen friends, we began to dream. we started to understand where that other road out of the village might lead. with mr. aydin’s help, we finished school and continued our studies. years passed. some of us were preparing to become doctors, others engineers or teachers. a future we had once had no words for was beginning to take shape. then, one day, another letter arrived: “my dear children, “never stop dreaming. your dreams can give you direction; your effort and determination will bring you closer to them. “my heartfelt congratulations to you all. i have always believed you could succeed. “your pen friend,\nmr. aydin.” we were astonished. all those letters we had believed came from people in different professions had been written by mr. aydin himself. it was our own teacher who had shown us that other roads existed. a good teacher offers children more than knowledge: they reveal possibilities the children cannot yet see. discovering that there is more than one path gives a child the courage to choose a future."},{"title":"Simple habits for living well","stem":"living-well","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#living-well","hay":"simple habits for living well wisdom and life lessons eat in moderation, walk twice as much, laugh three times as often, and love without limits. a saying commonly described as a tibetan proverb. eat moderately, stay active, remember to laugh, and love wholeheartedly. sometimes it is these simple habits that make life richer."},{"title":"The majority is not always right","stem":"price-of-belonging","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#price-of-belonging","hay":"the majority is not always right wisdom and life lessons one day, a kindly old man receives a warning from above: “tomorrow, the water will taste different. everyone who drinks it will lose their mind.” he hurries home and fills every jar and vessel he can find with water. the next morning, the warning comes true: one by one, those who drink the water lose their minds. the old man drinks only from the supply he has stored. a week or two passes. tired of being alone, he leaves his house to seek company. but whenever he exchanges a few words with anyone, they laugh at him. “have you gone mad? you belong in an asylum!” he cannot make anyone understand. at last, unable to bear the pressure, he returns home and smashes the jars and vessels holding his water. then he goes to the fountain everyone else drinks from, drinks its water, and lies down to sleep. widespread agreement does not turn wrong into right. sometimes people give up their own judgment and convictions just to be accepted. the real test is to preserve independent thought under pressure, rather than simply become like everyone else."},{"title":"Three questions","stem":"three-questions","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#three-questions","hay":"three questions wisdom and life lessons one day, a king summoned his vizier. “i shall ask you three questions,” he said. “if you cannot answer them by tomorrow, you will be dismissed from court. first: what does allah bear patiently? second: what does allah keep hidden? third: what does allah change through his wisdom and power?” however hard the vizier thought, he could find no answers. he returned home that evening in distress. he had a clever, perceptive servant who noticed his troubled expression and asked what was wrong. the vizier told him what had happened. “i know the answers to all three questions,” the servant said. “i shall give you two now and the third tomorrow. allah bears patiently the complaints his servants utter in sorrow, and he keeps their faults hidden.” the following day, the vizier went before the king and gave these two answers. the king was pleased. “did you think of those yourself, or did someone teach you?” the vizier told the truth. “my clever servant taught me, your majesty.” the king had the servant brought to court and ordered the vizier: “take off your robe of office and give it to him.” the vizier removed his robe, and the servant put it on. the vizier, in turn, dressed in the servant’s old clothes. bewildered by what had happened, he asked: “what about the third answer? what does allah change through his wisdom and power?” the servant smiled. “have you still not understood? in an instant, he makes a vizier a servant and a servant a vizier!” no position is ours forever, and wisdom is not confined to those of high rank. never take your status for granted or look down on someone because of theirs."},{"title":"Three scenes","stem":"three-scenes","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#three-scenes","hay":"three scenes wisdom and life lessons a young man set out on a journey and encountered three strange scenes along the way. first, he saw people sowing wheat in a field. as soon as the grains touched the soil, they sprouted, grew, and formed ears of wheat. yet instead of harvesting the ripe crop, the farmers set the field on fire. a little farther on, he came across a man struggling to lift a large stone. no matter how hard he tried, he could not raise it. but when he placed a second stone on top, he lifted them both. after adding a third, he lifted the load even more easily. unable to make sense of what he had seen, the young man continued on his way. the third scene was stranger still. a man was riding a sheep, with several people clinging to his shoulders. the sheep was running swiftly. others ran behind them, but however hard they tried, they could not catch up. pondering these sights, the young man reached a city. at its entrance, he greeted a kindly old man, who stopped to speak with him. the traveler described the strange scenes and asked what they meant. the old man said: “the farmers represent people who do good and then diminish its value by boasting about it. with their own hands, they burn the harvest they have grown.” he continued: “the man lifting stones represents how people become accustomed to wrongdoing. the first time someone does wrong, their conscience weighs heavily on them. unless they feel remorse and change their ways, doing it again becomes easier. an unrepented sin can open the door to another.” “and the sheep and the people riding it?” the young man asked. “in that symbolic scene,” the old man replied, “the sheep carries people towards paradise. those riding it represent religious scholars, while those clinging to their shoulders represent believers from all walks of life. those running behind, unable to catch up, represent people who remain distant from faith.” do not overshadow your kindness with boasting or silence your conscience by growing used to wrongdoing. when seeking guidance, turn to people whose knowledge, integrity, and actions are in harmony."},{"title":"Three statues","stem":"three-statues","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#three-statues","hay":"three statues wisdom and life lessons the rulers of two neighboring countries were close friends. one day, one of them decided to send the other a birthday gift. he summoned his finest sculptor and asked him to make three gold statues, each a handspan tall and identical in appearance. yet there was to be a hidden difference known only to the ruler and the craftsman. when the statues were ready, they were sent to the neighboring ruler with a letter: “to celebrate your birthday, i present you with these three gold statues. at first glance, they look exactly alike. yet one is more valuable than the other two. when you discover which one it is, please let me know.” the recipient first had the statues weighed. all three weighed exactly the same. they were measured and examined from every angle, but no difference could be found. the ruler invited people knowledgeable about art to his palace. everyone studied the statues carefully. days passed, but the mystery remained unsolved. at last, he heard of someone who might be able to solve the riddle and had the man brought to court. after examining the statues, the man asked for a thin wire. he inserted it into the ear of the first statue. it emerged from the other ear. when he inserted it into the second statue’s ear, it came out through the mouth. but when he put the wire into the third statue’s ear, it stayed inside and did not emerge anywhere. the mystery was solved. the ruler sent his friend this reply: “these three statues represent three kinds of people. “with the first, whatever is heard goes in one ear and out the other. “the second repeats whatever is heard without thinking. “the third keeps what is heard in the heart, knowing when to remain silent and when to speak. “the third statue is the most valuable, for it represents someone who knows how to listen, honor another’s trust, and speak at the right moment. “thank you for this meaningful gift.” wisdom lies not only in listening, but in what we do with what we hear. protect what is entrusted to you, think before speaking, and choose the right words at the right time."},{"title":"Prosper or perish","stem":"stronger-together","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#stronger-together","hay":"prosper or perish wisdom and life lessons by sharing, we are fed; divided, we fade away. — attributed to yunus emre sharing brings abundance to our tables and strength to our unity. we grow weaker when we drift apart and stronger when we support one another."},{"title":"Searching","stem":"searching","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#searching","hay":"searching wisdom and life lessons looking for something where it cannot be found is not searching; it is deceiving yourself—like seeking loyalty in the disloyal, decency in the shameless, or compassion in someone without a conscience. see people as their actions reveal them to be, rather than as you wish they were. do not build your hopes on expectations alone; consider both their words and their deeds."},{"title":"Two bowls of water","stem":"two-bowls-of-water","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#two-bowls-of-water","hay":"two bowls of water wisdom and life lessons long ago, there lived a ruler renowned for his wealth. wherever he went, he took some of his treasure with him and delighted in displaying it. he had a wise teacher whom he trusted deeply. one day, he asked him: “you have seen much of the world. tell me, what do you think of my power and wealth?” instead of answering, the teacher asked a question: “imagine, your majesty, that you are in a vast, scorching desert, on the verge of dying from thirst. would you give half your wealth for a bowl of water that could save your life?” “of course i would!” “and if your journey continued, your water ran out, and you once again faced death from thirst? would you give the rest of your wealth for a second bowl?” the ruler thought for a moment. “to stay alive, i certainly would.” the teacher smiled gently. “then do not boast too much of your riches, your majesty. it seems there are circumstances in which your entire fortune is worth no more than two bowls of water.” sometimes a blessing we take for granted is worth more than everything we own. do not boast of your wealth; cherish the simple things that make life possible."},{"title":"Not my concern","stem":"not-my-concern","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#not-my-concern","hay":"not my concern wisdom and life lessons sultan suleiman the magnificent had made the ottoman empire one of the most powerful states of his time. yet one thought sometimes troubled him: “could the ottoman empire one day weaken and fall?” according to tradition, he decided to share his concern with yahya efendi, a respected scholar whose mother had also nursed suleiman as a baby. the sultan trusted his learning and spiritual insight, and wrote to him: “what causes a state to collapse? could the ottoman empire itself fall one day? i would like to hear your thoughts.” yahya efendi’s reply was very short: “what has it got to do with me, my sultan?” reading the letter at topkapı palace, suleiman was astonished. he could not believe that yahya efendi would dismiss such a serious question. “there must be a meaning in these words that i have missed,” he thought, and went to visit him at his sufi lodge in beşiktaş. “my brother, i wrote to you about an important matter. please give my question a serious answer,” he said, with a note of reproach. yahya efendi replied: “my sultan, how could i treat your question lightly? i considered it carefully and sent you my conclusion.” “but i understood nothing from your answer. you wrote, ‘what has it got to do with me?’ it sounded as though you were telling me to leave you out of it.” yahya efendi explained: “my sultan, when oppression spreads through a state, when injustice runs unchecked, and those who witness it turn away saying, ‘what has it got to do with me?’, that state’s foundations begin to shake. “when sheep are devoured not by wolves, but by the shepherds entrusted with their care; when those who know the truth remain silent and conceal it; when the cries of the poor, the needy and the defenseless rise to the heavens, yet only the stones seem to hear them—then the end draws near. “in such a land, the treasury is depleted and the people lose trust. when rulers no longer respect the people, and the people no longer believe in the state, order breaks down and the state collapses from within.” the sultan’s eyes filled with tears as he listened. he lowered his head in acknowledgment. then he departed, thanking allah that there was a scholar who would speak the truth to him without fear. injustice spreads not only through the power of those who commit it, but also through the indifference of those who say, “what has it got to do with me?” protecting a society begins with each of us recognizing our own responsibility."},{"title":"What does it mean to be too late?","stem":"before-it-is-too-late","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#before-it-is-too-late","hay":"what does it mean to be too late? wisdom and life lessons being late means not doing something when it needs to be done. in our relationships, however, delay can carry a much greater cost. there is a time to express our love, to welcome the love offered to us, to forgive those who have hurt us and to apologize to those we have hurt. when we keep putting these things off, the distance between us can grow, and the opportunity may pass. none of us is immune to making mistakes. we can all misjudge a situation, speak without thinking or hurt someone’s feelings. what matters is not delaying when it is time to admit a mistake, put it right and make amends. life is short. let us try to live so that, looking back, we say “i’m glad i did” more often than “if only i had.” making room for love, reconciliation and fairness today leaves less room for regret tomorrow. do not put off expressing love, apologizing or making amends. one sincere gesture today may spare you regret tomorrow."},{"title":"What matters in life?","stem":"what-matters-in-life","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#what-matters-in-life","hay":"what matters in life? wisdom and life lessons a teacher enters the classroom, greets the students and takes a glass jar from his bag. he places it on the desk and says: “we each have one life. how we spend our time determines what we make room for.” he fills the jar with golf balls and asks: “would you say the jar is full?” “yes!” the students reply together. the teacher pours some pebbles into the jar and gently shakes it. they settle into the spaces between the golf balls. “is it full now?” “yes!” the students answer. this time, he pours in some sand. it fills the remaining gaps between the pebbles. “and now?” once again, the students agree that the jar is full. the teacher explains: “this jar represents your life. the golf balls are what matters most: your family, your friends, the people you love and your health. “the pebbles are other important things, such as your work, your home and your possessions. “the sand is the small stuff: everyday tasks and less important concerns. “if i had filled the jar with sand first, there would have been no room for the balls or the pebbles. life works much the same way. if you spend all your time and energy on the small stuff, you will have no room left for what matters most. “first, decide what the ‘golf balls’ in your life are and make room for them. then fit in the rest.” when there is no time left for what matters most, sometimes it is your priorities that need another look. do not let everyday concerns push family, love and health into the background."},{"title":"Life’s lessons","stem":"lifes-lessons","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#lifes-lessons","hay":"life’s lessons wisdom and life lessons throughout life, we keep learning: from some people, patience, love, loyalty, faithfulness and decency; from others, how to recognize hypocrisy, deceit, malice and betrayal. the people we meet teach us different lessons: some show us who we want to be; others show us who we do not want to become. what matters is what we take from those lessons."},{"title":"Where does this road go?","stem":"where-does-this-road-go","category":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","href":"categories/hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri.html#where-does-this-road-go","hay":"where does this road go? wisdom and life lessons a young man walking along a road comes across an elderly man sitting by the roadside. “excuse me, where does this road go?” he asks. “nowhere, son.” “what do you mean? how can a road go nowhere?” “well, i’ve been here for fifty years, and that road hasn’t moved an inch. it’s the people who come and go.” a road will not take you anywhere by itself. you must choose where you want to go—and take the steps to get there."},{"title":"An organization without an action plan","stem":"without-a-plan","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#without-a-plan","hay":"an organization without an action plan work, livelihood, and wealth an organization without an action plan is like a ship with a known destination but no chart, no chosen route, and no assigned duties for its crew. it may reach port thanks to luck, a skillful captain, or favorable weather. but that would not be the result of systematic management. lasting success needs more than fortunate circumstances: it requires careful planning and consistent action. over time, an organization without a plan also begins to lose its members. people are sustained not only by ideas, but by the hope of seeing those ideas put into practice. when hope fades, people leave; when people leave, the organization gradually weakens and eventually ceases to function. a goal provides direction; a plan defines the path toward it. an organization may advance by chance without a plan, but lasting success requires deliberate and consistent action. bakhtiyar sirajov"},{"title":"At the fish shop","stem":"fish-shop","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#fish-shop","hay":"at the fish shop work, livelihood, and wealth on his way to the bus stop after work, a man paused outside a fish shop. remembering how much his wife and daughter enjoyed fish, he went inside. “a kilo, please… actually, make it two.” just then, an elderly woman in a worn coat approached him hesitantly. “could you buy me a little fish too, please?” without hesitation, he turned to the fishmonger. “make that four kilos. two for my family and two for her.” then he asked the woman: “do you have any bread at home?” she shook her head. he went to the shop opposite, bought four loaves, and gave her two. “is there anything else you need?” she took an empty bottle of eye drops from her pocket. “i last bought these two months ago. i can’t afford another bottle. my sight hasn’t been as good lately…” he took the bottle and went to the pharmacy. when he returned, the woman was sitting on a chair. the fishmonger had brought it out so she would not have to stand while she waited. the man smiled at the gesture. he handed her the medicine, then put the money he had left into her palm. she thanked him again and again, blessing him as she set off home. the man picked up his bag of fish and headed towards the bus stop. then he stopped short: he had forgotten to pay. he returned to the shop, embarrassed. “i’m sorry—i forgot to settle up. i’ve given away all the money i had with me. i’ll bring it later.” the fishmonger smiled. “no need, my friend. let me have a share in the kindness. the fish is on me.” the man thanked him. with nothing left for the bus fare, he started walking home. he called his daughter. “i’ve bought some fish. tell mom i’m on my way, but i’ll be a little late.” rain began to fall. as he walked, the day’s weariness seemed to lift. he felt grateful that he had been able to ease someone’s burden—and that another person had chosen to help too. the tears on his cheeks mingled with the rain. you do not have to be wealthy to show kindness. whatever help you can offer may lighten someone’s burden and inspire another person to give. sometimes compassion is what we pass from hand to hand."},{"title":"Baklava","stem":"baklava","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#baklava","hay":"baklava work, livelihood, and wealth when i was a child, my family had very little money. one day, my father brought home some baklava. that evening, we gathered for tea. there was a small piece for each of us. the next day, we were having a celebration at school. my mother packed a piece of bread, a few olives, and a slice of baklava in my bag. my classmates had brought all sorts of food. one boy looked at my little portion and laughed.\n“did you bring that baklava for the mice?” the whole class burst out laughing. i felt as though everyone was staring at my tiny slice. my face burned with shame. unable to hold back my tears, i ran home. as soon as i stepped through the door, i shouted at my mother:\n“why did you give me just one little piece? everyone else had so much food! you embarrassed me!” my mother looked at me and said softly:\n“that was my piece, sweetheart. i saved it for you.” i fell silent. so she hadn’t eaten her share the night before. she had put it aside so i would have something sweet for the school celebration. and i had taken out on her the hurt that others had caused me. i wanted to hug her and apologize. but i was too ashamed to lift my head or say a word. years have passed since that day. whenever i remember it, i hear my mother’s gentle voice again, and silently ask her forgiveness. i have never been able to eat baklava since. never mock someone for being poor. a small portion may hold a great deal of love. and do not take out the hurt others cause you on those who love you."},{"title":"The chief accountant and the chief executive","stem":"investing-in-people","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#investing-in-people","hay":"the chief accountant and the chief executive work, livelihood, and wealth the chief accountant asked the ceo: “what if we invest time and money in developing our employees, and then they leave?” the ceo replied with a question: “what if we don’t, and they stay?” investing in employees’ development is investing in the company’s future. saving on learning today may cost far more tomorrow."},{"title":"Do you have enough spending money?","stem":"more-than-money","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#more-than-money","hay":"do you have enough spending money? work, livelihood, and wealth cherish your parents while they are still here. ask whether they have enough money, but do not let your care end with that question. sometimes they may keep their needs to themselves because they do not want to burden you. help where you can, respect their wishes, and preserve their dignity. remember that their needs are not only financial: hearing your voice, talking with you, and sharing a meal can mean a great deal to them too. show your parents love while you have the chance—through both practical help and the time you spend together. sometimes the best thing to add to “do you need anything?” is “i’ve come to spend some time with you.”"},{"title":"Everyone has work to do","stem":"everyone-has-work-to-do","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#everyone-has-work-to-do","hay":"everyone has work to do work, livelihood, and wealth i have been fortunate to learn from wise teachers. one was my literature teacher, the esteemed baladadash. he taught us: if someone asks you for something or assigns you a task and you cannot do it, never say, even if it is true, “i had so much work that i had no time and i could not get it done.” those words make the other person think, “don’t i have things of my own to do? am i bothering you for nothing?” everyone has things to do. when someone asks for help, it is usually because they need you, not because they have nothing better to do. do not ignore the request or leave them waiting in silence. if you cannot do it, say so at once and do not leave them in uncertainty, because they are counting on you and trust you. coming back much later to say “i had no time” destroys that trust. it is painful to live knowing that others regard you as unreliable and untrustworthy. bakhtiyar sirajov"},{"title":"A former minister at a seminar","stem":"plastic-cup","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#plastic-cup","hay":"a former minister at a seminar work, livelihood, and wealth a former minister invited to speak at a seminar stepped up to the lectern holding a plastic cup of coffee. after a few sentences, he paused, looked at the cup, and smiled. “if you’ll allow me, i’d like to share something that’s been on my mind. i spoke here last year too. back then, i was a minister.” the room grew quiet. “they booked me a business-class flight. a car was waiting at the airport. a comfortable hotel room had been arranged. the next morning, i was driven here, welcomed, and shown to my seat. before my speech, someone brought me coffee in a beautiful porcelain cup.” he raised the cup in his hand. “this time, i bought my own ticket and took a taxi from the airport to the hotel. this morning, someone checked my name on the list and let me in. i found an empty seat. when i asked about coffee, they pointed me towards the machine in the corridor. so i went and filled this cup.” a ripple of laughter passed through the audience. “at first, it felt strange. then i realized: last year, the porcelain cup wasn’t for me. it was for my position.” he paused before continuing. “i’m not telling you this because i feel slighted. i’ve still been invited to speak, and you’re here listening. i can simply see more clearly now the difference between the attention given to me and the privileges attached to my office.” he set the cup on the lectern. “when you hold a senior position, it’s easy to mistake the open doors, the praise, and the special treatment for proof of your own importance. yet some of those things belong to the role. tomorrow, someone else will occupy it, and the cup will be theirs. what stays with us is the kind of person we are and how we have treated others.” do not confuse the privileges of office with your worth as a person. a porcelain cup does not make you greater, nor a plastic one make you less. lasting respect is earned through conduct, not titles."},{"title":"The garden and the gardener","stem":"garden-and-the-gardener","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#garden-and-the-gardener","hay":"the garden and the gardener work, livelihood, and wealth a teacher said to his students: “imagine a gardener tending a garden: loosening the soil, watering the trees, and pruning dead branches. the garden grows, blossoms, and bears fruit. who makes it grow?” “the gardener,” one student answered. “the trees grow by themselves,” said another. the teacher smiled. “a gardener cannot grow in a tree’s place. but that does not make the gardener’s work unnecessary. it creates the conditions for growth. the tree has life within it; the gardener helps that life flourish.” he paused, then added: “people grow in much the same way. we should recognize both their own efforts and the support they receive along the way.” growth owes something to our own efforts and something to the care we receive. value your hard work, and remember those who helped you flourish."},{"title":"Henry Ford’s choice","stem":"henry-fords-choice","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#henry-fords-choice","hay":"henry ford’s choice work, livelihood, and wealth a story told about henry ford describes how he once invited two young friends to dinner. both hoped to work for his company. they had graduated from the same university and were equally talented and well qualified. they talked about all sorts of things that evening. as they left the restaurant, ford offered one of them a job. he wished the other well but said he would not be hiring him. disappointed, the young man summoned the courage to ask: “mr. ford, we didn’t discuss cars or our engineering skills all evening. why did you choose my friend?” “there are two reasons,” ford replied. “during dinner, i watched you both. your friend tasted his food before adding salt. you added salt without tasting it. i want to work with people who understand what is in front of them before deciding to change it—people who observe rather than assume.” he paused, then continued: “the second reason is how you treated people. your friend was courteous to the waiters too. you gave me your full attention but barely acknowledged them. to me, respect is not something reserved for people in important positions. we should value people for who they are, not for what they can do for us.” character reveals itself in small actions. seek to understand before making a decision, and show respect regardless of someone’s position."},{"title":"Keeping busy","stem":"busy-but-unproductive","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#busy-but-unproductive","hay":"keeping busy work, livelihood, and wealth a man bought ten eggs for ten coins and sold the lot for nine. his friends were puzzled. “you lose money on every sale. what’s the point?” “never mind the loss!” he replied. “people see me buying and selling, so they think i’m a merchant. what matters is that everyone sees i’m busy!” looking busy is not the same as doing something useful. judge work by what it achieves, not by the impression it creates."},{"title":"Hot bread","stem":"hot-bread","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#hot-bread","hay":"hot bread work, livelihood, and wealth the smell of freshly baked bread filled the street. an elderly man stopped outside the bakery. his grandson had been asking for warm bread for days. he checked the coins in his pocket and joined the queue. he had just enough for one loaf. if he could have afforded it, he would have bought two. when his turn came, he held out his money. instead of taking it, the baker handed him a bag. “here you are, sir. enjoy.” there were three loaves inside. “but i only asked for one.” “take all three. you can share them at home.” the old man leaned closer and lowered his voice. “i only have enough money for one.” the baker smiled. “there’s nothing to pay. it’s friday—we give away fresh bread every friday. these three are for you.” “may allah bless you, my son.” “amen. enjoy your bread!” he had walked only a few steps when a young man called after him. “sir, just a moment!” the old man turned. “this is my mother’s zakat—her charitable offering. we’d be grateful if you would accept it.” he slipped an envelope into the old man’s pocket. “may allah accept her gift and bless her,” the old man said. as he passed the market, a woman stopped him. she was carrying two full bags of groceries. “we’re giving out food for ramadan. please take these home with you.” “may allah bless your kindness, my daughter.” by the time he reached home, he was breathless with the effort of carrying everything. his daughter opened the door and took the bags. “welcome home, dad.” “where’s your mother?” “in the kitchen, making eggs for iftar. you know how much orkhan loves them.” his grandson came running. “grandpa, did you get warm bread?” “yes, my boy. three loaves!” “hooray for grandpa!” his daughter looked inside the bags in astonishment. “dad, you hardly had any money this morning. how did you buy all this?” “allah provided, my daughter.” his wife came out of the kitchen, and he told them everything that had happened. when the call to prayer sounded, the family gathered to break their fast. there were eggs and warm bread, and the bags held butter, cheese, halva, and other groceries. it had been a long time since their table had been so full or their hearts so much at ease. after the meal, orkhan stood up. “i’m going to my room to pray.” his grandfather smiled. “you can pray before bed, my boy. it’s still early.” “no, grandpa. i prayed at this time yesterday. i asked allah for warm bread and cheese, and today they came. now i’m going to ask him to send money to have your teeth fixed. it’s so hard for you to chew.” a wistful smile crossed his mother’s face. “if only your father were alive to see you now…” “i see dad in my dreams, mom. in my dreams, he tells me to pray.” suddenly, the old man remembered the envelope. he went to his coat, hanging by the door, and took it out. inside was far more money than he had expected. his eyes filled with tears. “what is it?” his wife asked anxiously. he handed her the envelope. “look… there’s enough here for both of us to have our teeth fixed before eid.” then he looked at his grandson and whispered: “thank you, allah. so many kind hands brought joy into our home today.” a child’s prayer and the kindness of strangers met at this family’s table. when we notice someone’s need and offer help, we may help make their hopes come true. the bread we share can bring both nourishment and joy to a home."},{"title":"The credit ledger","stem":"credit-ledger","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#credit-ledger","hay":"the credit ledger work, livelihood, and wealth one day during ramadan, a stranger in plain clothes walked into a neighborhood shop and approached the shopkeeper. “brother, would you let me have bread, tea, and eggs on credit?” “of course,” the shopkeeper answered kindly. “there’s a woman who’s been taking bread on credit for three months. honestly, i don’t know whether she’ll ever be able to repay me. but she’s raising three children without their father. we can’t let them go hungry. allah will provide for us too. if you’re in need, take what you need. don’t worry about paying for the bread. you’re welcome to it.” the stranger smiled. “i think i’ve found the right place,” he thought. “how much do the debts in your ledger come to?” the shopkeeper looked surprised. “why do you ask?” “i’d like to pay them all.” the shopkeeper glanced from the stranger to the old ledger he had taken from beneath the counter. “all of them?” “yes, brother. please add them up.” seeing that the man was serious, the shopkeeper calculated the total. the stranger took money from his inside pocket, counted it, and laid it on the counter. “may allah bless you. you’ve stood by these people through difficult times. let this be my ramadan gift. tell them their outstanding debts have been paid. they don’t need to know my name.” then he pointed to the ledger. “let me take that too. there’s no need for their names to become a subject of gossip or for these debts to trouble them again. it is enough that allah knows our intentions.” he took the ledger and quietly left. even after putting the money in the till, the shopkeeper could hardly believe what had happened. he looked through the little window. the stranger was gone. it was almost time to break the fast, and everyone was hurrying home. just then, surayya’s seven-year-old daughter appeared in the doorway. her father had died, leaving her mother to raise the children alone. “uncle ahmed,” she said shyly, “mama asks for a loaf of bread and three eggs for iftar. we’ll pay you next month, when my uncle comes.” her words brought the shopkeeper out of his daze. out of habit, he reached beneath the counter for the ledger. then he paused and smiled. “come, my child. take the bread, the eggs, and some butter too. tell your mother that the uncle already came and left.” true generosity protects the dignity of the person receiving it. a burden quietly lifted from one person’s shoulders can inspire kindness in another."},{"title":"Mercedes","stem":"mercedes","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#mercedes","hay":"mercedes work, livelihood, and wealth “mercedes” was neither a company founder’s surname nor the name of an existing car manufacturer. it was the name of a girl whose father was passionate about cars. austrian businessman emil jellinek first used his daughter mercédès’s name as a pseudonym in motor racing. later, a new car he commissioned from daimler also came to bear her name. mercedes cars achieved remarkable success at nice race week in 1901. the name was registered as a trademark in 1902. when daimler and benz merged in 1926, the name “mercedes-benz” was born. a girl’s name had become part of motoring history. yet while her name became famous, mercédès jellinek’s own life was far from easy. her first marriage ended in divorce, and her second husband died. she died of bone cancer in 1929, aged thirty-nine, and was buried in vienna’s central cemetery. her life was brief, but her name lived on. behind a name known around the world were a father and his beloved daughter. behind a world-famous name, there may be a deeply personal story of love. remembering the people whose names became famous brands gives those names a human meaning."},{"title":"Pay rent for the water","stem":"pay-rent-for-the-water","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#pay-rent-for-the-water","hay":"pay rent for the water work, livelihood, and wealth a man buys a well from a farmer. but when he tries to draw water, the seller stops him. “i sold you the well, not the water in it!” an argument follows. unable to settle their dispute, they go before a judge. after hearing both sides, the judge turns to the seller. “if the water belongs to you, why are you keeping it in someone else’s well? either take your water away or pay the owner rent!” someone who tries to cheat another out of what is rightfully theirs may be caught out by their own words. fair-minded reasoning exposes the contradiction behind the trick."},{"title":"The scarf seller","stem":"scarf-seller","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#scarf-seller","hay":"the scarf seller work, livelihood, and wealth a bitter winter night had settled over the town. ice covered the ground, and it was difficult even to keep my footing. it felt as though my breath might freeze. taking careful steps, i finally reached the bus. “thank goodness i made it without falling,” i thought. just then, a boy carrying scarves approached me. i had no energy to listen to him. all i wanted was to get on the bus and warm up. “would you like one, miss?” he asked, holding out a scarf. “no, thank you,” i said, hoping to end the conversation. “go on, miss. it might come in handy.” “i haven’t got any money,” i said, hoping he would leave me alone. “that’s all right. take it. i don’t want any money. it’s a gift from me.” “no, i couldn’t. i already have a scarf.” with that, i hurried onto the bus. at that moment, i was thinking only of myself. i walked towards my seat without giving the boy another thought. before sitting down, i reached into my coat pocket for my phone. my hand touched a small packet. i took it out and stared: there was a scarf inside. the boy had slipped it into my pocket without my noticing. i quickly got off the bus. he was still standing in the same spot, offering scarves to the passengers. he could not have been more than twelve or thirteen. his coat was thin, his hands were bare, and his nose was red with cold. his dark eyes shone like stars in the night, and when he smiled, his teeth gleamed white. i went over, rested a hand on his shoulder, and held up the scarf. “did you put this in my pocket?” he smiled. “yes, miss. you might need it. it’s a present.” “but how will you earn anything if you give your scarves away? isn’t it hard standing out here in the cold?” “i’ll be all right,” he said, looking at me kindly. i held out some money. “no, miss. i don’t want it. i gave you that scarf.” had he asked, “but didn’t you say you had no money? where did this come from?” i would have wished the ground would swallow me. yet he did not question me. he had believed what i said, thought i needed help, and shared what he had. “please take it,” i said, firmly this time. seeing that i meant it, he accepted the money. then he reached into his pocket for change. “keep the change.” “no, miss. this is too much. i can’t.” he began hurriedly sorting through his coins. a few slipped from his fingers and fell to the ground. “it’s all right. this is my gift to you,” i said with a smile. a little shyly, he smiled back—a smile that seemed warm enough to melt the ice. “thank you, miss. allah bless you.” “thank you, too, my young friend,” i thought. “you have so little, yet such a generous heart, and you ask for nothing in return.” i got back on the bus and sat down. outside, the cold was as fierce as ever. the boy was still standing there—a child who had not lost his trust in people, whose smile could turn winter into spring, and who was trying to earn something to help his family. we pulled away. the town and the scarf seller disappeared into the darkness behind us. i thought of him, and of everyone who had nowhere to shelter on a night like this. a lump rose in my throat, and i struggled to hold back my tears. the bus traveled on, following the winding road between mountains blanketed in snow. generosity is measured not by how much we own, but by our willingness to share. sometimes the person we think needs our help is the one who reminds us what kindness means."},{"title":"The weight of the butter","stem":"weight-of-the-butter","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#weight-of-the-butter","hay":"the weight of the butter work, livelihood, and wealth a baker regularly bought butter from a farmer. after a while, he noticed that the portions were getting lighter. he accused the farmer of cheating him and took his complaint to the judge. the judge summoned the farmer and asked: “how do you weigh the butter you sell?” the farmer replied: “i have no weights for my scales. every day, i put the one-kilogram loaf i buy from this baker on one side and the butter i’m going to sell him on the other. i add butter until the scales balance.” the measure you use for others may one day be used for you. the bread you give determines the butter you receive."},{"title":"We are rich","stem":"we-are-rich","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#we-are-rich","hay":"we are rich work, livelihood, and wealth according to legend, emperor nintoku of japan once climbed a tall tower to look out over the surrounding land. when he saw no smoke rising from the houses, he understood that his people were living in poverty: they had nothing to cook. he immediately decreed that, for three years, the people would pay no taxes and owe no labor to the imperial court. he even postponed repairs to the palace so that they could devote their efforts to supporting themselves. three years passed. the people’s circumstances improved, and their homes were well provided for again. nintoku climbed the tower once more. seeing smoke rising from houses in every direction, he turned joyfully to his wife. “now we are rich!” the empress pointed to their neglected palace, with its leaking roof. “you call this wealth?” nintoku replied: “if our people are poor, so are we. if our people are rich, so are we.” true wealth is measured by the well-being of the people, not by the riches in the treasury. a leader’s greatness lies in what they do to improve people’s lives, not in the title they hold."},{"title":"Giving without money","stem":"giving-without-money","category":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","href":"categories/emek-ruzi-ve-servet.html#giving-without-money","hay":"giving without money work, livelihood, and wealth in a story told about the buddha, a poor man approaches him and asks: “why am i poor?” “perhaps you do not recognize the riches you already possess or share them with others.” “but i have nothing to give!” “you have five treasures you can share.” “what are they? please explain.” the buddha replies: “with your face, you can offer someone a smile. “with your eyes, you can look at them with compassion and love. “with your words, you can comfort and encourage them. “in your heart, you can wish them happiness. “with your hands, you can help them and do good.” you do not have to be wealthy to be generous. a smile, a kind word, a compassionate glance and a helping hand are valuable gifts too. there is always some kindness you can share."},{"title":"Belling the cat","stem":"belling-the-cat","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#belling-the-cat","hay":"belling the cat justice and society one day, the mice gathered to discuss how to protect themselves from the cat that never left them in peace. everyone had a suggestion, but none seemed suitable. at last, a young mouse spoke up.\n“let’s hang a bell around the cat’s neck! whenever it comes near, we’ll hear it and hide. it won’t be able to catch us by surprise anymore.” the mice looked at one another in delight. what a wonderful idea! why hadn’t they thought of it before? they all applauded the young mouse. when the excitement had died down a little, an old mouse who had been listening quietly in a corner spoke.\n“a fine idea. with a bell around its neck, the cat won’t be able to sneak up on us. but one question remains: who will put the bell on the cat?” everyone fell silent. not a single mouse answered. a good idea is only part of the task. before applauding a plan, consider who will carry it out—and how."},{"title":"Humanity","stem":"humanity-etibar-siracsoy","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#humanity-etibar-siracsoy","hay":"humanity justice and society how full the world is of wonders and delicate beauty... in every flower, in every whisper of the wind, in the color of the sky, there is a hidden wisdom. this beauty is like a silent trust placed in the human soul. sadly, however, people sometimes disrupt this harmony with their own hands, burning not only the land but also hopes, destinies, and memories. war damages not only the land but also the light within us. every life lost is, in truth, a part of the world falling silent. yet our purpose in life is not to destroy, but to protect, create, and sustain life. perhaps the greatest question is this: when will humanity find its strength in sustaining life rather than destroying it? when will the earth be filled with the sound of peace rather than silence? perhaps then the world will be not only beautiful, but also a place that is cherished. humanity is elevated by protecting life and serving peace and goodness. the more we devote our strength to sustaining life, the more beautiful the world becomes for us all. etibar siracsoy"},{"title":"The woman whose house was robbed","stem":"who-was-keeping-watch","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#who-was-keeping-watch","hay":"the woman whose house was robbed justice and society long ago, an elderly woman’s home was robbed. distraught, she went before the king to complain. after hearing her story, the king asked indignantly: “why were you sleeping so soundly that you didn’t even notice a thief entering your home?” the woman replied: “your majesty, we thought you were keeping watch. that is why we slept so peacefully at home.” people’s peace of mind rests on their safety, not on promises. a ruler’s duty is to fulfill their responsibilities, not to blame those who have suffered."},{"title":"Land and homeland","stem":"land-and-homeland","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#land-and-homeland","hay":"land and homeland justice and society mix soil with water, and it becomes mud. let blood be shed in its defense, and it becomes a homeland. a homeland is more than the place we live. it holds the sacrifices and memories of those who protected it and entrusted it to us. honoring that legacy means remembering them and building a peaceful future for generations to come."},{"title":"Sharing justice","stem":"sharing-justice","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#sharing-justice","hay":"sharing justice justice and society let no one shed a tear,\nor let those who always smile remember those who weep. let there be no such scourge as money,\nor let those with wealth learn how to share. let no one say “i love you,”\nunless their actions show what love truly means. let no festival come,\nunless there is meat for every table. justice means that everyone has a share in joy, sustenance, and love. true humanity is remembering another’s hardship even in our own happiness—and sharing what we have."},{"title":"The judge’s fair verdict","stem":"judges-answer","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#judges-answer","hay":"the judge’s fair verdict justice and society according to an old tale, a merchant lost a purse containing 800 guilders. a carpenter passing along the same road found it. an honest, devout man, he reasoned that anyone who had lost so much money would surely come looking for it. he kept the purse safe, intending to return it to its owner. one day, after a church service, the priest made an announcement: “a merchant visiting our city has lost 800 guilders. he promises a reward of 100 guilders to whoever returns the money.” as soon as the carpenter heard this, he went home, fetched the purse, and handed it to the priest. the merchant came to the church when he heard the news. he counted his money, but instead of the promised 100 guilders, he offered the carpenter just five. the carpenter asked him to honor his word. unwilling to pay the reward, the merchant protested: “there were 900 guilders in my purse, not 800. you’ve already taken your hundred!” the priest intervened. he had known the carpenter for years and vouched for his honesty. when the dispute grew heated, the matter was brought before the town court. the judge first addressed the merchant: “will you place your hand on the bible and swear that your purse contained 900 guilders?” the merchant took the oath without hesitation. then the judge turned to the carpenter: “and do you swear that the purse you found contained 800 guilders?” the carpenter also placed his hand on the bible and swore. as everyone waited for the ruling, the judge said calmly: “the matter is clear. the carpenter found 800 guilders, while the merchant has sworn that he lost 900. therefore, the purse that was found cannot belong to this merchant. it will remain with the carpenter until its rightful owner comes forward. the merchant may continue searching for his missing 900 guilders.” greed can cost us what we already have while we reach for more. those who lie to deny others their due may find that their own words expose them."},{"title":"The lambs’ skins","stem":"lambs-skins","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#lambs-skins","hay":"the lambs’ skins justice and society “better to shear the rams than skin the lambs.” this saying appears in a tale associated with asad pasha al-azm, an ottoman governor of damascus. according to the tale, one day asad pasha discovered that the treasury was empty. he summoned his advisers to find a solution. they proposed imposing an additional tax on the city’s weavers. “how much would that raise?” the pasha asked. “fifty or sixty purses of gold.” “but these people can barely make ends meet. how would they pay?” “they could sell whatever gold and jewelry they have at home, pasha.” asad pasha rejected the proposal. “i shall find the money another way.” the next day, he sent word to the mufti, inviting him to a private meeting that night. when the religious official arrived, the pasha said: “mufti, we have received information that you drink alcohol secretly in your home. that goes against both your office and the rules you uphold. i must report the matter to istanbul, but i wished to speak to you first.” alarmed, the mufti began to plead. he offered a thousand gold coins to keep the news from reaching istanbul. the pasha refused. the mufti doubled his offer, but the pasha refused again. eventually, they settled on six thousand gold coins. the following day, the pasha summoned the judge. “we have learned from reliable sources that you accept bribes and use your position for personal gain.” fearing for his office and reputation, the judge also began to plead. “please do not dismiss me, sir. i would be disgraced.” after much bargaining, he too agreed to pay six thousand gold coins. next, the pasha summoned the treasurer, the chief of public order, merchants, and other wealthy men, one by one. according to the tale, he collected two hundred purses of gold in this way. then he called his advisers together again. “have you heard of me imposing an additional tax on the people of damascus?” “no, pasha.” “and yet i have collected not fifty, but two hundred purses of gold.” “how did you manage that?” the pasha replied: “better to shear the rams than skin the lambs.” justice means sharing burdens according to people’s means, rather than taking the last morsel from those who have little. ill-gotten gains should be dealt with through laws that apply equally to everyone, not through secret bargains."},{"title":"The Bedouin whose camel was stolen","stem":"stolen-camel","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#stolen-camel","hay":"the bedouin whose camel was stolen justice and society a bedouin was riding his camel across the desert when he came upon a man who could barely stand. his lips were cracked with thirst. “water… please give me some water,” the stranger begged. the bedouin dismounted and handed him his waterskin. the man drank his fill. then, without warning, he shoved his rescuer aside, climbed onto the camel, and rode away. realizing he could not catch him, the bedouin called out:\n“take the camel! but i ask one thing: don’t tell anyone how you got it!” surprised, the thief stopped the camel and looked back.\n“why?” “if people hear that you repaid kindness with betrayal, they may be afraid to offer water to someone dying of thirst. i have lost my camel. i don’t want others to lose their hope of finding help.” when we betray someone who helps us, we hurt more than that person—we weaken other people’s faith in kindness. value the kindness you receive, and never let someone’s ingratitude make you indifferent to others."},{"title":"With such a people, the world could be conquered","stem":"market-of-good-neighbors","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#market-of-good-neighbors","hay":"with such a people, the world could be conquered justice and society one day, a king disguises himself and goes into the city to see how his people are faring. he walks through the market, asking about the quality of the goods, their prices and the shopkeepers’ livelihoods. he enters the first shop on the street and greets the owner. “i’d like half a batman (a traditional unit of weight) of butter, half a batman of honey, and some cheese.” the shopkeeper welcomes him warmly and weighs out the butter. unaware that his customer is the king, he says: “sir, i can certainly sell you the honey and cheese as well. but this butter is my first sale of the day, while my neighbor has sold nothing yet. i would be grateful if you bought the rest from the shop opposite.” pleased by these words, the king pays for the butter and crosses the street. there, he asks for honey and cheese. the elderly shopkeeper weighs the honey and hands it over. “thank allah, you have brought me my first sale of the day. may i ask a favor? please buy your cheese from the shop next door. its owner is still waiting for his first customer.” the king goes to the third shop and buys the cheese. he is deeply moved by the care these shopkeepers show for one another’s livelihoods. as he leaves the market, he says with satisfaction: “with such a people, the world could be conquered!” a community grows stronger when people care about their neighbors’ livelihoods as well as their own. true success lies not in leaving others behind, but in moving forward together."},{"title":"Why injustice when there is justice?","stem":"choose-justice","category":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","href":"categories/edalet-ve-cemiyyet.html#choose-justice","hay":"why injustice when there is justice? justice and society words attributed to hacı bektaş veli: why hatred when there is love? why war when there is peace? why strife when there is brotherhood? why enmity when there is friendship? why intolerance when there is tolerance? why captivity when there is freedom? why injustice when there is justice? love, peace and justice are more than wishes; they are values to uphold through our everyday choices. choosing them—and defending them through our actions—is our responsibility."},{"title":"Aging","stem":"aging","category":"Aging and time","slug":"yaslanma-ve-zaman","href":"categories/yaslanma-ve-zaman.html#aging","hay":"aging aging and time growing older is like climbing a mountain: the higher you go, the heavier your steps and the harder it is to catch your breath—but the wider the view becomes. our strength may diminish with the years, but experience teaches us to see further and understand life more deeply."},{"title":"Time — life flowing silently","stem":"life-flows-silently","category":"Aging and time","slug":"yaslanma-ve-zaman","href":"categories/yaslanma-ve-zaman.html#life-flows-silently","hay":"time — life flowing silently aging and time time is a strange wonder... we can neither see it, nor hold it, nor bring it back. it passes silently—in the movement of clock hands, in the changing days, in the gray appearing in our hair, in children growing up, in memories receding into the distance... often, we understand time only after we have lost it. a day that seemed ordinary yesterday becomes our most treasured memory years later. a conversation, a glance, a smile that we once considered insignificant, or a simple evening spent with someone we love, may later become one of the most beautiful moments of our lives. time stops for no one. it yields neither to wealth, nor to fame, nor to human desire. it is the greatest treasure: it passes in the same way for everyone, yet each of us values it differently. not everyone is given the same span of life. a person’s life is measured, in truth, not in years but in the meaningful moments they have lived. sometimes we think that life still lies ahead and that we have plenty of time. we will do it tomorrow, go next week, talk someday, meet later... but the time we call ‘later’ does not always come. time takes much from us: our youth, certain people, certain dreams, certain opportunities... yet in return it gives us memories, experience, and the chance to understand the true meaning of life. so let us not measure time simply by the years that pass. let us value our time not by how long we live, but by how we live. let us tell our loved ones today that we love them. let us not put off meeting our friends. let us look at beautiful landscapes, watch the sunrise, listen to the sea, and savor the moments that bring joy to our hearts. for time passes... let us not put off expressing our love or making time for our loved ones. a moment that has passed does not return; we must appreciate today. etibar siracsoy"},{"title":"To those who have reached sixty","stem":"life-after-sixty","category":"Aging and time","slug":"yaslanma-ve-zaman","href":"categories/yaslanma-ve-zaman.html#life-after-sixty","hay":"to those who have reached sixty aging and time turning sixty is not a signal to step aside. it may be a chance to make room for wishes you have postponed for years and to give yourself more care. let yourself enjoy some of what you have worked for. keep future needs in mind, but make room for comfort, pleasure, and things you have long wanted to do. you need not deny yourself everything simply to leave everything to others. keep loving your children and grandchildren and helping where you can. but you do not have to carry every worry for them. give your adult children room to make decisions, take responsibility, and find their own way. spending time and resources on yourself does not make you less caring. pay attention to your body. move in ways that suit your health and abilities, take walks, and rest. discuss your health needs and appropriate check-ups with your doctor. avoid pushing yourself unnecessarily, but do not let age alone persuade you to give up activities you enjoy. look after your appearance for your own sake. wear clean, comfortable clothes that feel like you. being older does not mean giving up your sense of style or the colors you love. keep your curiosity alive. read, listen to music, and learn something new. use technology, if you wish, to stay close to people and discover more of the world. you do not have to follow every new trend; choose what is useful or enjoyable to you. listen to younger people. their lives need not follow the pattern of yours. share your experience, but ask for their views before offering advice. they may have as much to teach you as you have to teach them. when you say, “in my day…,” remember: this is your day too. your memories may belong to the past, but your life is happening now. nurture relationships with people who respect you and let you be yourself. care about others’ troubles, while setting limits with people who repeatedly belittle or exhaust you. make time for what you enjoy. garden, cook, write, dance, or travel—whatever your circumstances and interests allow. even a day without grand plans can hold small pleasures. go out when you can. meet a friend, sit in a park, or visit an exhibition. if getting out is difficult, a phone call or a cup of tea with someone at home can also bring connection. stay involved in life in ways that work for you. try to ease the weight of old hurts. apologize when you have hurt someone. being ready to forgive may take time, so do not rush yourself. forgiveness does not have to mean forgetting what happened or returning to a hurtful relationship. talk when you are sad. ask for help when you need it. you do not owe anyone constant strength or a permanent smile. love, laugh, stay curious, and make plans. give yourself permission not only to rest, but also to begin again. live this part of your life according to your values and circumstances, rather than other people’s expectations. caring for yourself does not require loving others less. at every age, your time, comfort, and hopes matter. this is still your life."},{"title":"What changed after sixty","stem":"what-changed-after-sixty","category":"Aging and time","slug":"yaslanma-ve-zaman","href":"categories/yaslanma-ve-zaman.html#what-changed-after-sixty","hay":"what changed after sixty aging and time i asked a friend who was nearing seventy what changes he had noticed in himself as he grew older. this was his answer: i no longer tell older people, “you’ve already told me that story.” i let them tell it. sometimes sharing a memory allows them to relive a beautiful moment. i no longer try to correct every mistake. it is not my job to make everyone perfect. peace of mind is often worth more than having everything just right. i am generous with kind words. a sincere compliment lifts the other person’s spirits—and mine too. i no longer fret over a small crease or a spot on my clothes. being presentable matters, but there is more to a person’s worth than appearance. i have learned to step back from relationships that leave me emotionally drained. i do not have to be close to everyone. i treasure relationships built on mutual respect and understanding. i am no longer ashamed of my feelings. joy, tenderness and longing are all part of being human. i have learned to set needless pride aside for the sake of a relationship. being right every time does not make you happy. sometimes reaching out matters more. i make time for things that bring me joy. i have come to understand that my own happiness needs care too. before helping, i ask whether my help is needed and wanted. good intentions do not excuse me from respecting another person’s choices. i no longer complain endlessly about my health, my neighbors, my relatives or retirement. i do not ignore life’s difficulties, but i am also grateful to be alive and to have people i love beside me. i am slower to tell young people, “when i was your age…” as though my life should be their model. nor do i say, “i gave you the best years of my life,” to make someone feel indebted to me. every generation faces different circumstances. the present cannot be judged solely by the standards of the past. i give my wife more attention. i remember how young, strong and full of joy she once was. if the years have tired her and she needs more care now, i try to be there for her. i cherish the life we have shared. i have stopped blaming myself for everything. i have made mistakes, but i have also done my best for myself and my children. i learn from the past instead of using it to punish myself. growing older can teach us that peace matters more than perfection, and understanding more than always being right. life becomes richer when we learn to treat ourselves and others with compassion."},{"title":"A fly in a china shop","stem":"fly-in-a-china-shop","category":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","href":"categories/temsiller-ve-meseller.html#fly-in-a-china-shop","hay":"a fly in a china shop parables and fables a fly could never wreck a china shop on its own. but if it flew into the ear of a bull standing inside and sent it into a panic, the thrashing animal could smash everything. in this image, the fly represents lies, superstition, and prejudice. the bull represents someone who accepts what they hear without question and acts out of anger or fear. sometimes the destructive power of a lie comes from the strength of those who believe it and act without thinking. do not act on everything you hear. check the source, examine the claim, and then decide. do not let someone else’s lie cause harm through your actions."},{"title":"The elephant and the rope","stem":"elephant-and-the-rope","category":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","href":"categories/temsiller-ve-meseller.html#elephant-and-the-rope","hay":"the elephant and the rope parables and fables a parable tells of a traveler who came upon a curious sight: enormous elephants, each tied to a stake by a thin rope around one front leg. there were no chains and no cages. “animals this strong could easily break those ropes,” he thought. “why don’t they even try?” he asked a nearby trainer. “when they’re young, we tie them with ropes like these,” the trainer explained. “however hard they pull, they cannot break free. eventually, they stop trying. as they grow, their strength increases, but their belief that they cannot break the rope remains.” the traveler looked at the elephants again. it was not only the ropes that held them in place. it was their belief that what had been impossible in the past must still be impossible today. do not assume that what you could not do yesterday is beyond you today. your experience, strength, and circumstances can change. do not let a past failure set the limits of your future."},{"title":"The donkey’s law","stem":"donkeys-law","category":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","href":"categories/temsiller-ve-meseller.html#donkeys-law","hay":"the donkey’s law parables and fables one day, a donkey became king of the forest. a few days after taking the throne, he issued a decree: “everyone must go to sleep at six in the evening and wake at six in the morning. for those twelve hours, not a sound may be heard in the forest. anyone who breaks this law will be punished!” the animals were bewildered, but none objected. as evening fell, silence settled over the forest. then, one morning, a rooster crowed at five. uproar followed. the rooster was brought before a court and executed on the king’s orders. after that, no one dared break the law. every animal arranged its life around the hours the donkey had chosen. years passed. the donkey grew old and weak. near the end of his life, a lion came to visit. after asking how he was, the lion said: “one question has troubled us for years. what wisdom lay behind making everyone sleep from six in the evening until six in the morning? many times, we wanted to challenge the law. but we feared disturbing the peace. we thought you must know something we didn’t understand.” the donkey gave a bitter smile. “there was no wisdom in it. i made that law out of sheer foolishness. what astonished me was that you spent years looking for wisdom in my stupidity.” the lion fell silent. the donkey continued: “first, you feared me. then you grew accustomed to the rule. in the end, you forgot that any other way of living was possible.” not every command contains wisdom, and not every old rule has a sound reason behind it. a rule accepted out of fear can become a habit, and a habit can become an unquestioned truth. keep asking: “what purpose does this rule serve?”"},{"title":"In praise of the hunter","stem":"lions-untold-story","category":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","href":"categories/temsiller-ve-meseller.html#lions-untold-story","hay":"in praise of the hunter parables and fables until the lion learns to write, every story will praise the hunter. — commonly described as an african proverb those who tell the story often cast themselves as the hero. to understand the truth, listen to those whose voices have gone unheard as well. if you do not tell your own story, others will tell it from their perspective."},{"title":"The lion’s tracks","stem":"lions-tracks","category":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","href":"categories/temsiller-ve-meseller.html#lions-tracks","hay":"the lion’s tracks parables and fables a timid hunter who liked to boast went into a forest looking for a lion. he came across a woodcutter and asked: “have you seen a lion around here? or at least come across its tracks?” the woodcutter replied: “i’ve seen the lion itself. come along, if you like. i’ll take you to it.” the hunter turned pale. “no, no, thank you! i was only looking for its tracks, not the lion itself.” courage is revealed when it is tested, not when it is talked about. a reputation for bravery built on boasting can crumble at the first sign of difficulty."},{"title":"The tortoise’s miscalculation","stem":"tortoises-miscalculation","category":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","href":"categories/temsiller-ve-meseller.html#tortoises-miscalculation","hay":"the tortoise’s miscalculation parables and fables someone asked a tortoise: “how long will it take you to reach the nearby village?” “allowing for rain, mud, wind, and all the hills, it’s a three-day journey. but let’s say six days, just to be safe,” the tortoise replied. six days passed, but the tortoise had not arrived. they went looking and finally found it only halfway there. “what happened? you haven’t managed a three-day journey even in six days?” the tortoise answered miserably: “don’t ask, my friends. i allowed for the rain, the mud, the wind, and the hills. what i forgot was people with cruel intentions. whenever i picked up a little speed, someone would grab me and turn me onto my back.” difficult conditions are not the only things that can hold us back; other people’s malice can too. may allah bring you people who help you up when you fall, rather than those who knock you down."},{"title":"Two donkeys","stem":"two-donkeys","category":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","href":"categories/temsiller-ve-meseller.html#two-donkeys","hay":"two donkeys parables and fables two donkeys were walking down a road when they spotted a monkey sitting in a tree. one said to the other: “let’s have some fun at his expense. we’ll ask whether he has any friends. if he says yes, we’ll say, ‘what fool would be friends with you?’ if he says no, we’ll say, ‘who would be foolish enough to be your friend anyway?’” they walked up to the tree and called out: “monkey, do you have any friends?” the monkey looked down at them and replied: “everywhere i look, all i see are asses. who am i supposed to be friends with?” those who try to make others look foolish sometimes become the laughingstock themselves. putting others down does not make you any better."},{"title":"The ant carrying water","stem":"ant-carrying-water","category":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","href":"categories/temsiller-ve-meseller.html#ant-carrying-water","hay":"the ant carrying water parables and fables according to legend, the cruel ruler nimrod ordered a huge fire to be built in the town square, intending to throw the prophet abraham into the flames. the fire rose higher and higher, and every creature hurried away from the unbearable heat. only one little ant was heading towards the fire. it carried a drop of water in its mouth. another ant called out:\n“where are you going? can’t you see those flames?” “nimrod wants to burn the prophet abraham. i’m taking water to put out the fire.” “with one drop?” the other ant scoffed. “it’ll evaporate before it even reaches the embers! what difference could you possibly make?” the ant carrying the water paused.\n“perhaps this drop won’t put out the fire. but at least it will be clear whose side i’m on.” and with that, it went on its way. we may not always have the power to stop cruelty, but we can choose not to be indifferent. even the smallest act in defense of someone in danger matters."},{"title":"A remarkable lesson","stem":"remarkable-lesson","category":"History and notable figures","slug":"tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler","href":"categories/tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler.html#remarkable-lesson","hay":"a remarkable lesson history and notable figures a tale is told about the ottoman sultan murad ii and his son mehmed. as a child, the future mehmed the conqueror would fidget and misbehave during lessons. whenever his teacher, akshamsaddin, reprimanded him, he would reply: “you can’t tell me what to do. i’m the sultan’s son!” akshamsaddin was troubled by the prince’s behavior. at first, he hesitated to complain to the sultan, but eventually he gathered his courage, requested an audience, and explained the situation. murad thought for a moment, then whispered a plan into the teacher’s ear. akshamsaddin protested: “your majesty, i couldn’t possibly do that!” but the sultan stood firm. “we must do this so that my son learns.” the next day, the prince disrupted the lesson again. when his teacher warned him, he gave his usual reply: “i’m the sultan’s son!” at that moment, the door opened and murad ii entered the classroom. as they had agreed, akshamsaddin pretended to be angry. he sternly scolded the sultan for entering without permission and ordered him to leave and knock again. the sultan lowered his head, apologized, and stepped outside. mehmed was astonished. his father, before whom everyone bowed, had accepted the teacher’s reprimand and obeyed the classroom rules. a little later, there was a knock at the door. this time, murad asked permission to enter and apologized again. according to the tale, the prince’s behavior changed from that day on. without a lengthy lecture, his father had shown him that being the sultan’s son did not give him the right to treat others disrespectfully. in raising a child, example speaks louder than a lengthy lecture. a parent’s authority comes not from placing themselves above the rules, but from showing the respect and responsibility they expect of their child."},{"title":"Albrecht Dürer","stem":"albrecht-durer","category":"History and notable figures","slug":"tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler","href":"categories/tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler.html#albrecht-durer","hay":"albrecht dürer history and notable figures a legend about albrecht dürer the story goes that two brothers, albrecht and albert, grew up in a poor family with many children near nuremberg. both dreamed of becoming painters. their father worked from morning until night, but could not afford to pay for their studies. night after night, the brothers talked by candlelight, searching for a way to pursue their dreams. finally, they decided to draw lots. one would study painting; the other would work in a mine to pay for his brother’s education. after four years, they would exchange roles. albrecht won the draw. he left to study art, while albert began working in the mine. one learned to handle a brush and explored the world of color. the other descended underground each day, working hard so his brother could continue his studies. four years passed. albrecht’s talent attracted attention, and his work began to bring him recognition and an income. when he returned home, he embraced his brother and said joyfully:\n“now it’s your turn, albert. go and study. follow your dream. you supported me; now i’ll support you.” but albert slowly shook his head.\n“no, brother… it’s too late for me.” he held out his hands. his palms were calloused, his fingers bent from years of heavy labor.\n“these hands can no longer hold a brush the way they once could.” albrecht gently took his brother’s hands in his own. he wanted to speak, but no words came. his tears fell onto albert’s work-worn hands. only then did he fully understand what the fulfillment of his dream had cost his brother. the legend connects dürer’s famous drawing praying hands with this sacrifice. in the story, those hands symbolize brotherly love, gratitude, and the quiet labor behind another person’s success. our achievements also belong, in part, to those who have generously given us their time, strength, and support. true gratitude means recognizing their sacrifices, remembering them, and returning their care with care of our own."},{"title":"Mihrimah Sultan and the architect Sinan","stem":"mihrimah-and-sinan","category":"History and notable figures","slug":"tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler","href":"categories/tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler.html#mihrimah-and-sinan","hay":"mihrimah sultan and the architect sinan history and notable figures a beautiful legend links two mosques in istanbul: the story of mihrimah sultan and the architect sinan. mihrimah, the daughter of sultan suleiman the magnificent, bore a name of persian origin: mihr means “sun,” and mah means “moon.” according to the legend, when mihrimah was seventeen, two men wished to marry her: rüstem pasha and sinan. she married rüstem pasha in 1539. the tale says that sinan, already married and approaching fifty, kept his love to himself. unable to share his life with mihrimah, he gave his feelings expression through his art. sinan first built a mosque bearing her name in üsküdar, then another in edirnekapı. both buildings are indeed his work; the idea that they express a secret love belongs to the legend. those who tell the story see a woman in flowing skirts in the silhouette of the üsküdar mosque. in the slender minaret, luminous interior, and delicate ornamentation of the edirnekapı mosque, they look for echoes of mihrimah’s beauty. some even liken its decorative patterns to her long hair. the most memorable part of the story is the meeting of the sun and moon. it is said that from a certain spot in istanbul, the sun can be seen setting behind the edirnekapı mosque while the moon rises behind the mosque in üsküdar. the legend associates this scene with the spring equinox and presents it as sinan’s silent declaration of love. mihrimah—the sun and moon… did sinan deliberately plan this view, or did people later give his work this romantic meaning? we cannot present that interpretation as established history. yet the legend lives on. looking at the two mosques, people see more than stone, domes, and minarets. sometimes they also search for the trace of a feeling never put into words. not every love leads to a life together. when people channel their feelings into creativity, those feelings can live on in works that bring beauty to others."},{"title":"Sinan’s waterless house","stem":"sinans-waterless-house","category":"History and notable figures","slug":"tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler","href":"categories/tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler.html#sinans-waterless-house","hay":"sinan’s waterless house history and notable figures a legend about mimar sinan during the reign of suleiman the magnificent, istanbul was growing rapidly, and its water shortage was becoming increasingly severe. according to legend, the sultan summoned the architect sinan and asked:\n“people pay dearly for water and suffer great hardship. is there a way to free istanbul from this shortage?” sinan asked for time to survey the surrounding countryside. with his assistants, he examined springs, riverbeds, and valleys, searching for ways to bring water into the city. then he returned to the sultan.\n“it can be done,” he said. “but it will require a great deal of work and money.” “as long as it is possible,” the sultan replied, “the money will be provided.” sinan set to work. in time, water flowed through the channels and aqueducts he built to the city’s public fountains. water that had once been so difficult to obtain now supplied people’s everyday needs. the sultan ordered that the public water supply be protected and forbade private connections without permission. but he made an exception for sinan: in recognition of his service, he gave him verbal permission to bring water directly to his home. years passed. suleiman died, and fewer and fewer people remained who remembered his permission. one day, the elderly architect was summoned to the palace. a complaint had been brought before the imperial council: why did sinan have a private water connection when others were forbidden one? “sultan suleiman gave me permission,” sinan explained. “do you have a written decree?” “no,” he answered quietly. “i trusted the sultan’s word. i hesitated to ask him for written confirmation as well.” after much discussion, the council decided that sinan would not be punished for his past use of the water, but the supply to his home would be cut off. according to the legend, sinan fell gravely ill near the end of his life. he asked for water to moisten his dry lips, but nothing flowed from the tap in his home. then the architect who had brought water to an entire city said softly:\n“we did this work not for our own benefit, but to please allah. it is from him that we hope for our reward. though there is no water in our home, our work still serves the people.” people’s gratitude may fade, but that does not diminish the value of a good deed. serving others without seeking personal gain is a mark of character; remembering those who have served us is a responsibility we share."},{"title":"Three essentials for a city","stem":"three-essentials-for-a-city","category":"History and notable figures","slug":"tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler","href":"categories/tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler.html#three-essentials-for-a-city","hay":"three essentials for a city history and notable figures every city needs three things: a sewer system, a bathhouse, and a library. the sewer system cleanses the city, the bathhouse cleanses the body, and the library cleanses the soul. — attributed to sultan mehmed the conqueror our inner lives deserve as much care as the cleanliness of our cities and bodies. books bring clarity to our thinking and help us understand ourselves and the world more deeply."}];
